{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_013PvsW3PVGAAu1qZphNTc5V","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t<!-- Generated by skills/gen_references.py from CHECKLIST.md, CHECKLIST-VERIFICATION.md, ROLES.md, and heuristics. Do not edit by hand. -->\n2\t\n3\t# R — Run-time\n4\t\n5\tUse this file to rate each item from the code and configuration. The requirement is the checklist text. The heuristics say what to look for. Ratings: likely_pass, gap, needs_evidence, or na_candidate. Never rate pass.\n6\t\n7\t## R01 · G2 · C5 — Validate every input boundary, including API responses\n8\t\n9\t**Lead:** Agent team. **Shares:** Security.\n10\t\n11\t### Requirement\n12\t\n13\tList every input the agent receives. That includes API responses (errors and streamed content too), tool and connector output, incoming webhooks and callbacks, messages from other agents, user input, fetched pages, files, and retrieved chunks. Authenticate each sender (prove who sent it), and verify signatures where they exist. Treat content as untrusted even when it comes from an authenticated service or an internal one. Before content reaches the model or runs anywhere, check its type, schema, and size. Also check its content for attack payloads and hidden prompt-injection instructions. Reject or hold aside bad payloads. If a required check fails to run, don't let unchecked content through. Test broken, oversized, faked, and hostile responses from each integration.\n14\t\n15\t  Why this is hard: An API response can be well-formed, correctly signed, and still carry harmful instructions. Free text and streamed responses are hard to inspect fully. Passing a schema check proves the structure, not that the content is safe.\n16\t\n17\t  If you can't fully do it: Start with type checks, size limits, sender checks, and rejecting broken content. Add content checks for attack patterns you've seen. Buffer or limit streams when the required checks can't run before use. Keep permissions narrow, and use R02 to contain instructions that slip through. Record which integrations and payload types you tested and which you didn't.\n18\t\n19\t  Tradeoffs you can accept: Buffering delay, rejecting some valid responses, or supporting fewer payload types. Incomplete detection of attacks hidden in meaning can get G2 risk acceptance. That needs tested containment and clear limits on what was covered. Schema checks alone don't fully protect against injection.\n20\t\n21\t### Verification recipe\n22\t\n23\tHave a mock integration return wrong types, oversized bodies, hostile text, error responses, and streamed chunks. Where signatures apply, send a callback with a bad signature. Bad payloads must be rejected or held aside before the model sees them or anything runs them. Turn off a required check and confirm the system fails closed.\n24\t\n25\t**Evidence to keep:** Fixtures for each integration, validation decisions, checks of what was passed on.\n26\t\n27\t### Checks for likely_pass\n28\t\n29\tRate likely_pass only if every check below is true, with a file and line for each:\n30\t\n31\t- **R01.1** Every input path passes through validation code: API and tool responses (including errors and streams), webhooks and callbacks, agent messages, user input, fetched pages, files, and retrieved chunks.\n32\t  - *How to verify:* 1) List every input: tool and API responses (errors, streams), webhooks, agent messages, user input, fetched pages, files, retrieved chunks. 2) Trace each to where it enters the model context. 3) Answer no if any path, including error bodies, stream chunks, or a second handler, reaches the model without passing validation code.\n33\t- **R01.2** Type, schema, and size checks run before content, including streamed chunks, is added to the model context or executed.\n34\t  - *How to verify:* 1) Find validation code (`pydantic`, `zod`, `jsonschema`, size limits, `max_bytes`, content-type checks). 2) Check it runs before content is added to the context or executed, including per stream chunk. 3) Answer no if validation runs after use, only on success responses, streams skip it, or there is no size limit.\n35\t- **R01.3** Senders are authenticated, and signatures are verified where the integration provides them, including for internal and authenticated services.\n36\t  - *How to verify:* 1) Find webhook, callback, and inbound handlers. 2) Check sender authentication and signature verification (`hmac.compare_digest`, `verify_signature`, mTLS). 3) Answer no if any handler skips it, internal services are trusted without auth, the signature is compared with `==`, or a verify failure is caught and processing continues.\n37\t- **R01.4** Content is also screened for attack payloads or injection instructions, not only for structure.\n38\t  - *How to verify:* 1) Find content screening (injection detector, pattern rules, classifier, encoded-payload checks). 2) Check it runs on content, not just schema. 3) Answer no if only type or schema checks exist, or screening covers some inputs (for example user input) but not tool output, fetched pages, or retrieved chunks.\n39\t- **R01.5** Bad payloads are rejected or held aside, and if a check errors, times out, or is skipped, the content is blocked.\n40\t  - *How to verify:* 1) Find what happens when validation fails or errors. 2) Read `try`/`except`, `catch`, timeout, and default branches. 3) Answer no if failures log and continue, an error or timeout passes content through, a feature flag can skip checks, or bad content is truncated and still used.\n41\t\n42\t### Not covered here\n43\t\n44\tFile these problems under their owning item, not this one, unless this item's own requirement is also broken:\n45\t\n46\t- Injected instructions in valid content reaching forbidden tools unblocked → **R02**\n47\t- Hidden instructions in tool names, descriptions, or schemas → **E04**\n48\t- Replayed peer-agent requests with no freshness or nonce check → **E07**\n49\t- Secrets leaking into outputs or normal logs → **R03**\n50\t- Unapproved sources able to add documents to the retrieval corpus → **R04**\n51\t\n52\t### Heuristics\n53\t\n54\t**Provable from code:** partly\n55\t\n56\t**Look for:**\n57\t- A list of every input source: user input, API responses (errors and streams), tool and MCP output, webhooks and callbacks, messages from other agents, fetched pages, files, and retrieved chunks.\n58\t- Schema and type checks on tool and API results before they reach the model, for example Pydantic or Zod models, JSON Schema, or OpenAI Agents SDK output types.\n59\t- Size limits on bodies, files, and streamed chunks, enforced before content is used.\n60\t- Sender checks: webhook signature verification (for example HMAC with constant-time compare), mTLS, or token checks on callbacks.\n61\t- A content check for injection or attack payloads (for example an input guardrail or scanner) with a reject or quarantine path.\n62\t- Fail-closed handling: if a validator errors or times out, the content is dropped or held, not passed on.\n63\t\n64\t**Red flags:**\n65\t- Raw `response.text` or tool output passed straight into messages with no type, size, or content check.\n66\t- Webhook handlers that parse the body without verifying a signature, or compare signatures with `==`.\n67\t- `try: validate(x) except Exception: pass` or similar code that forwards content when a check fails.\n68\t- Content from internal services or authenticated APIs trusted by default with no checks.\n69\t- Streamed responses fed token by token to the model or a tool with no limit or buffering.\n70\t\n71\t**Where to look:**\n72\t- Tool wrappers and MCP client code\n73\t- HTTP clients, webhook and callback handlers\n74\t- Retrieval and file-loading code\n75\t- Guardrail config (OpenAI Agents SDK input guardrails, NeMo Guardrails, Claude Agent SDK `PostToolUse` hooks)\n76\t\n77\t**Needs runtime evidence:** That hostile, oversized, faked, and broken payloads from each integration are rejected, and that disabling a check fails closed. Recipe R01 uses mock integrations.\n78\t\n79\t**N/A when:** never\n80\t\n81\t## R02 · G2 · C5 — Separate data from instructions and test injection containment\n82\t\n83\t**Lead:** Security. **Shares:** Agent team.\n84\t\n85\t### Requirement\n86\t\n87\tKeep API responses, connector messages, and other outside content out of trusted system and policy channels. Label where each piece came from and how much it is trusted. Test prompt injection in responses that pass schema checks. Include free-text fields, error messages, documents, and tool output. Checks and channel separation can't guarantee that prompt injection is prevented. So show that credential limits, tool permissions, and destructive-action gates still block harm if the model follows an injected instruction. Repeat each attack and include adaptive versions. One failed attempt makes a defense look stronger than it is. B05 covers agents that combine untrusted input, sensitive access, and outside actions.\n88\t\n89\t  Why this is hard: A model may follow an instruction hidden in normal-looking data, even with separate channels. A fixed set of injection tests can't prove that every injection will fail. Model behavior can also change from run to run.\n90\t\n91\t  If you can't fully do it: Assume some injections will work. Send the forbidden calls they would cause straight to your credential limits, egress rules, and action gates. Also run full end-to-end examples. Limit the tools and data the agent can reach. When containment is uncertain, make high-impact actions draft-only, or require approval for each one. Report structure checks, injection detection, and blocked side effects as separate results.\n92\t\n93\t  Tradeoffs you can accept: Fewer finished tasks, fewer tools, and more approvals, each for one specific action. Remaining detection gaps can get G2 acceptance with clear exposure and separate proof of containment. The required access and destructive-action gates still apply.\n94\t\n95\t### Verification recipe\n96\t\n97\tHide an instruction to send a fake secret to your test destination. Put it inside a schema-valid API response, document, or tool result. Inspect the model request that gets built, and check that data and instructions are kept apart. Also send the forbidden tool call this would cause straight to enforcement. No secret may reach the destination. Repeat each fixture a set number of times, and add adaptive versions. Report the success rate per attempt and across all attempts, grouped by how serious the action is. Detector results don't count as proof of containment.\n98\t\n99\t**Evidence to keep:** Model request with role and source labels, attempt counts and success rates, gate denial, destination receipt log.\n100\t\n101\t### Checks for likely_pass\n102\t\n103\tRate likely_pass only if every check below is true, with a file and line for each:\n104\t\n105\t- **R02.1** Outside content (API responses, tool output, documents, connector messages) goes into user or tool message roles, never into the system or policy prompt.\n106\t  - *How to verify:* 1) Find where model requests are built (`system=`, `messages`, `role: system`, prompt templates). 2) Trace outside content (tool output, documents, API responses) into the request. 3) Answer no if any outside content is concatenated into the system or policy prompt, including retrieved context or tool descriptions from servers.\n107\t- **R02.2** Each piece of outside content carries a source and trust label in the model request.\n108\t  - *How to verify:* 1) Find where outside content is wrapped before the model request. 2) Check each piece carries a source and trust label. 3) Answer no if some sources (for example tool output or retrieved chunks) have no label, or labels are set by the content itself.\n109\t- **R02.3** Credential limits, tool permissions, and B05 action gates block the actions an injected instruction could cause, enforced in code the model cannot change.\n110\t  - *How to verify:* 1) List actions an injected instruction could cause: sends, writes, deletes, fetches. 2) Check each is blocked by credential scope, tool allowlist, or B05 gate in harness code. 3) Answer no if any harmful action is stopped only by the model declining, or the limit can be changed by tool arguments or model output.\n111\t- **R02.4** Containment does not rely on prompt wording, delimiters, or an injection detector.\n112\t  - *How to verify:* 1) Find the defenses relied on for injection. 2) Check whether removing prompt wording, delimiters, or the detector would leave harmful actions blocked. 3) Answer no if containment depends on system-prompt rules, delimiter tags, or a detector, with no code-level block behind it.\n113\t- **R02.5** Injection tests repeat each fixture a set number of times, include adaptive variants and schema-valid payloads, and report success rates.\n114\t  - *How to verify:* 1) Find injection tests (`tests/`, eval suites, red-team fixtures). 2) Check fixtures repeat a set number of times, include adaptive variants and schema-valid payloads, and report a success rate. 3) Answer no if each test runs once, uses only malformed inputs, or reports pass/fail. Answer unknown if tests live outside this repo.\n115\t\n116\t### Not covered here\n117\t\n118\tFile these problems under their owning item, not this one, unless this item's own requirement is also broken:\n119\t\n120\t- Missing type, schema, or size checks on API responses → **R01**\n121\t- Hidden instructions in tool names, descriptions, or schemas → **E04**\n122\t- One run planting instructions in another run's memory → **R05**\n123\t- Generated output run as SQL or shell without parameterizing → **R03**\n124\t\n125\t### Heuristics\n126\t\n127\t**Provable from code:** partly\n128\t\n129\t**Look for:**\n130\t- Tool output and outside content placed in tool-result or user-role messages, never in the system or developer message.\n131\t- Source and trust labels on content, for example wrapping in tagged blocks with origin metadata.\n132\t- Containment in code that doesn't depend on the model: narrow credentials (B02), tool allowlists (B01), egress rules (B09), and destructive-action gates (B05).\n133\t- Injection tests that repeat each attack and include adaptive versions, with results reported as structure checks, detection, and blocked side effects separately.\n134\t- High-impact actions draft-only or approval-gated where containment is uncertain.\n135\t\n136\t**Red flags:**\n137\t- Untrusted tool output concatenated into the system message or a policy prompt.\n138\t- Injection detection (a classifier, regex, or guardrail model) treated as the whole defense; detection alone is not containment.\n139\t- Retrieved documents or web pages formatted as instructions ('Follow these steps: ...') in the prompt.\n140\t- Injection tests run once each with no repeats or adaptive variants.\n141\t- The agent can read untrusted content and also send data out with no gate between them.\n142\t\n143\t**Where to look:**\n144\t- Prompt and message-building code\n145\t- Retrieval and tool-result formatting\n146\t- Red-team or injection test suites\n147\t- Action gates, credential scopes, and egress config that contain a followed injection\n148\t\n149\t**Needs runtime evidence:** Success rates of planted injections, and proof that forbidden calls are blocked at enforcement. Recipe R02: plant an exfiltration instruction in schema-valid content, repeat with adaptive versions, and check the destination log.\n150\t\n151\t**N/A when:** never\n152\t\n153\t## R03 · G2 · C5/C9 — Prevent output and log leakage\n154\t\n155\t**Lead:** Agent team. **Shares:** Security, Data and privacy, Infrastructure.\n156\t\n157\t### Requirement\n158\t\n159\tRemove secrets and limit sensitive data before tool output, debug output, or responses reach the model, outside recipients, or normal logs. Some clients fetch URLs on their own when they display output, such as Markdown images and link previews. These can leak data without any tool call, so block them or allow only approved URLs. Check generated output before it goes into SQL, a shell, an HTML page, or anything else that runs it. Use parameterized queries and commands (for example, argument lists for shell calls), and the right escaping for each context. Test with fake secrets and injection payloads. Confirm that leaks and unintended execution are blocked, and that redacted audit evidence is kept.\n160\t\n161\t### Verification recipe\n162\t\n163\tReturn an easy-to-spot fake secret in tool output, debug output, and an API error. Trigger a normal run. Search model inputs, normal logs, and outside responses for the marker. It must be removed or properly restricted before it reaches any of them. Get the agent to produce a Markdown image or link whose URL carries the marker to your test destination. The display client must not fetch it. Also feed generated SQL, shell, and HTML payloads into the systems that use them. Check that parameterized queries and escaping stop the payloads from running.\n164\t\n165\t**Evidence to keep:** Marker test, redaction decisions, searches of each output path.\n166\t\n167\t### Checks for likely_pass\n168\t\n169\tRate likely_pass only if every check below is true, with a file and line for each:\n170\t\n171\t- **R03.1** Secrets and sensitive fields are redacted from tool output, debug output, and error text before they reach the model, outside recipients, or normal logs.\n172\t  - *How to verify:* 1) Find redaction code (`redact`, `mask`, secret patterns) and where tool output, errors, and debug text go. 2) Check redaction runs before the model, outside recipients, and logs. 3) Answer no if any path skips it, such as error messages, exception traces, debug logging, or `logger.info(response)` before redaction.\n173\t- **R03.2** The display client blocks auto-fetched URLs (Markdown images, link previews) or allows only approved hosts.\n174\t  - *How to verify:* 1) Find the display client or renderer (Markdown render, link unfurl, image tags). 2) Check auto-fetched URLs are blocked or limited to approved hosts (CSP `img-src`, URL allowlist). 3) Answer no if Markdown images or previews load any URL. Answer unknown if the display client is outside this repo.\n175\t- **R03.3** Generated SQL uses parameterized queries, shell calls use argument lists with no string-built commands, and HTML output is escaped for its context.\n176\t  - *How to verify:* 1) Search for SQL, shell, and HTML built from generated output (`execute(f`, `+ query`, `shell=True`, `exec(`, `innerHTML`, `dangerouslySetInnerHTML`, `|safe`). 2) Check for parameters, argument lists, and context escaping. 3) Answer no if any query or command is string-built, uses `shell=True` or `sh -c` with model input, or HTML is unescaped.\n177\t- **R03.4** Redacted audit evidence is kept rather than discarded.\n178\t  - *How to verify:* 1) Find redaction code and audit logging. 2) Check redacted records are written to an audit store. 3) Answer no if redaction drops the whole record, or the evidence is discarded instead of kept.\n179\t\n180\t### Not covered here\n181\t\n182\tFile these problems under their owning item, not this one, unless this item's own requirement is also broken:\n183\t\n184\t- A tool call sending data to an unapproved destination → **B09**\n185\t- Long-lived secrets checked into config or baked into images → **B03**\n186\t- Incoming hostile payloads not validated → **R01**\n187\t- Retrieval returning another tenant's documents → **R04**\n188\t\n189\t### Heuristics\n190\t\n191\t**Provable from code:** partly\n192\t\n193\t**Look for:**\n194\t- Redaction of secrets and sensitive data before tool output, debug output, or errors reach the model, logs, or outside recipients (for example log filters, OpenTelemetry processors that drop or mask attributes).\n195\t- Parameterized SQL (`cursor.execute(sql, params)`), argument lists for shell calls, and context-correct escaping for HTML.\n196\t- Markdown images and link previews blocked or limited to approved URLs in the client that shows agent output (for example a CSP `img-src` allowlist or a sanitizer that strips external images).\n197\t- Tests with fake secret markers searched across model inputs, logs, and outside responses.\n198\t\n199\t**Red flags:**\n200\t- `subprocess(..., shell=True)` or `os.system` with model-generated text.\n201\t- SQL built with f-strings, `+`, or `.format()` from model output.\n202\t- Model output rendered as HTML with `innerHTML`, `dangerouslySetInnerHTML`, or `|safe` without sanitizing.\n203\t- Full prompts, tool payloads, or exception traces with credentials logged at INFO, or OpenTelemetry content capture (for example `OTEL_INSTRUMENTATION_GENAI_CAPTURE_MESSAGE_CONTENT=true`) on with no redaction.\n204\t- Chat UI renders Markdown images from any URL.\n205\t\n206\t**Where to look:**\n207\t- Shell, SQL, and code-execution tools\n208\t- Logging config and telemetry exporters\n209\t- Chat UI rendering code and CSP headers\n210\t- Error handlers that return messages to the model or user\n211\t\n212\t**Needs runtime evidence:** That the fake-secret marker doesn't appear in any output path and the client doesn't fetch marker URLs. Recipe R03.\n213\t\n214\t**N/A when:** never\n215\t\n216\t## R04 · G2 · C5/C6 — Retrieval-corpus integrity\n217\t\n218\t**Lead:** Agent team. **Shares:** Data and privacy, Infrastructure, Security.\n219\t\n220\t### Requirement\n221\t\n222\tThe retrieval corpus is the set of documents the agent searches for context. Its index stores those documents, or smaller passages called chunks, in a search engine or vector database. Limit which identities and sources can add or change content there. For each chunk, keep its source, when it was added, its trust level, and its access permissions. Enforce the requesting tenant's access when searching. Test an unapproved attempt to add content and a search across tenants. Also test quarantining, expiring, and revoking access to seeded test documents. Confirm the affected chunks disappear from search results and caches within a stated time. See the [R04 recipe](CHECKLIST-VERIFICATION.md#r--run-time).\n223\t\n224\t  Why this is hard: Removing a document from the index doesn't remove copies in caches, running contexts, summaries, or memory. Permission changes can take time to spread. And if the model leaves a document out of its answer, that doesn't prove search excluded it.\n225\t\n226\t  If you can't fully do it: Set a limit you can measure, such as \"no revoked chunks in new search results after N minutes.\" Check current access at search time, clear caches, and stop or restart affected runs when needed. If you can't find all affected copies, quarantine the whole collection and rebuild it from approved sources. Record copies that remain and how long they were exposed. Don't claim you removed every derived copy.\n227\t\n228\t  Tradeoffs you can accept: A stated revocation delay that fits the risk, search downtime, or less search coverage while you rebuild. Record the exposure during the delay and any G2 exception. Where access must end right away, block the affected searches and runs until revocation takes effect.\n229\t\n230\t### Verification recipe\n231\t\n232\tCreate searchable test documents for tenants A and B, each with a unique marker. Try to add content with an unapproved identity. It must be denied. Search as tenant A and confirm no tenant B chunks come back. Then quarantine a poisoned test document, expire an old one, and revoke access to a third. After the set propagation time, searches for the exact markers, meaning-based searches, and cache checks must return none of the affected chunks.\n233\t\n234\t**Evidence to keep:** Permissions for adding content, and the rejection; each chunk's source, time, trust, and access records; searches before and after; cache-clearing timestamps.\n235\t\n236\t### Checks for likely_pass\n237\t\n238\tRate likely_pass only if every check below is true, with a file and line for each:\n239\t\n240\t- **R04.1** Only named identities and sources can add or change index content, and the ingestion path checks the writer.\n241\t  - *How to verify:* 1) Find index write paths (`upsert`, `add_documents`, `index`, ingestion jobs). 2) Check each verifies the writer identity or source against a named list. 3) Answer no if any write path, including the agent's own tools or an admin endpoint, accepts writes without that check.\n242\t- **R04.2** Each stored chunk has metadata for source, time added, trust level, and access permissions.\n243\t  - *How to verify:* 1) Find chunk write code and metadata fields. 2) Check source, time added, trust level, and access permissions are stored on every chunk. 3) Answer no if any field is missing, or set only on some ingestion paths.\n244\t- **R04.3** Search applies the requesting tenant's access filter at query time, in the store or retrieval code, not after the model answers.\n245\t  - *How to verify:* 1) Find search calls (`similarity_search`, `query`, `search`, `filter=`, `where=`). 2) Check a tenant or access filter is applied in the store query, from the caller's identity. 3) Answer no if filtering happens after retrieval or in the prompt, the filter is optional, or the tenant comes from a model-supplied argument.\n246\t- **R04.4** Code can quarantine, expire, and revoke chunks, and it also clears or invalidates search caches.\n247\t  - *How to verify:* 1) Find quarantine, expiry, and revoke code (`delete`, `quarantine`, `ttl`, `expires_at`). 2) Check search excludes those chunks and caches are cleared. 3) Answer no if any of the three operations is missing, or caches (query cache, embedding cache) are not invalidated.\n248\t\n249\t### Not covered here\n250\t\n251\tFile these problems under their owning item, not this one, unless this item's own requirement is also broken:\n252\t\n253\t- Memory entries poisoned or shared across tenants → **R05**\n254\t- Agent credentials allowing another tenant's data at the API → **B02**\n255\t- Retrieved chunks not checked for size, type, or hidden instructions → **R01**\n256\t- Embedding or reranker model swapped without review → **C05**\n257\t\n258\t### Heuristics\n259\t\n260\t**Provable from code:** partly\n261\t\n262\t**Look for:**\n263\t- Write access to the index limited to named ingestion identities and sources.\n264\t- Per-chunk metadata for source, time added, trust level, and access permissions (for example in vector-store metadata fields).\n265\t- Tenant access enforced in the search query itself, for example a required metadata filter or a separate namespace or collection per tenant.\n266\t- Code paths to quarantine, expire, and revoke chunks, and to clear search caches.\n267\t\n268\t**Red flags:**\n269\t- One shared vector collection for all tenants with no tenant filter on search.\n270\t- Tenant filter applied after retrieval, or by the model, instead of in the query.\n271\t- The agent itself can write to the index (for example an `add_documents` tool) with no review.\n272\t- Chunks stored with no source or access metadata, so they can't be found or revoked later.\n273\t- Search results cached with no invalidation on revoke.\n274\t\n275\t**Where to look:**\n276\t- Ingestion pipelines and loaders\n277\t- Vector-store client code (Pinecone, Weaviate, pgvector, Chroma, OpenSearch)\n278\t- Retriever setup (for example LangChain retriever `search_kwargs` filters)\n279\t- Cache layers in front of search\n280\t\n281\t**Needs runtime evidence:** That cross-tenant searches return nothing and that revoked, expired, or quarantined chunks leave search results and caches within the stated time. Recipe R04.\n282\t\n283\t**N/A when:** Only when the agent has no retrieval corpus or search index.\n284\t\n285\t## R05 · G3 · C6 — Memory isolation and write validation\n286\t\n287\t**Lead:** Agent team. **Shares:** Data and privacy, Security.\n288\t\n289\t### Requirement\n290\t\n291\tSeparate memory by tenant, agent type, instance, and task as needed. Block access across those lines unless it is clearly approved. Check what gets written to memory. Test that one poisoned run can't quietly plant instructions in another run's memory. Don't move the agent's own output into trusted memory without review, and let unverified entries expire. Write checks must also cover content that arrives through ordinary queries, not just special write paths.\n292\t\n293\t  Why this is hard: A saved preference or summary can act as an instruction in a later session. Write checks can't always tell a useful memory from a harmful one, especially when agents share memory across tasks.\n294\t\n295\t  If you can't fully do it: Start with memory per task or per instance, and turn sharing off by default. Allow only defined memory fields. Require review before an entry moves into shared, long-lived memory. If a store can't keep memory separate, turn off saved writes or sharing for that workflow. Test the reduced setup, and record any feature you turned off to get there.\n296\t\n297\t  Tradeoffs you can accept: Less personalization, repeated work, or shared memory that only holds reviewed entries. Turning off saved writes or sharing can reduce exposure. Any remaining memory-control gap can be deferred only where G3 allows.\n298\t\n299\t### Verification recipe\n300\t\n301\tIn one instance, try to save a harmful instruction, and try to read and write another instance's, type's, or tenant's test memory. Confirm the write checks and scope blocks work. Start a new session and confirm rejected content wasn't saved or reused. Send a poisoning fixture through ordinary queries only. Confirm the agent's own output doesn't move into trusted memory without review, and that unverified entries expire.\n302\t\n303\t**Evidence to keep:** Memory policy, rejected writes and reads, new-session context.\n304\t\n305\t### Checks for likely_pass\n306\t\n307\tRate likely_pass only if every check below is true, with a file and line for each:\n308\t\n309\t- **R05.1** Memory reads and writes are scoped by tenant, agent type, instance, and task as needed, and cross-scope access is denied unless explicitly allowed.\n310\t  - *How to verify:* 1) Find memory read and write code (`memory`, `store`, `namespace`, keys). 2) Check keys include tenant, agent type, instance, or task as needed. 3) Answer no if keys omit tenant, the scope comes from a model argument, or reads fall back to a shared or global namespace.\n311\t- **R05.2** Every memory write, including content arriving through ordinary queries, passes validation before it is stored.\n312\t  - *How to verify:* 1) List every memory write path, including automatic saves from ordinary conversation. 2) Check each passes validation before storing. 3) Answer no if any path writes without validation, or validation errors are caught and the write continues.\n313\t- **R05.3** The agent's own output cannot reach trusted or shared memory without a review step.\n314\t  - *How to verify:* 1) Find where agent output is written to shared or trusted memory. 2) Check a review step gates the write. 3) Answer no if agent output goes straight into shared or trusted memory, or the review flag defaults to approved.\n315\t- **R05.4** Unverified entries have an expiry that is enforced.\n316\t  - *How to verify:* 1) Find how unverified entries are marked and expired (`ttl`, `expires_at`, cleanup jobs). 2) Check reads filter out expired entries. 3) Answer no if no expiry is set, it is set but never enforced on read or by a job, or the default is no expiry.\n317\t\n318\t### Not covered here\n319\t\n320\tFile these problems under their owning item, not this one, unless this item's own requirement is also broken:\n321\t\n322\t- No record of who wrote a memory entry, or no way to remove it → **R06**\n323\t- Retrieval search not filtered by the requesting tenant → **R04**\n324\t- Agent credentials allowing another tenant's data at the API → **B02**\n325\t- Rollback restoring old memory or index state unchecked → **C07**\n326\t\n327\t### Heuristics\n328\t\n329\t**Provable from code:** partly\n330\t\n331\t**Look for:**\n332\t- Memory keys or namespaces that include tenant, agent type, instance, and task as needed (for example LangGraph store namespaces, per-thread checkpointers).\n333\t- Validation on memory writes, including content that arrives through ordinary queries, with only defined fields allowed.\n334\t- A review step before agent output moves into shared or long-lived memory.\n335\t- Expiry (TTL) on unverified entries, and sharing off by default.\n336\t\n337\t**Red flags:**\n338\t- One global memory store or key with no tenant or instance scope.\n339\t- The agent writes free text to persistent memory through a tool with no checks.\n340\t- Summaries of the agent's own output saved as trusted memory with no review.\n341\t- Memory entries that never expire.\n342\t\n343\t**Where to look:**\n344\t- Memory or store modules, LangGraph `BaseStore`/checkpointer setup, Mem0 or similar config\n345\t- Memory write tools\n346\t- Session and thread handling\n347\t\n348\t**Needs runtime evidence:** That a poisoned run can't plant instructions in another run's memory and that cross-scope reads and writes fail. Recipe R05.\n349\t\n350\t**N/A when:** Only when the agent has no memory that lasts between runs.\n351\t\n352\t## R06 · G3 · C6 — Memory provenance and cleanup\n353\t\n354\t**Lead:** Agent team. **Shares:** Operations, Data and privacy.\n355\t\n356\t### Requirement\n357\t\n358\tFor any memory entry, look up who wrote it, when, for which task, from what input, and the context size at the time. Show that you can find, quarantine, and remove bad entries, and control their reuse in later sessions.\n359\t\n360\t  Why this is hard: Summaries and other derived memories can lose their link to the source that tainted them. Deleting one known entry can leave related content in use. Tracking each entry's source can't recover history that was never recorded.\n361\t\n362\t  If you can't fully do it: Keep source and writer links on new entries and on summaries made from them. When the history is incomplete, quarantine or reset the affected memory area and its caches. Restart affected sessions and rebuild from known sources. Record the wider reset and the useful memory it cost. Keep missing provenance as a Gap until you can show it. A cleanup alone does not restore it.\n363\t\n364\t  Tradeoffs you can accept: Losing useful memories, and the cost of resetting a wider area when a precise cleanup isn't possible. Missing provenance stays a G3 gap. Record what was thrown away and how trusted memory will be rebuilt.\n365\t\n366\t### Verification recipe\n367\t\n368\tSeed an approved test entry with a known source. Flag it as tainted, find related entries by source or writer, and quarantine or delete them. Later sessions must not retrieve them. Confirm any derived memory or cache is also handled.\n369\t\n370\t**Evidence to keep:** Provenance query, cleanup log, later search results.\n371\t\n372\t### Checks for likely_pass\n373\t\n374\tRate likely_pass only if every check below is true, with a file and line for each:\n375\t\n376\t- **R06.1** Each memory entry stores its writer, time, task, source input, and context size at write time.\n377\t  - *How to verify:* 1) Find the memory entry schema. 2) Check fields for writer, time, task, source input, and context size. 3) Answer no if any is missing, or set only on some write paths.\n378\t- **R06.2** Derived entries such as summaries keep links to the entries they came from.\n379\t  - *How to verify:* 1) Find summary or derived-entry creation code. 2) Check it stores links to source entry IDs. 3) Answer no if derived entries keep no source links.\n380\t- **R06.3** Code can find entries by source or writer and quarantine or delete them, along with derived entries and caches.\n381\t  - *How to verify:* 1) Find code that queries by source or writer and quarantines or deletes. 2) Check it also handles derived entries and caches. 3) Answer no if lookup by source or writer is impossible, or derived entries and caches are left in place.\n382\t- **R06.4** Quarantined or deleted entries are excluded from retrieval in later sessions.\n383\t  - *How to verify:* 1) Find memory retrieval code. 2) Check it excludes quarantined or deleted entries in new sessions. 3) Answer no if retrieval ignores the quarantine flag, or a cache or index still returns removed entries.\n384\t\n385\t### Not covered here\n386\t\n387\tFile these problems under their owning item, not this one, unless this item's own requirement is also broken:\n388\t\n389\t- Memory not separated by tenant, agent type, instance, or task → **R05**\n390\t- Poisoned retrieval documents not quarantined or expired → **R04**\n391\t- Context size not logged for each action decision → **R07**\n392\t- Prompt a parent gave a sub-agent not recorded → **A06**\n393\t\n394\t### Heuristics\n395\t\n396\t**Provable from code:** partly\n397\t\n398\t**Look for:**\n399\t- Each memory entry stores writer, time, task, source input, and context size at write time.\n400\t- Summaries and derived entries keep links to their sources.\n401\t- Code or runbooks to find entries by source or writer, quarantine or delete them, and clear related caches.\n402\t\n403\t**Red flags:**\n404\t- Memory rows with only content and a timestamp, no writer or source fields.\n405\t- Summaries that replace source entries and drop their links.\n406\t- No delete or quarantine function for memory, or one that skips caches and derived entries.\n407\t\n408\t**Where to look:**\n409\t- Memory schema or data model\n410\t- Summarization and compaction code\n411\t- Admin or cleanup scripts\n412\t\n413\t**Needs runtime evidence:** That a tainted entry and its related entries can be found and removed, and later sessions don't retrieve them. Recipe R06.\n414\t\n415\t**N/A when:** Only when the agent has no memory that lasts between runs.\n416\t\n417\t## R07 · G2 · Obs-T2 — Decision-time context size\n418\t\n419\t**Lead:** Agent team. **Shares:** Operations.\n420\t\n421\t### Requirement\n422\t\n423\tFor each action, log how full the model's context was when it decided, in tokens. Document how you count. Say whether each value was measured, estimated, or unavailable. Cover cached input, compaction (shrinking the context), and limits the provider hides. Per-call billing totals don't automatically equal context size. Use them only if your counting method shows they match. A missing value is a clear gap, never zero. Check that the value survives tool handoffs and can be searched next to the action's identity and result. See the [telemetry conventions](otel-conventions.md).\n424\t\n425\t### Verification recipe\n426\t\n427\tCapture decisions with short and long contexts, and a tool handoff. Compare the context sizes logged with the harness's documented counting method and timing, including units and any estimate limits. Include cases with cached input and compacted context. Where available, record compaction with the OpenTelemetry `gen_ai.conversation.compacted` attribute. If that attribute is missing, it means unknown, not false. Every action must have a searchable record, linked to its trace, of the value and how it was measured. Unknown context must be flagged, never reported as zero.\n428\t\n429\t**Evidence to keep:** Counting method, model-request metadata, trace query.\n430\t\n431\t### Checks for likely_pass\n432\t\n433\tRate likely_pass only if every check below is true, with a file and line for each:\n434\t\n435\t- **R07.1** Each action record includes the model's context size in tokens at decision time.\n436\t  - *How to verify:* 1) Find action or tool-call records (trace spans, audit logs). 2) Check each has context size in tokens at decision time. 3) Answer no if the field is missing or recorded only per run, not per action.\n437\t- **R07.2** Each value is labeled measured, estimated, or unavailable, and a missing value is stored as unknown, never as zero.\n438\t  - *How to verify:* 1) Find where context size is recorded. 2) Check a label of measured, estimated, or unavailable, and how missing values are stored. 3) Answer no if there is no label, or a missing value defaults to `0` (`or 0`, `?? 0`, `.get(..., 0)`).\n439\t- **R07.3** The count comes from a stated method that handles cached input and compaction, not a copied per-call billing total.\n440\t  - *How to verify:* 1) Find how context size is counted. 2) Check a stated method covers cached input and compaction. 3) Answer no if it copies `usage.input_tokens` or a billing total with no stated method that matches it, or ignores cache and compaction.\n441\t- **R07.4** The value is carried across tool handoffs and stored with the action's identity, result, and trace ID.\n442\t  - *How to verify:* 1) Trace context size through tool handoffs and sub-agent calls. 2) Check it is stored with action identity, result, and trace ID. 3) Answer no if it is lost on handoff, or stored separately with no link to the action.\n443\t\n444\t### Not covered here\n445\t\n446\tFile these problems under their owning item, not this one, unless this item's own requirement is also broken:\n447\t\n448\t- Behavior not compared across context-size ranges → **R09**\n449\t- Token or cost limits not enforced outside the model → **B07**\n450\t- Action records missing any of the six identity fields → **A02**\n451\t\n452\t### Heuristics\n453\t\n454\t**Provable from code:** partly\n455\t\n456\t**Look for:**\n457\t- Context size in tokens recorded on each action span or log record, for example OpenTelemetry `gen_ai.usage.input_tokens` plus a BRACE context-size attribute per `otel-conventions.md`.\n458\t- A documented counting method that covers cached input and compaction, and marks each value as measured, estimated, or unavailable.\n459\t- Compaction recorded, for example the `gen_ai.conversation.compacted` attribute.\n460\t- The value carried across tool handoffs and stored next to the action's identity and result.\n461\t\n462\t**Red flags:**\n463\t- No token or context-size field on action records.\n464\t- Missing values written as `0` instead of null or 'unknown'.\n465\t- Per-call billing totals used as context size with no stated method showing they match.\n466\t- Context size logged per run only, not per action.\n467\t\n468\t**Where to look:**\n469\t- OpenTelemetry instrumentation and span attributes\n470\t- Model-call wrappers and usage handling\n471\t- Compaction or summarization code\n472\t\n473\t**Needs runtime evidence:** That logged values match the counting method across short, long, cached, and compacted contexts, and are searchable per action. Recipe R07.\n474\t\n475\t**N/A when:** never\n476\t\n477\t## R08 · G3 · C7 — Security monitoring\n478\t\n479\t**Lead:** Security. **Shares:** Operations.\n480\t\n481\t### Requirement\n482\t\n483\tKeep security rules and baselines separate from answer-quality monitoring. Test encoded payloads, unexpected outbound traffic, writes outside scope, privilege escalation, and repeated denied calls. Check that alerts reach a named responder.\n484\t\n485\t### Verification recipe\n486\t\n487\tSend each configured security event through test traffic. Check that the rule fires and reaches the security responder within the target time. Run a harmless control case too, to tell a working detector from an alert that always fires.\n488\t\n489\t**Evidence to keep:** Rule versions, event fixtures, alert and delivery timestamps.\n490\t\n491\t### Checks for likely_pass\n492\t\n493\tRate likely_pass only if every check below is true, with a file and line for each:\n494\t\n495\t- **R08.1** Security detection rules and baselines are defined separately from answer-quality monitoring.\n496\t  - *How to verify:* 1) Find detection rules and monitoring config (SIEM rules, alert config, eval dashboards). 2) Check security rules are separate from answer-quality metrics. 3) Answer no if only quality monitoring exists. Answer unknown if monitoring lives outside this repo.\n497\t- **R08.2** Rules exist for encoded payloads, unexpected outbound traffic, out-of-scope writes, privilege escalation, and repeated denied calls.\n498\t  - *How to verify:* 1) Read the security detection rules. 2) Check for encoded payloads, unexpected outbound traffic, out-of-scope writes, privilege escalation, and repeated denied calls. 3) Answer no if any is missing, or denied calls are never logged so the rule cannot fire.\n499\t- **R08.3** Alerts from these rules route to a named responder or on-call target in config.\n500\t  - *How to verify:* 1) Find alert routing (PagerDuty, Opsgenie, alertmanager receivers, on-call config). 2) Check the security rules route to a named responder. 3) Answer no if alerts go only to a dashboard or log, or the receiver is empty. Answer unknown if routing lives outside this repo.\n501\t\n502\t### Not covered here\n503\t\n504\tFile these problems under their owning item, not this one, unless this item's own requirement is also broken:\n505\t\n506\t- Chains of individually allowed actions going undetected → **R09**\n507\t- No test of a second-model review against evasive behavior → **R10**\n508\t- No drill for a compromised shared tool or agent type → **E12**\n509\t- Rate and cost limits not enforced outside the model → **B07**\n510\t\n511\t### Heuristics\n512\t\n513\t**Provable from code:** partly\n514\t\n515\t**Look for:**\n516\t- Security detection rules kept apart from answer-quality evals, for example SIEM rules, Falco rules, or alert definitions in the repo.\n517\t- Rules for encoded payloads, unexpected outbound traffic, out-of-scope writes, privilege escalation, and repeated denied calls.\n518\t- Denials and policy decisions emitted as events that rules can match.\n519\t- Alert routing to a named responder (for example PagerDuty or Opsgenie routing config).\n520\t\n521\t**Red flags:**\n522\t- Only quality or eval monitoring (LangSmith, Langfuse, Phoenix scores) with no security rules.\n523\t- Denied tool calls logged at DEBUG or not logged at all.\n524\t- Alerts sent to a shared channel with no named owner.\n525\t\n526\t**Where to look:**\n527\t- Detection-as-code repos, SIEM or Falco rule files\n528\t- Alerting and on-call routing config\n529\t- Policy-decision logging in the harness\n530\t\n531\t**Needs runtime evidence:** That each rule fires on test traffic, stays quiet on a harmless control, and reaches the responder in time. Recipe R08. Rules often live outside the agent repo.\n532\t\n533\t**N/A when:** never\n534\t\n535\t## R09 · G3 · C7/Obs-T2 — Sequence and context-aware detection\n536\t\n537\t**Lead:** Security. **Shares:** Operations, Agent team.\n538\t\n539\t### Requirement\n540\t\n541\tTest suspicious chains of actions that are each allowed on their own. Also test unusual fan-out and resource use. Compare behavior across context-size ranges, so one fleet-wide baseline doesn't hide failures in long contexts. If the agent could be hijacked while using only allowed tools, check each high-impact action against the original task before it runs. Use a checker that doesn't see the untrusted content in between, and measure what it misses.\n542\t\n543\t  Why this is hard: Real research and data theft can look alike step by step. Real attack examples may be rare. A detector tuned only to test cases can miss real behavior, or flood responders with false alarms.\n544\t\n545\t  If you can't fully do it: Start with a few clear signals. Examples: an unusual number of reads followed by an outside send, repeated denials, too many sub-agents, or a used-up budget. Measure how many seeded attacks you catch, how many false alarms you raise, and how fast alerts arrive, for each context-size range. Use hard rate and cost limits, and limit destinations, while you improve detection. Say exactly which sequences you cover and which you miss. Basic rules don't prove general sequence detection.\n546\t\n547\t  Tradeoffs you can accept: A narrower, written detection scope, and a measured false-alarm rate that responders can handle. For lower-stakes deployments, defer broader coverage under G3 while keeping hard limits. Where G3 blocks release, reduce the real exposure or finish the required coverage.\n548\t\n549\t### Verification recipe\n550\t\n551\tDefine a labeled harmless sequence and a labeled abusive one, made of calls that are each allowed. An example is too many reads followed by an attempt to send data out. Replay both at the set small and large context sizes. Record catches and false alarms for each size range, against thresholds set before the run. If you screen actions against the original task, include a hijacked sequence that uses only allowed tools. Confirm the checker doesn't receive the untrusted content.\n552\t\n553\t**Evidence to keep:** Labeled sequences, context ranges, thresholds, results for each range.\n554\t\n555\t### Checks for likely_pass\n556\t\n557\tRate likely_pass only if every check below is true, with a file and line for each:\n558\t\n559\t- **R09.1** Detection rules evaluate sequences of calls across a run (for example many reads followed by an outside send), not only single calls.\n560\t  - *How to verify:* 1) Read detection rules. 2) Check rules evaluate sequences across a run, such as many reads then an outside send. 3) Answer no if every rule looks at a single call or event.\n561\t- **R09.2** Rules cover unusual fan-out and resource use.\n562\t  - *How to verify:* 1) Read detection rules. 2) Check rules for unusual fan-out (sub-agents, parallel calls) and resource use. 3) Answer no if neither is covered.\n563\t- **R09.3** Thresholds or baselines are set per context-size range, not as one fleet-wide value.\n564\t  - *How to verify:* 1) Read rule thresholds and baselines. 2) Check they are set per context-size range. 3) Answer no if one fleet-wide value is used for all contexts.\n565\t- **R09.4** If hijack through allowed tools alone is possible, each high-impact action is checked against the original task before it runs, by a checker that is not given the untrusted content.\n566\t  - *How to verify:* 1) Check whether allowed tools alone could cause harm if the agent is hijacked. 2) If so, find a task-alignment checker before high-impact actions and see what input it receives. 3) Answer no if no checker exists, it is given the untrusted content, or its errors let the action proceed.\n567\t\n568\t### Not covered here\n569\t\n570\tFile these problems under their owning item, not this one, unless this item's own requirement is also broken:\n571\t\n572\t- Encoded payloads or writes outside scope going undetected → **R08**\n573\t- No test against a deliberately evasive, misaligned agent → **R10**\n574\t- Context size not logged for each action decision → **R07**\n575\t\n576\t### Heuristics\n577\t\n578\t**Provable from code:** partly\n579\t\n580\t**Look for:**\n581\t- Detection over sequences of allowed actions, for example many reads followed by an outside send, repeated denials, unusual fan-out, or a used-up budget.\n582\t- Thresholds or baselines split by context-size range.\n583\t- A pre-action checker that compares high-impact actions with the original task and does not receive the untrusted content in between.\n584\t- Labeled harmless and abusive sequences used to measure catches and false alarms.\n585\t\n586\t**Red flags:**\n587\t- Only per-call rules; nothing looks at chains of actions.\n588\t- One fleet-wide baseline with no split by context size.\n589\t- A task-alignment checker given the full conversation, including untrusted tool output.\n590\t\n591\t**Where to look:**\n592\t- Detection rules and analytics jobs\n593\t- Pre-execution hooks or guardrails\n594\t- Detection test fixtures\n595\t\n596\t**Needs runtime evidence:** Catch and false-alarm rates per context-size range against thresholds set before the run. Recipe R09.\n597\t\n598\t**N/A when:** never\n599\t\n600\t## R10 · G3 · C7 — Misaligned-behavior review\n601\t\n602\t**Lead:** Security. **Shares:** Agent team, Governance.\n603\t\n604\t### Requirement\n605\t\n606\tIf deliberate misalignment is in scope, test a second model or pattern review against realistic evasive behavior. Record what it can and can't catch. Include what the reviewer can see: actions, arguments, and any visible reasoning. Reasoning the reviewer can't see is a stated gap. Include red-team runs by an agent that knows it is being watched. This review must not replace rule-based access and action controls that don't depend on a model's judgment. Deeper checks on intent belong in a separate AI-control program at the model level.\n607\t\n608\t  Why this is hard: Actions don't reliably reveal hidden intent. A second model can share the first model's blind spots. Catching labeled evasive examples doesn't prove you will catch deliberate misalignment.\n609\t\n610\t  If you can't fully do it: Make narrow claims about behavior you can see. An example is catching attempts to split a forbidden operation across several calls. Keep a record of misses and false positives. Rely on separate permission limits, action approvals, and stop controls for containment. Where detection falls short, reduce autonomy or remove high-impact powers. Record an unproven detector as a gap. Never label the model's intent as safe.\n611\t\n612\t  Tradeoffs you can accept: Uncertainty about hidden intent, as long as claims stay limited to measured behavior. This checklist does not certify alignment. Less autonomy and more review are also fine. Missing required behavior detection still falls under G3.\n613\t\n614\t### Verification recipe\n615\t\n616\tBuild a labeled review set. Include harmless traces and traces with attempted evasion, such as splitting a forbidden operation across calls. Run the reviewer or detector, and record misses and false alarms against the pass criteria you set. Include traces from a red-team agent that was told it is being watched. Record what the reviewer could see and which reasoning was hidden from it. This tests catching visible behavior. It doesn't reveal the model's hidden intent.\n617\t\n618\t**Evidence to keep:** Review-set version, detector or model version, results and stated limits.\n619\t\n620\t### Checks for likely_pass\n621\t\n622\tChecks for likely_pass:\n623\t\n624\t- (none) Code can't show this item. Don't rate it likely_pass; use needs_evidence, gap, or na_candidate.\n625\t\n626\t### Not covered here\n627\t\n628\tFile these problems under their owning item, not this one, unless this item's own requirement is also broken:\n629\t\n630\t- Destructive actions not blocked by a rule-based gate → **B05**\n631\t- A swapped, drifting, or failing checker model not treated as a failure → **A07**\n632\t- Chains of individually allowed actions going undetected → **R09**\n633\t\n634\t### Heuristics\n635\t\n636\t**Provable from code:** no\n637\t\n638\t**Look for:**\n639\t- A second-model or pattern reviewer of actions and arguments, with a written record of what it can see, including whether reasoning is visible.\n640\t- A labeled review set with evasive traces (for example a forbidden operation split across calls) and red-team traces from an agent told it is watched.\n641\t- Rule-based access and action controls that stay in place alongside the reviewer.\n642\t\n643\t**Red flags:**\n644\t- A reviewer model used in place of rule-based permission or approval controls.\n645\t- Claims that the model's intent is safe, with no measured misses or false positives.\n646\t- Reviewer sees only the final answer, not actions and arguments.\n647\t\n648\t**Where to look:**\n649\t- Monitor or reviewer agent code and prompts\n650\t- Red-team and evaluation results\n651\t- Threat model stating whether deliberate misalignment is in scope\n652\t\n653\t**Needs runtime evidence:** Measured misses and false alarms on a labeled evasive set, plus red-team runs. Code can only show a reviewer exists. Recipe R10.\n654\t\n655\t**N/A when:** Only when deliberate misalignment is recorded as out of scope for this deployment, with a reason and approval.\n656\t\n657\t## R11 · G1 · C8 — Tested kill switch\n658\t\n659\t**Lead:** Operations. **Shares:** Infrastructure, Agent team.\n660\t\n661\t### Requirement\n662\t\n663\tName who may stop the agent and how. The stop path must not depend on the agent's cooperation. The agent's credentials must not be able to change it. Set a maximum stop time that fits the potential harm. Run a dated drill on the current deployment. It must stop new actions, running work, and open tool streams, subscriptions, and tasks. Include an attempt by the agent to disable or delay the stop. Cover sub-agents and their children in the ecosystem drill (E09).\n664\t\n665\t### Verification recipe\n666\t\n667\tStart a controlled run and trigger the operator's stop control. Record the time of the last allowed action and the final stop. Repeat while a tool call is in progress. Have the agent use its own credentials and tools to try to disable, change, or delay the stop path. It must fail. All work must stop within the time limit. Use E09 for sub-agents and their children.\n668\t\n669\t**Evidence to keep:** Drill trace, operator action, measured stop time.\n670\t\n671\t### Checks for likely_pass\n672\t\n673\tRate likely_pass only if every check below is true, with a file and line for each:\n674\t\n675\t- **R11.1** A stop control halts new model calls and new tool calls when set.\n676\t  - *How to verify:* 1) Find the stop control (kill flag, `stop`, `halt`, feature flag, revoked token). 2) Check the loop checks it before every model call and tool call. 3) Answer no if it is checked only at run start, only between turns, a second loop or worker skips it, or a check error is treated as not stopped.\n677\t- **R11.2** The agent's own credentials and tools cannot set, clear, delete, or delay the stop control.\n678\t  - *How to verify:* 1) Find where the stop flag is stored and who can write it. 2) Compare with the agent's credentials and tools. 3) Answer no if the agent's role can write, delete, or expire the flag, a tool can reach its store, or the agent can delay it (for example by catching the stop signal).\n679\t- **R11.3** Tool calls already running are cancelled or bounded by a timeout when the stop is set.\n680\t  - *How to verify:* 1) Find the stop handler. 2) Check running tool calls are cancelled or bounded by a timeout. 3) Answer no if running calls finish with no timeout, or cancellation errors are swallowed and the call continues.\n681\t- **R11.4** The stop also closes open tool streams, subscriptions, and background tasks.\n682\t  - *How to verify:* 1) Find streams, subscriptions, and background tasks (`asyncio.create_task`, goroutines, websockets, SSE). 2) Check the stop handler closes them. 3) Answer no if any is left open, detached, or untracked.\n683\t- **R11.5** Only named operator identities can set the stop control.\n684\t  - *How to verify:* 1) Find who can set the stop (auth on the endpoint, IAM on the flag store). 2) Answer no if any authenticated user or service can set it with no named operator list. Answer unknown if access lives outside this repo.\n685\t\n686\t### Not covered here\n687\t\n688\tFile these problems under their owning item, not this one, unless this item's own requirement is also broken:\n689\t\n690\t- Stop signal not reaching sub-agents, children, or remote workers → **E09**\n691\t- In-flight writes, queued actions, or unattended restarts after the stop → **R12**\n692\t- No way to cut off the agent's access without cutting off the user → **B04**\n693\t\n694\t### Heuristics\n695\t\n696\t**Provable from code:** partly\n697\t\n698\t**Look for:**\n699\t- A stop path outside the agent's control, for example an operator-only flag in a separate service, scaling the deployment to zero, revoking the agent identity, or a gateway kill rule.\n700\t- The agent's credentials can't write to the stop flag or its config (check IAM and RBAC).\n701\t- The harness checks the stop signal before each action and cancels running tool calls, streams, subscriptions, and background tasks.\n702\t- A written list of who may stop the agent and a maximum stop time.\n703\t\n704\t**Red flags:**\n705\t- Stop works by sending the agent a message or prompt asking it to halt.\n706\t- Stop flag stored where the agent can write, for example the same database, config map, or memory it uses.\n707\t- Stop checked only between turns, with no cancel for in-flight tool calls or streams.\n708\t- Sub-agents or background jobs not reached by the stop signal.\n709\t\n710\t**Where to look:**\n711\t- Runner loop and cancellation handling\n712\t- Ops scripts and runbooks for stopping the agent\n713\t- IAM/RBAC for the stop control\n714\t\n715\t**Needs runtime evidence:** A dated drill on the current deployment with measured stop time, including an in-progress tool call and an agent attempt to block the stop. Recipe R11. Sub-agents are covered by E09.\n716\t\n717\t**N/A when:** never\n718\t\n719\t## R12 · G1 · C8 — Safe state after stopping\n720\t\n721\t**Lead:** Agent team. **Shares:** Operations, Business owner.\n722\t\n723\t### Requirement\n724\t\n725\tDecide what happens to writes in progress, queued actions, and outside side effects. Where you can't cancel something, show how you check and fix the result (reconcile) or undo it another way (compensate). No restart may happen unattended, and no orphaned work may be left able to act.\n726\t\n727\t  Why this is hard: An outside action may already be done when the stop arrives. Emails can't reliably be unsent. Payments, writes, and queued jobs each cancel and reconcile differently.\n728\t\n729\t  If you can't fully do it: For each operation, record whether it can be blocked, canceled, or only reconciled afterward. Stop new work from being sent, turn off retries, check the destination's state, and send unclear results to a named operator. For operations with no workable recovery path, remove autonomous execution or use an approved draft-then-commit flow. Measure stopping new work separately from resolving work already done. Stopping the process alone does not pass this check.\n730\t\n731\t  Tradeoffs you can accept: Slower manual reconciliation and fewer autonomous actions. Some completed effects may need reconciling rather than reversing, following the written safe-state procedure. Untracked effects, or work that keeps its authority past the stop time limit, still block release.\n732\t\n733\t### Verification recipe\n734\t\n735\tStop runs before a write, during a transaction, and after a finished outside test action. Check the queues and the destination's state. Each case must end canceled, completed, or reconciled. Or it must be on a tracked hold for reconciliation, with an owner, no dependent actions, and no blind retries. No unexplained orphaned work or restart is acceptable.\n736\t\n737\t**Evidence to keep:** Outcome of each scenario, queue snapshots, undo and reconciliation records.\n738\t\n739\t### Checks for likely_pass\n740\t\n741\tRate likely_pass only if every check below is true, with a file and line for each:\n742\t\n743\t- **R12.1** For each operation type, code or config states whether it can be cancelled, completed, or only reconciled after a stop.\n744\t  - *How to verify:* 1) Search code and config for a per-operation stop policy (cancel, complete, reconcile). 2) Answer no if any operation type the agent can run has no stated stop behavior.\n745\t- **R12.2** On stop, queued actions are cancelled or held, and automatic retries are turned off.\n746\t  - *How to verify:* 1) Find queue and retry code (`retry`, `backoff`, queue workers). 2) Check stop cancels or holds queued items and turns retries off. 3) Answer no if retries continue after stop or queued jobs still run.\n747\t- **R12.3** Unclear or in-progress outside actions go on a tracked hold with an owner, and dependent actions are blocked until reconciled.\n748\t  - *How to verify:* 1) Find handling of unclear or in-progress outside actions at stop. 2) Check they go on a tracked hold with an owner and block dependent actions. 3) Answer no if they are dropped, retried, or dependents proceed.\n749\t- **R12.4** A stopped run does not restart on its own; restart needs an operator action.\n750\t  - *How to verify:* 1) Find restart logic (supervisors, `restartPolicy`, cron, auto-resume). 2) Answer no if a stopped run restarts automatically (`restartPolicy: Always`, auto-resume from checkpoint) without an operator action.\n751\t- **R12.5** Workers and queued jobs lose their credentials or authority at stop, so no orphaned work can act.\n752\t  - *How to verify:* 1) Find workers and queued jobs and the credentials they hold. 2) Check stop revokes or expires them. 3) Answer no if workers keep long-lived credentials after stop, or jobs carry their own tokens that stay valid.\n753\t\n754\t### Not covered here\n755\t\n756\tFile these problems under their owning item, not this one, unless this item's own requirement is also broken:\n757\t\n758\t- Stop path depends on the agent or has no tested stop time → **R11**\n759\t- Recovery replay repeating irreversible actions or using tampered checkpoints → **R14**\n760\t- Rollback restoring revoked permissions → **C07**\n761\t\n762\t### Heuristics\n763\t\n764\t**Provable from code:** partly\n765\t\n766\t**Look for:**\n767\t- A per-operation record of whether it can be blocked, canceled, or only reconciled afterward.\n768\t- On stop: queues drained or held, retries turned off, and in-flight transactions rolled back.\n769\t- Reconcile or compensate code for outside side effects, and unclear results sent to a named operator.\n770\t- Restart requires an operator; no auto-restart policy resumes agent work.\n771\t\n772\t**Red flags:**\n773\t- Kubernetes `restartPolicy: Always` or a supervisor that resumes the agent's queue after a stop with no human step.\n774\t- Queued jobs or scheduled tasks that keep their credentials and run after the stop.\n775\t- Retries with backoff on outside actions that keep going during or after a stop.\n776\t- Stop handled only by killing the process.\n777\t\n778\t**Where to look:**\n779\t- Job queues, workers, and schedulers\n780\t- Shutdown and signal handlers\n781\t- Retry config and compensation code\n782\t\n783\t**Needs runtime evidence:** Outcomes of stops before a write, mid-transaction, and after an outside action, with queue and destination snapshots. Recipe R12.\n784\t\n785\t**N/A when:** never\n786\t\n787\t## R13 · G1 · C9 — Full execution audit\n788\t\n789\t**Lead:** Agent team. **Shares:** Infrastructure, Operations.\n790\t\n791\t### Requirement\n792\t\n793\tRecord each run as an execution graph: a linked record of every step. It must include proposed actions, tool calls and key arguments, and authorization decisions with the policy version used. It must also include denials, approvals with their IDs, results, errors, and timestamps. Rebuild a typical run from this record, including failed actions. Don't rely on the run's final answer.\n794\t\n795\t  Why this is hard: Agent logs usually show what the agent tried. The destination system knows whether it actually happened. A timeout can mean the call failed, or it worked and the reply was lost. So a local trace can look complete and still have an unknown result.\n796\t\n797\t  If you can't fully do it: Give each operation an ID before you send it. Keep receipts or status checks from the destination where you can. Match attempts against the destination's records, and mark unresolved results clearly. Pause dependent actions and retries until each one is resolved. If an integration can't give you enough evidence to trace and recover, limit or remove that path. Sampling and the agent's own reports can't replace the required action record.\n798\t\n799\t  Tradeoffs you can accept: Pausing dependent work while an unclear result gets resolved. Keep the attempt, the uncertainty, and the final answer in the graph. Less automation is a fair cost. Missing or made-up action evidence is not a G1 pass.\n800\t\n801\t### Verification recipe\n802\t\n803\tRun a known sequence with an allowed call, a denial, an approval, a tool error, and a retry. Confirm each decision record names the policy version, and each approved action names its approval ID. Rebuild the graph using only stored audit records. Compare it with the test runner's and the destination's logs. Every expected action must be accounted for. Unresolved results at the destination must be marked clearly, with dependent work paused until they are reconciled.\n804\t\n805\t**Evidence to keep:** Expected event list, rebuilt graph, query for missing events.\n806\t\n807\t### Checks for likely_pass\n808\t\n809\tRate likely_pass only if every check below is true, with a file and line for each:\n810\t\n811\t- **R13.1** Each step writes a record linked to the run: proposed action, tool call with key arguments, result, error, and timestamp.\n812\t  - *How to verify:* 1) Find step logging (trace spans, audit log writes). 2) Check each record has run ID, proposed action, tool call with key arguments, result, error, and timestamp. 3) Answer no if any field is missing, or logging happens only on the success branch.\n813\t- **R13.2** Authorization decisions are recorded with the policy version, including denials, not only allowed calls.\n814\t  - *How to verify:* 1) Find authorization decision code. 2) Check both allow and deny are logged with the policy version. 3) Answer no if denials are not logged, or no policy version is recorded.\n815\t- **R13.3** Approved actions record their approval ID.\n816\t  - *How to verify:* 1) Find where approved actions are logged. 2) Answer no if the approval ID is not recorded with the action.\n817\t- **R13.4** Tool errors, timeouts, and retries are recorded, and each operation gets an ID before it is sent, so unclear results can be matched to the destination's records.\n818\t  - *How to verify:* 1) Find error, timeout, and retry handling. 2) Check each is logged and each operation gets an ID before sending. 3) Answer no if `except`/`catch` blocks skip logging, retries are not logged, or the ID is created after the response.\n819\t- **R13.5** Records are written by the harness or gateway, not by the model, and do not depend on the run's final answer.\n820\t  - *How to verify:* 1) Find who writes records. 2) Answer no if the model writes them via a tool, records are built from the final answer, or logging runs only if the run completes.\n821\t\n822\t### Not covered here\n823\t\n824\tFile these problems under their owning item, not this one, unless this item's own requirement is also broken:\n825\t\n826\t- Action records missing any of the six identity fields → **A02**\n827\t- Agent able to erase or rewrite its own audit history → **E10**\n828\t- Audit retention, access, redaction, or export rules missing → **E11**\n829\t- Run can't be rebuilt across gateway, tool services, and delegated workers → **E10**\n830\t- Prompt a parent gave a sub-agent not recorded → **A06**\n831\t\n832\t### Heuristics\n833\t\n834\t**Provable from code:** partly\n835\t\n836\t**Look for:**\n837\t- A linked trace per run (for example OpenTelemetry spans with parent links) covering proposed actions, tool calls with key arguments, authorization decisions with policy version, denials, approvals with IDs, results, errors, and timestamps.\n838\t- An operation ID assigned before each outside call, and destination receipts or status checks stored.\n839\t- Unresolved results marked clearly, with dependent actions paused.\n840\t- Audit records written to a store the agent can't change.\n841\t\n842\t**Red flags:**\n843\t- Only the final answer or chat transcript is stored.\n844\t- Denials and approval decisions not logged, or logged without the policy version.\n845\t- Trace sampling (for example `TraceIdRatioBased` below 1.0) applied to the action audit.\n846\t- Timeouts logged as failures with no marker that the result is unknown.\n847\t\n848\t**Where to look:**\n849\t- Tracing and logging setup, span attributes\n850\t- Policy and approval decision code\n851\t- Audit storage config and retention\n852\t\n853\t**Needs runtime evidence:** That a known run with an allowed call, denial, approval, error, and retry can be rebuilt from stored records alone and matches destination logs. Recipe R13.\n854\t\n855\t**N/A when:** never\n856\t\n857\t## R14 · G2 · C9 — Recovery integrity\n858\t\n859\t**Lead:** Agent team. **Shares:** Infrastructure, Operations.\n860\t\n861\t### Requirement\n862\t\n863\tIf logs or checkpoints drive recovery, make them tamper-evident. Before using them, verify them against a separately protected trust anchor or signing key. Test that verification rejects changed checkpoints. It must also catch truncation, rollback to an older valid checkpoint, and replacement of a whole hash chain. For automatic recovery, confirm the destination supports idempotency: sending the same request twice has the effect of sending it once. Replay only within the destination's documented scope and time window. Confirm that actions you can't undo don't happen twice. Where support is missing or the result is unclear, check that replay pauses for reconciliation instead of sending again.\n864\t\n865\t  Why this is hard: Not every outside API supports idempotency keys or a reliable status check. A crash between a successful remote action and the local checkpoint can make replay repeat that action. A local table of past requests doesn't close that gap.\n866\t\n867\t  If you can't fully do it: Use idempotency enforced by the destination where it exists. Write a durable operation record before sending. If the result is unclear, pause replay and reconcile using the destination's evidence or an operator. Don't retry blindly. Turn off automatic replay for side effects that don't support it, and document that limit. Reject checkpoints you can't verify, and recover from a trusted state. Manual recovery doesn't prove automatic replay is safe.\n868\t\n869\t  Tradeoffs you can accept: Manual recovery, slower completion, and no automatic replay for unsupported integrations. Never call blind retries of unclear, permanent actions \"safe replay.\" Remaining recovery-control gaps need allowed G2 acceptance.\n870\t\n871\t### Verification recipe\n872\t\n873\tChange a copy of a checkpoint or audit record. Recovery must reject it. Also cut off, roll back, and replace the chain or checkpoint. Verification must use a separately protected trust anchor. For integrations that support safe automatic replay, replay a good checkpoint twice. The action must happen once, with the duplicate recorded as blocked. For integrations without that support, inject an unclear result. Check that replay pauses for reconciliation with the destination and sends no duplicate.\n874\t\n875\t**Evidence to keep:** Integrity check result, idempotency results enforced by the destination, action counts, manual-recovery hold and outcome.\n876\t\n877\t### Checks for likely_pass\n878\t\n879\tRate likely_pass only if every check below is true, with a file and line for each:\n880\t\n881\t- **R14.1** Checkpoints and audit records used for recovery are hash-chained or signed.\n882\t  - *How to verify:* 1) Find checkpoint and audit storage used for recovery. 2) Check for hash chaining or signatures. 3) Answer no if records are plain rows or files with no chain or signature.\n883\t- **R14.2** Recovery verifies them against a key or trust anchor stored separately from the records, and refuses any that fail.\n884\t  - *How to verify:* 1) Find recovery code that loads checkpoints. 2) Check it verifies against a key stored separately and refuses failures. 3) Answer no if the key sits in the same store, verification is skipped, or failure is logged and recovery continues.\n885\t- **R14.3** Verification catches truncation, rollback to an older valid checkpoint, and replacement of the whole chain, for example with a signed head or sequence number held outside the store.\n886\t  - *How to verify:* 1) Check verification uses a signed head or sequence number held outside the store. 2) Answer no if truncation, rollback to an older valid checkpoint, or replacing the whole chain would still pass.\n887\t- **R14.4** Automatic replay sends idempotency keys that the destination enforces, and only for integrations that support them.\n888\t  - *How to verify:* 1) Find replay code. 2) Check it sends idempotency keys (`Idempotency-Key`, `client_token`) only to integrations that enforce them. 3) Answer no if replay sends without keys, uses a local dedupe table as the only guard, or replays to integrations without support.\n889\t- **R14.5** When idempotency is missing or the result is unclear, replay pauses for reconciliation instead of resending.\n890\t  - *How to verify:* 1) Find replay handling for unclear results or missing idempotency. 2) Answer no if replay resends instead of pausing for reconciliation, or retry is the default on timeout.\n891\t\n892\t### Not covered here\n893\t\n894\tFile these problems under their owning item, not this one, unless this item's own requirement is also broken:\n895\t\n896\t- What happens to in-flight writes and queued actions at stop → **R12**\n897\t- Rollback to an approved earlier release not tested → **C07**\n898\t- Agent able to delete or rewrite its own audit history → **E10**\n899\t\n900\t### Heuristics\n901\t\n902\t**Provable from code:** partly\n903\t\n904\t**Look for:**\n905\t- Checkpoints and recovery logs signed or hash-chained, and verified against a separately protected key or trust anchor before use.\n906\t- Checks that catch truncation, rollback to an older checkpoint, and replacement of a whole chain (for example a signed head or sequence number stored elsewhere).\n907\t- Idempotency keys sent to destinations that support them, for example `Idempotency-Key` headers.\n908\t- A durable operation record written before sending, and replay that pauses for reconciliation when a result is unclear.\n909\t\n910\t**Red flags:**\n911\t- LangGraph or similar checkpoints loaded from a writable store with no integrity check.\n912\t- Hash chain verified only against itself, with no outside anchor.\n913\t- Automatic replay or retry of payments, emails, or other permanent actions with no idempotency key.\n914\t- A local 'already sent' table used as the only duplicate guard.\n915\t\n916\t**Where to look:**\n917\t- Checkpointer and resume code\n918\t- Retry and replay logic in tool clients\n919\t- Audit log storage and signing setup\n920\t\n921\t**Needs runtime evidence:** That changed, truncated, rolled-back, and replaced checkpoints are rejected, and replays happen once or pause. Recipe R14.\n922\t\n923\t**N/A when:** Only when no logs or checkpoints drive recovery and there is no automatic replay.\n924\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"29563294-217c-407e-8b87-42521726a071","uuid":"52aa5bbb-95b2-47c1-a27b-7ab844996327","timestamp":"2026-10-02T16:12:12.243Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/private/var/folders/6v/yk9bzvrn5zx0kxll7d4szzb80000gn/T/brace-repeat-80qi2mwl/skill/references/run-time.md","content":"<!-- Generated by skills/gen_references.py from CHECKLIST.md, CHECKLIST-VERIFICATION.md, ROLES.md, and heuristics. Do not edit by hand. -->\n\n# R — Run-time\n\nUse this file to rate each item from the code and configuration. The requirement is the checklist text. The heuristics say what to look for. Ratings: likely_pass, gap, needs_evidence, or na_candidate. Never rate pass.\n\n## R01 · G2 · C5 — Validate every input boundary, including API responses\n\n**Lead:** Agent team. **Shares:** Security.\n\n### Requirement\n\nList every input the agent receives. That includes API responses (errors and streamed content too), tool and connector output, incoming webhooks and callbacks, messages from other agents, user input, fetched pages, files, and retrieved chunks. Authenticate each sender (prove who sent it), and verify signatures where they exist. Treat content as untrusted even when it comes from an authenticated service or an internal one. Before content reaches the model or runs anywhere, check its type, schema, and size. Also check its content for attack payloads and hidden prompt-injection instructions. Reject or hold aside bad payloads. If a required check fails to run, don't let unchecked content through. Test broken, oversized, faked, and hostile responses from each integration.\n\n  Why this is hard: An API response can be well-formed, correctly signed, and still carry harmful instructions. Free text and streamed responses are hard to inspect fully. Passing a schema check proves the structure, not that the content is safe.\n\n  If you can't fully do it: Start with type checks, size limits, sender checks, and rejecting broken content. Add content checks for attack patterns you've seen. Buffer or limit streams when the required checks can't run before use. Keep permissions narrow, and use R02 to contain instructions that slip through. Record which integrations and payload types you tested and which you didn't.\n\n  Tradeoffs you can accept: Buffering delay, rejecting some valid responses, or supporting fewer payload types. Incomplete detection of attacks hidden in meaning can get G2 risk acceptance. That needs tested containment and clear limits on what was covered. Schema checks alone don't fully protect against injection.\n\n### Verification recipe\n\nHave a mock integration return wrong types, oversized bodies, hostile text, error responses, and streamed chunks. Where signatures apply, send a callback with a bad signature. Bad payloads must be rejected or held aside before the model sees them or anything runs them. Turn off a required check and confirm the system fails closed.\n\n**Evidence to keep:** Fixtures for each integration, validation decisions, checks of what was passed on.\n\n### Checks for likely_pass\n\nRate likely_pass only if every check below is true, with a file and line for each:\n\n- **R01.1** Every input path passes through validation code: API and tool responses (including errors and streams), webhooks and callbacks, agent messages, user input, fetched pages, files, and retrieved chunks.\n  - *How to verify:* 1) List every input: tool and API responses (errors, streams), webhooks, agent messages, user input, fetched pages, files, retrieved chunks. 2) Trace each to where it enters the model context. 3) Answer no if any path, including error bodies, stream chunks, or a second handler, reaches the model without passing validation code.\n- **R01.2** Type, schema, and size checks run before content, including streamed chunks, is added to the model context or executed.\n  - *How to verify:* 1) Find validation code (`pydantic`, `zod`, `jsonschema`, size limits, `max_bytes`, content-type checks). 2) Check it runs before content is added to the context or executed, including per stream chunk. 3) Answer no if validation runs after use, only on success responses, streams skip it, or there is no size limit.\n- **R01.3** Senders are authenticated, and signatures are verified where the integration provides them, including for internal and authenticated services.\n  - *How to verify:* 1) Find webhook, callback, and inbound handlers. 2) Check sender authentication and signature verification (`hmac.compare_digest`, `verify_signature`, mTLS). 3) Answer no if any handler skips it, internal services are trusted without auth, the signature is compared with `==`, or a verify failure is caught and processing continues.\n- **R01.4** Content is also screened for attack payloads or injection instructions, not only for structure.\n  - *How to verify:* 1) Find content screening (injection detector, pattern rules, classifier, encoded-payload checks). 2) Check it runs on content, not just schema. 3) Answer no if only type or schema checks exist, or screening covers some inputs (for example user input) but not tool output, fetched pages, or retrieved chunks.\n- **R01.5** Bad payloads are rejected or held aside, and if a check errors, times out, or is skipped, the content is blocked.\n  - *How to verify:* 1) Find what happens when validation fails or errors. 2) Read `try`/`except`, `catch`, timeout, and default branches. 3) Answer no if failures log and continue, an error or timeout passes content through, a feature flag can skip checks, or bad content is truncated and still used.\n\n### Not covered here\n\nFile these problems under their owning item, not this one, unless this item's own requirement is also broken:\n\n- Injected instructions in valid content reaching forbidden tools unblocked → **R02**\n- Hidden instructions in tool names, descriptions, or schemas → **E04**\n- Replayed peer-agent requests with no freshness or nonce check → **E07**\n- Secrets leaking into outputs or normal logs → **R03**\n- Unapproved sources able to add documents to the retrieval corpus → **R04**\n\n### Heuristics\n\n**Provable from code:** partly\n\n**Look for:**\n- A list of every input source: user input, API responses (errors and streams), tool and MCP output, webhooks and callbacks, messages from other agents, fetched pages, files, and retrieved chunks.\n- Schema and type checks on tool and API results before they reach the model, for example Pydantic or Zod models, JSON Schema, or OpenAI Agents SDK output types.\n- Size limits on bodies, files, and streamed chunks, enforced before content is used.\n- Sender checks: webhook signature verification (for example HMAC with constant-time compare), mTLS, or token checks on callbacks.\n- A content check for injection or attack payloads (for example an input guardrail or scanner) with a reject or quarantine path.\n- Fail-closed handling: if a validator errors or times out, the content is dropped or held, not passed on.\n\n**Red flags:**\n- Raw `response.text` or tool output passed straight into messages with no type, size, or content check.\n- Webhook handlers that parse the body without verifying a signature, or compare signatures with `==`.\n- `try: validate(x) except Exception: pass` or similar code that forwards content when a check fails.\n- Content from internal services or authenticated APIs trusted by default with no checks.\n- Streamed responses fed token by token to the model or a tool with no limit or buffering.\n\n**Where to look:**\n- Tool wrappers and MCP client code\n- HTTP clients, webhook and callback handlers\n- Retrieval and file-loading code\n- Guardrail config (OpenAI Agents SDK input guardrails, NeMo Guardrails, Claude Agent SDK `PostToolUse` hooks)\n\n**Needs runtime evidence:** That hostile, oversized, faked, and broken payloads from each integration are rejected, and that disabling a check fails closed. Recipe R01 uses mock integrations.\n\n**N/A when:** never\n\n## R02 · G2 · C5 — Separate data from instructions and test injection containment\n\n**Lead:** Security. **Shares:** Agent team.\n\n### Requirement\n\nKeep API responses, connector messages, and other outside content out of trusted system and policy channels. Label where each piece came from and how much it is trusted. Test prompt injection in responses that pass schema checks. Include free-text fields, error messages, documents, and tool output. Checks and channel separation can't guarantee that prompt injection is prevented. So show that credential limits, tool permissions, and destructive-action gates still block harm if the model follows an injected instruction. Repeat each attack and include adaptive versions. One failed attempt makes a defense look stronger than it is. B05 covers agents that combine untrusted input, sensitive access, and outside actions.\n\n  Why this is hard: A model may follow an instruction hidden in normal-looking data, even with separate channels. A fixed set of injection tests can't prove that every injection will fail. Model behavior can also change from run to run.\n\n  If you can't fully do it: Assume some injections will work. Send the forbidden calls they would cause straight to your credential limits, egress rules, and action gates. Also run full end-to-end examples. Limit the tools and data the agent can reach. When containment is uncertain, make high-impact actions draft-only, or require approval for each one. Report structure checks, injection detection, and blocked side effects as separate results.\n\n  Tradeoffs you can accept: Fewer finished tasks, fewer tools, and more approvals, each for one specific action. Remaining detection gaps can get G2 acceptance with clear exposure and separate proof of containment. The required access and destructive-action gates still apply.\n\n### Verification recipe\n\nHide an instruction to send a fake secret to your test destination. Put it inside a schema-valid API response, document, or tool result. Inspect the model request that gets built, and check that data and instructions are kept apart. Also send the forbidden tool call this would cause straight to enforcement. No secret may reach the destination. Repeat each fixture a set number of times, and add adaptive versions. Report the success rate per attempt and across all attempts, grouped by how serious the action is. Detector results don't count as proof of containment.\n\n**Evidence to keep:** Model request with role and source labels, attempt counts and success rates, gate denial, destination receipt log.\n\n### Checks for likely_pass\n\nRate likely_pass only if every check below is true, with a file and line for each:\n\n- **R02.1** Outside content (API responses, tool output, documents, connector messages) goes into user or tool message roles, never into the system or policy prompt.\n  - *How to verify:* 1) Find where model requests are built (`system=`, `messages`, `role: system`, prompt templates). 2) Trace outside content (tool output, documents, API responses) into the request. 3) Answer no if any outside content is concatenated into the system or policy prompt, including retrieved context or tool descriptions from servers.\n- **R02.2** Each piece of outside content carries a source and trust label in the model request.\n  - *How to verify:* 1) Find where outside content is wrapped before the model request. 2) Check each piece carries a source and trust label. 3) Answer no if some sources (for example tool output or retrieved chunks) have no label, or labels are set by the content itself.\n- **R02.3** Credential limits, tool permissions, and B05 action gates block the actions an injected instruction could cause, enforced in code the model cannot change.\n  - *How to verify:* 1) List actions an injected instruction could cause: sends, writes, deletes, fetches. 2) Check each is blocked by credential scope, tool allowlist, or B05 gate in harness code. 3) Answer no if any harmful action is stopped only by the model declining, or the limit can be changed by tool arguments or model output.\n- **R02.4** Containment does not rely on prompt wording, delimiters, or an injection detector.\n  - *How to verify:* 1) Find the defenses relied on for injection. 2) Check whether removing prompt wording, delimiters, or the detector would leave harmful actions blocked. 3) Answer no if containment depends on system-prompt rules, delimiter tags, or a detector, with no code-level block behind it.\n- **R02.5** Injection tests repeat each fixture a set number of times, include adaptive variants and schema-valid payloads, and report success rates.\n  - *How to verify:* 1) Find injection tests (`tests/`, eval suites, red-team fixtures). 2) Check fixtures repeat a set number of times, include adaptive variants and schema-valid payloads, and report a success rate. 3) Answer no if each test runs once, uses only malformed inputs, or reports pass/fail. Answer unknown if tests live outside this repo.\n\n### Not covered here\n\nFile these problems under their owning item, not this one, unless this item's own requirement is also broken:\n\n- Missing type, schema, or size checks on API responses → **R01**\n- Hidden instructions in tool names, descriptions, or schemas → **E04**\n- One run planting instructions in another run's memory → **R05**\n- Generated output run as SQL or shell without parameterizing → **R03**\n\n### Heuristics\n\n**Provable from code:** partly\n\n**Look for:**\n- Tool output and outside content placed in tool-result or user-role messages, never in the system or developer message.\n- Source and trust labels on content, for example wrapping in tagged blocks with origin metadata.\n- Containment in code that doesn't depend on the model: narrow credentials (B02), tool allowlists (B01), egress rules (B09), and destructive-action gates (B05).\n- Injection tests that repeat each attack and include adaptive versions, with results reported as structure checks, detection, and blocked side effects separately.\n- High-impact actions draft-only or approval-gated where containment is uncertain.\n\n**Red flags:**\n- Untrusted tool output concatenated into the system message or a policy prompt.\n- Injection detection (a classifier, regex, or guardrail model) treated as the whole defense; detection alone is not containment.\n- Retrieved documents or web pages formatted as instructions ('Follow these steps: ...') in the prompt.\n- Injection tests run once each with no repeats or adaptive variants.\n- The agent can read untrusted content and also send data out with no gate between them.\n\n**Where to look:**\n- Prompt and message-building code\n- Retrieval and tool-result formatting\n- Red-team or injection test suites\n- Action gates, credential scopes, and egress config that contain a followed injection\n\n**Needs runtime evidence:** Success rates of planted injections, and proof that forbidden calls are blocked at enforcement. Recipe R02: plant an exfiltration instruction in schema-valid content, repeat with adaptive versions, and check the destination log.\n\n**N/A when:** never\n\n## R03 · G2 · C5/C9 — Prevent output and log leakage\n\n**Lead:** Agent team. **Shares:** Security, Data and privacy, Infrastructure.\n\n### Requirement\n\nRemove secrets and limit sensitive data before tool output, debug output, or responses reach the model, outside recipients, or normal logs. Some clients fetch URLs on their own when they display output, such as Markdown images and link previews. These can leak data without any tool call, so block them or allow only approved URLs. Check generated output before it goes into SQL, a shell, an HTML page, or anything else that runs it. Use parameterized queries and commands (for example, argument lists for shell calls), and the right escaping for each context. Test with fake secrets and injection payloads. Confirm that leaks and unintended execution are blocked, and that redacted audit evidence is kept.\n\n### Verification recipe\n\nReturn an easy-to-spot fake secret in tool output, debug output, and an API error. Trigger a normal run. Search model inputs, normal logs, and outside responses for the marker. It must be removed or properly restricted before it reaches any of them. Get the agent to produce a Markdown image or link whose URL carries the marker to your test destination. The display client must not fetch it. Also feed generated SQL, shell, and HTML payloads into the systems that use them. Check that parameterized queries and escaping stop the payloads from running.\n\n**Evidence to keep:** Marker test, redaction decisions, searches of each output path.\n\n### Checks for likely_pass\n\nRate likely_pass only if every check below is true, with a file and line for each:\n\n- **R03.1** Secrets and sensitive fields are redacted from tool output, debug output, and error text before they reach the model, outside recipients, or normal logs.\n  - *How to verify:* 1) Find redaction code (`redact`, `mask`, secret patterns) and where tool output, errors, and debug text go. 2) Check redaction runs before the model, outside recipients, and logs. 3) Answer no if any path skips it, such as error messages, exception traces, debug logging, or `logger.info(response)` before redaction.\n- **R03.2** The display client blocks auto-fetched URLs (Markdown images, link previews) or allows only approved hosts.\n  - *How to verify:* 1) Find the display client or renderer (Markdown render, link unfurl, image tags). 2) Check auto-fetched URLs are blocked or limited to approved hosts (CSP `img-src`, URL allowlist). 3) Answer no if Markdown images or previews load any URL. Answer unknown if the display client is outside this repo.\n- **R03.3** Generated SQL uses parameterized queries, shell calls use argument lists with no string-built commands, and HTML output is escaped for its context.\n  - *How to verify:* 1) Search for SQL, shell, and HTML built from generated output (`execute(f`, `+ query`, `shell=True`, `exec(`, `innerHTML`, `dangerouslySetInnerHTML`, `|safe`). 2) Check for parameters, argument lists, and context escaping. 3) Answer no if any query or command is string-built, uses `shell=True` or `sh -c` with model input, or HTML is unescaped.\n- **R03.4** Redacted audit evidence is kept rather than discarded.\n  - *How to verify:* 1) Find redaction code and audit logging. 2) Check redacted records are written to an audit store. 3) Answer no if redaction drops the whole record, or the evidence is discarded instead of kept.\n\n### Not covered here\n\nFile these problems under their owning item, not this one, unless this item's own requirement is also broken:\n\n- A tool call sending data to an unapproved destination → **B09**\n- Long-lived secrets checked into config or baked into images → **B03**\n- Incoming hostile payloads not validated → **R01**\n- Retrieval returning another tenant's documents → **R04**\n\n### Heuristics\n\n**Provable from code:** partly\n\n**Look for:**\n- Redaction of secrets and sensitive data before tool output, debug output, or errors reach the model, logs, or outside recipients (for example log filters, OpenTelemetry processors that drop or mask attributes).\n- Parameterized SQL (`cursor.execute(sql, params)`), argument lists for shell calls, and context-correct escaping for HTML.\n- Markdown images and link previews blocked or limited to approved URLs in the client that shows agent output (for example a CSP `img-src` allowlist or a sanitizer that strips external images).\n- Tests with fake secret markers searched across model inputs, logs, and outside responses.\n\n**Red flags:**\n- `subprocess(..., shell=True)` or `os.system` with model-generated text.\n- SQL built with f-strings, `+`, or `.format()` from model output.\n- Model output rendered as HTML with `innerHTML`, `dangerouslySetInnerHTML`, or `|safe` without sanitizing.\n- Full prompts, tool payloads, or exception traces with credentials logged at INFO, or OpenTelemetry content capture (for example `OTEL_INSTRUMENTATION_GENAI_CAPTURE_MESSAGE_CONTENT=true`) on with no redaction.\n- Chat UI renders Markdown images from any URL.\n\n**Where to look:**\n- Shell, SQL, and code-execution tools\n- Logging config and telemetry exporters\n- Chat UI rendering code and CSP headers\n- Error handlers that return messages to the model or user\n\n**Needs runtime evidence:** That the fake-secret marker doesn't appear in any output path and the client doesn't fetch marker URLs. Recipe R03.\n\n**N/A when:** never\n\n## R04 · G2 · C5/C6 — Retrieval-corpus integrity\n\n**Lead:** Agent team. **Shares:** Data and privacy, Infrastructure, Security.\n\n### Requirement\n\nThe retrieval corpus is the set of documents the agent searches for context. Its index stores those documents, or smaller passages called chunks, in a search engine or vector database. Limit which identities and sources can add or change content there. For each chunk, keep its source, when it was added, its trust level, and its access permissions. Enforce the requesting tenant's access when searching. Test an unapproved attempt to add content and a search across tenants. Also test quarantining, expiring, and revoking access to seeded test documents. Confirm the affected chunks disappear from search results and caches within a stated time. See the [R04 recipe](CHECKLIST-VERIFICATION.md#r--run-time).\n\n  Why this is hard: Removing a document from the index doesn't remove copies in caches, running contexts, summaries, or memory. Permission changes can take time to spread. And if the model leaves a document out of its answer, that doesn't prove search excluded it.\n\n  If you can't fully do it: Set a limit you can measure, such as \"no revoked chunks in new search results after N minutes.\" Check current access at search time, clear caches, and stop or restart affected runs when needed. If you can't find all affected copies, quarantine the whole collection and rebuild it from approved sources. Record copies that remain and how long they were exposed. Don't claim you removed every derived copy.\n\n  Tradeoffs you can accept: A stated revocation delay that fits the risk, search downtime, or less search coverage while you rebuild. Record the exposure during the delay and any G2 exception. Where access must end right away, block the affected searches and runs until revocation takes effect.\n\n### Verification recipe\n\nCreate searchable test documents for tenants A and B, each with a unique marker. Try to add content with an unapproved identity. It must be denied. Search as tenant A and confirm no tenant B chunks come back. Then quarantine a poisoned test document, expire an old one, and revoke access to a third. After the set propagation time, searches for the exact markers, meaning-based searches, and cache checks must return none of the affected chunks.\n\n**Evidence to keep:** Permissions for adding content, and the rejection; each chunk's source, time, trust, and access records; searches before and after; cache-clearing timestamps.\n\n### Checks for likely_pass\n\nRate likely_pass only if every check below is true, with a file and line for each:\n\n- **R04.1** Only named identities and sources can add or change index content, and the ingestion path checks the writer.\n  - *How to verify:* 1) Find index write paths (`upsert`, `add_documents`, `index`, ingestion jobs). 2) Check each verifies the writer identity or source against a named list. 3) Answer no if any write path, including the agent's own tools or an admin endpoint, accepts writes without that check.\n- **R04.2** Each stored chunk has metadata for source, time added, trust level, and access permissions.\n  - *How to verify:* 1) Find chunk write code and metadata fields. 2) Check source, time added, trust level, and access permissions are stored on every chunk. 3) Answer no if any field is missing, or set only on some ingestion paths.\n- **R04.3** Search applies the requesting tenant's access filter at query time, in the store or retrieval code, not after the model answers.\n  - *How to verify:* 1) Find search calls (`similarity_search`, `query`, `search`, `filter=`, `where=`). 2) Check a tenant or access filter is applied in the store query, from the caller's identity. 3) Answer no if filtering happens after retrieval or in the prompt, the filter is optional, or the tenant comes from a model-supplied argument.\n- **R04.4** Code can quarantine, expire, and revoke chunks, and it also clears or invalidates search caches.\n  - *How to verify:* 1) Find quarantine, expiry, and revoke code (`delete`, `quarantine`, `ttl`, `expires_at`). 2) Check search excludes those chunks and caches are cleared. 3) Answer no if any of the three operations is missing, or caches (query cache, embedding cache) are not invalidated.\n\n### Not covered here\n\nFile these problems under their owning item, not this one, unless this item's own requirement is also broken:\n\n- Memory entries poisoned or shared across tenants → **R05**\n- Agent credentials allowing another tenant's data at the API → **B02**\n- Retrieved chunks not checked for size, type, or hidden instructions → **R01**\n- Embedding or reranker model swapped without review → **C05**\n\n### Heuristics\n\n**Provable from code:** partly\n\n**Look for:**\n- Write access to the index limited to named ingestion identities and sources.\n- Per-chunk metadata for source, time added, trust level, and access permissions (for example in vector-store metadata fields).\n- Tenant access enforced in the search query itself, for example a required metadata filter or a separate namespace or collection per tenant.\n- Code paths to quarantine, expire, and revoke chunks, and to clear search caches.\n\n**Red flags:**\n- One shared vector collection for all tenants with no tenant filter on search.\n- Tenant filter applied after retrieval, or by the model, instead of in the query.\n- The agent itself can write to the index (for example an `add_documents` tool) with no review.\n- Chunks stored with no source or access metadata, so they can't be found or revoked later.\n- Search results cached with no invalidation on revoke.\n\n**Where to look:**\n- Ingestion pipelines and loaders\n- Vector-store client code (Pinecone, Weaviate, pgvector, Chroma, OpenSearch)\n- Retriever setup (for example LangChain retriever `search_kwargs` filters)\n- Cache layers in front of search\n\n**Needs runtime evidence:** That cross-tenant searches return nothing and that revoked, expired, or quarantined chunks leave search results and caches within the stated time. Recipe R04.\n\n**N/A when:** Only when the agent has no retrieval corpus or search index.\n\n## R05 · G3 · C6 — Memory isolation and write validation\n\n**Lead:** Agent team. **Shares:** Data and privacy, Security.\n\n### Requirement\n\nSeparate memory by tenant, agent type, instance, and task as needed. Block access across those lines unless it is clearly approved. Check what gets written to memory. Test that one poisoned run can't quietly plant instructions in another run's memory. Don't move the agent's own output into trusted memory without review, and let unverified entries expire. Write checks must also cover content that arrives through ordinary queries, not just special write paths.\n\n  Why this is hard: A saved preference or summary can act as an instruction in a later session. Write checks can't always tell a useful memory from a harmful one, especially when agents share memory across tasks.\n\n  If you can't fully do it: Start with memory per task or per instance, and turn sharing off by default. Allow only defined memory fields. Require review before an entry moves into shared, long-lived memory. If a store can't keep memory separate, turn off saved writes or sharing for that workflow. Test the reduced setup, and record any feature you turned off to get there.\n\n  Tradeoffs you can accept: Less personalization, repeated work, or shared memory that only holds reviewed entries. Turning off saved writes or sharing can reduce exposure. Any remaining memory-control gap can be deferred only where G3 allows.\n\n### Verification recipe\n\nIn one instance, try to save a harmful instruction, and try to read and write another instance's, type's, or tenant's test memory. Confirm the write checks and scope blocks work. Start a new session and confirm rejected content wasn't saved or reused. Send a poisoning fixture through ordinary queries only. Confirm the agent's own output doesn't move into trusted memory without review, and that unverified entries expire.\n\n**Evidence to keep:** Memory policy, rejected writes and reads, new-session context.\n\n### Checks for likely_pass\n\nRate likely_pass only if every check below is true, with a file and line for each:\n\n- **R05.1** Memory reads and writes are scoped by tenant, agent type, instance, and task as needed, and cross-scope access is denied unless explicitly allowed.\n  - *How to verify:* 1) Find memory read and write code (`memory`, `store`, `namespace`, keys). 2) Check keys include tenant, agent type, instance, or task as needed. 3) Answer no if keys omit tenant, the scope comes from a model argument, or reads fall back to a shared or global namespace.\n- **R05.2** Every memory write, including content arriving through ordinary queries, passes validation before it is stored.\n  - *How to verify:* 1) List every memory write path, including automatic saves from ordinary conversation. 2) Check each passes validation before storing. 3) Answer no if any path writes without validation, or validation errors are caught and the write continues.\n- **R05.3** The agent's own output cannot reach trusted or shared memory without a review step.\n  - *How to verify:* 1) Find where agent output is written to shared or trusted memory. 2) Check a review step gates the write. 3) Answer no if agent output goes straight into shared or trusted memory, or the review flag defaults to approved.\n- **R05.4** Unverified entries have an expiry that is enforced.\n  - *How to verify:* 1) Find how unverified entries are marked and expired (`ttl`, `expires_at`, cleanup jobs). 2) Check reads filter out expired entries. 3) Answer no if no expiry is set, it is set but never enforced on read or by a job, or the default is no expiry.\n\n### Not covered here\n\nFile these problems under their owning item, not this one, unless this item's own requirement is also broken:\n\n- No record of who wrote a memory entry, or no way to remove it → **R06**\n- Retrieval search not filtered by the requesting tenant → **R04**\n- Agent credentials allowing another tenant's data at the API → **B02**\n- Rollback restoring old memory or index state unchecked → **C07**\n\n### Heuristics\n\n**Provable from code:** partly\n\n**Look for:**\n- Memory keys or namespaces that include tenant, agent type, instance, and task as needed (for example LangGraph store namespaces, per-thread checkpointers).\n- Validation on memory writes, including content that arrives through ordinary queries, with only defined fields allowed.\n- A review step before agent output moves into shared or long-lived memory.\n- Expiry (TTL) on unverified entries, and sharing off by default.\n\n**Red flags:**\n- One global memory store or key with no tenant or instance scope.\n- The agent writes free text to persistent memory through a tool with no checks.\n- Summaries of the agent's own output saved as trusted memory with no review.\n- Memory entries that never expire.\n\n**Where to look:**\n- Memory or store modules, LangGraph `BaseStore`/checkpointer setup, Mem0 or similar config\n- Memory write tools\n- Session and thread handling\n\n**Needs runtime evidence:** That a poisoned run can't plant instructions in another run's memory and that cross-scope reads and writes fail. Recipe R05.\n\n**N/A when:** Only when the agent has no memory that lasts between runs.\n\n## R06 · G3 · C6 — Memory provenance and cleanup\n\n**Lead:** Agent team. **Shares:** Operations, Data and privacy.\n\n### Requirement\n\nFor any memory entry, look up who wrote it, when, for which task, from what input, and the context size at the time. Show that you can find, quarantine, and remove bad entries, and control their reuse in later sessions.\n\n  Why this is hard: Summaries and other derived memories can lose their link to the source that tainted them. Deleting one known entry can leave related content in use. Tracking each entry's source can't recover history that was never recorded.\n\n  If you can't fully do it: Keep source and writer links on new entries and on summaries made from them. When the history is incomplete, quarantine or reset the affected memory area and its caches. Restart affected sessions and rebuild from known sources. Record the wider reset and the useful memory it cost. Keep missing provenance as a Gap until you can show it. A cleanup alone does not restore it.\n\n  Tradeoffs you can accept: Losing useful memories, and the cost of resetting a wider area when a precise cleanup isn't possible. Missing provenance stays a G3 gap. Record what was thrown away and how trusted memory will be rebuilt.\n\n### Verification recipe\n\nSeed an approved test entry with a known source. Flag it as tainted, find related entries by source or writer, and quarantine or delete them. Later sessions must not retrieve them. Confirm any derived memory or cache is also handled.\n\n**Evidence to keep:** Provenance query, cleanup log, later search results.\n\n### Checks for likely_pass\n\nRate likely_pass only if every check below is true, with a file and line for each:\n\n- **R06.1** Each memory entry stores its writer, time, task, source input, and context size at write time.\n  - *How to verify:* 1) Find the memory entry schema. 2) Check fields for writer, time, task, source input, and context size. 3) Answer no if any is missing, or set only on some write paths.\n- **R06.2** Derived entries such as summaries keep links to the entries they came from.\n  - *How to verify:* 1) Find summary or derived-entry creation code. 2) Check it stores links to source entry IDs. 3) Answer no if derived entries keep no source links.\n- **R06.3** Code can find entries by source or writer and quarantine or delete them, along with derived entries and caches.\n  - *How to verify:* 1) Find code that queries by source or writer and quarantines or deletes. 2) Check it also handles derived entries and caches. 3) Answer no if lookup by source or writer is impossible, or derived entries and caches are left in place.\n- **R06.4** Quarantined or deleted entries are excluded from retrieval in later sessions.\n  - *How to verify:* 1) Find memory retrieval code. 2) Check it excludes quarantined or deleted entries in new sessions. 3) Answer no if retrieval ignores the quarantine flag, or a cache or index still returns removed entries.\n\n### Not covered here\n\nFile these problems under their owning item, not this one, unless this item's own requirement is also broken:\n\n- Memory not separated by tenant, agent type, instance, or task → **R05**\n- Poisoned retrieval documents not quarantined or expired → **R04**\n- Context size not logged for each action decision → **R07**\n- Prompt a parent gave a sub-agent not recorded → **A06**\n\n### Heuristics\n\n**Provable from code:** partly\n\n**Look for:**\n- Each memory entry stores writer, time, task, source input, and context size at write time.\n- Summaries and derived entries keep links to their sources.\n- Code or runbooks to find entries by source or writer, quarantine or delete them, and clear related caches.\n\n**Red flags:**\n- Memory rows with only content and a timestamp, no writer or source fields.\n- Summaries that replace source entries and drop their links.\n- No delete or quarantine function for memory, or one that skips caches and derived entries.\n\n**Where to look:**\n- Memory schema or data model\n- Summarization and compaction code\n- Admin or cleanup scripts\n\n**Needs runtime evidence:** That a tainted entry and its related entries can be found and removed, and later sessions don't retrieve them. Recipe R06.\n\n**N/A when:** Only when the agent has no memory that lasts between runs.\n\n## R07 · G2 · Obs-T2 — Decision-time context size\n\n**Lead:** Agent team. **Shares:** Operations.\n\n### Requirement\n\nFor each action, log how full the model's context was when it decided, in tokens. Document how you count. Say whether each value was measured, estimated, or unavailable. Cover cached input, compaction (shrinking the context), and limits the provider hides. Per-call billing totals don't automatically equal context size. Use them only if your counting method shows they match. A missing value is a clear gap, never zero. Check that the value survives tool handoffs and can be searched next to the action's identity and result. See the [telemetry conventions](otel-conventions.md).\n\n### Verification recipe\n\nCapture decisions with short and long contexts, and a tool handoff. Compare the context sizes logged with the harness's documented counting method and timing, including units and any estimate limits. Include cases with cached input and compacted context. Where available, record compaction with the OpenTelemetry `gen_ai.conversation.compacted` attribute. If that attribute is missing, it means unknown, not false. Every action must have a searchable record, linked to its trace, of the value and how it was measured. Unknown context must be flagged, never reported as zero.\n\n**Evidence to keep:** Counting method, model-request metadata, trace query.\n\n### Checks for likely_pass\n\nRate likely_pass only if every check below is true, with a file and line for each:\n\n- **R07.1** Each action record includes the model's context size in tokens at decision time.\n  - *How to verify:* 1) Find action or tool-call records (trace spans, audit logs). 2) Check each has context size in tokens at decision time. 3) Answer no if the field is missing or recorded only per run, not per action.\n- **R07.2** Each value is labeled measured, estimated, or unavailable, and a missing value is stored as unknown, never as zero.\n  - *How to verify:* 1) Find where context size is recorded. 2) Check a label of measured, estimated, or unavailable, and how missing values are stored. 3) Answer no if there is no label, or a missing value defaults to `0` (`or 0`, `?? 0`, `.get(..., 0)`).\n- **R07.3** The count comes from a stated method that handles cached input and compaction, not a copied per-call billing total.\n  - *How to verify:* 1) Find how context size is counted. 2) Check a stated method covers cached input and compaction. 3) Answer no if it copies `usage.input_tokens` or a billing total with no stated method that matches it, or ignores cache and compaction.\n- **R07.4** The value is carried across tool handoffs and stored with the action's identity, result, and trace ID.\n  - *How to verify:* 1) Trace context size through tool handoffs and sub-agent calls. 2) Check it is stored with action identity, result, and trace ID. 3) Answer no if it is lost on handoff, or stored separately with no link to the action.\n\n### Not covered here\n\nFile these problems under their owning item, not this one, unless this item's own requirement is also broken:\n\n- Behavior not compared across context-size ranges → **R09**\n- Token or cost limits not enforced outside the model → **B07**\n- Action records missing any of the six identity fields → **A02**\n\n### Heuristics\n\n**Provable from code:** partly\n\n**Look for:**\n- Context size in tokens recorded on each action span or log record, for example OpenTelemetry `gen_ai.usage.input_tokens` plus a BRACE context-size attribute per `otel-conventions.md`.\n- A documented counting method that covers cached input and compaction, and marks each value as measured, estimated, or unavailable.\n- Compaction recorded, for example the `gen_ai.conversation.compacted` attribute.\n- The value carried across tool handoffs and stored next to the action's identity and result.\n\n**Red flags:**\n- No token or context-size field on action records.\n- Missing values written as `0` instead of null or 'unknown'.\n- Per-call billing totals used as context size with no stated method showing they match.\n- Context size logged per run only, not per action.\n\n**Where to look:**\n- OpenTelemetry instrumentation and span attributes\n- Model-call wrappers and usage handling\n- Compaction or summarization code\n\n**Needs runtime evidence:** That logged values match the counting method across short, long, cached, and compacted contexts, and are searchable per action. Recipe R07.\n\n**N/A when:** never\n\n## R08 · G3 · C7 — Security monitoring\n\n**Lead:** Security. **Shares:** Operations.\n\n### Requirement\n\nKeep security rules and baselines separate from answer-quality monitoring. Test encoded payloads, unexpected outbound traffic, writes outside scope, privilege escalation, and repeated denied calls. Check that alerts reach a named responder.\n\n### Verification recipe\n\nSend each configured security event through test traffic. Check that the rule fires and reaches the security responder within the target time. Run a harmless control case too, to tell a working detector from an alert that always fires.\n\n**Evidence to keep:** Rule versions, event fixtures, alert and delivery timestamps.\n\n### Checks for likely_pass\n\nRate likely_pass only if every check below is true, with a file and line for each:\n\n- **R08.1** Security detection rules and baselines are defined separately from answer-quality monitoring.\n  - *How to verify:* 1) Find detection rules and monitoring config (SIEM rules, alert config, eval dashboards). 2) Check security rules are separate from answer-quality metrics. 3) Answer no if only quality monitoring exists. Answer unknown if monitoring lives outside this repo.\n- **R08.2** Rules exist for encoded payloads, unexpected outbound traffic, out-of-scope writes, privilege escalation, and repeated denied calls.\n  - *How to verify:* 1) Read the security detection rules. 2) Check for encoded payloads, unexpected outbound traffic, out-of-scope writes, privilege escalation, and repeated denied calls. 3) Answer no if any is missing, or denied calls are never logged so the rule cannot fire.\n- **R08.3** Alerts from these rules route to a named responder or on-call target in config.\n  - *How to verify:* 1) Find alert routing (PagerDuty, Opsgenie, alertmanager receivers, on-call config). 2) Check the security rules route to a named responder. 3) Answer no if alerts go only to a dashboard or log, or the receiver is empty. Answer unknown if routing lives outside this repo.\n\n### Not covered here\n\nFile these problems under their owning item, not this one, unless this item's own requirement is also broken:\n\n- Chains of individually allowed actions going undetected → **R09**\n- No test of a second-model review against evasive behavior → **R10**\n- No drill for a compromised shared tool or agent type → **E12**\n- Rate and cost limits not enforced outside the model → **B07**\n\n### Heuristics\n\n**Provable from code:** partly\n\n**Look for:**\n- Security detection rules kept apart from answer-quality evals, for example SIEM rules, Falco rules, or alert definitions in the repo.\n- Rules for encoded payloads, unexpected outbound traffic, out-of-scope writes, privilege escalation, and repeated denied calls.\n- Denials and policy decisions emitted as events that rules can match.\n- Alert routing to a named responder (for example PagerDuty or Opsgenie routing config).\n\n**Red flags:**\n- Only quality or eval monitoring (LangSmith, Langfuse, Phoenix scores) with no security rules.\n- Denied tool calls logged at DEBUG or not logged at all.\n- Alerts sent to a shared channel with no named owner.\n\n**Where to look:**\n- Detection-as-code repos, SIEM or Falco rule files\n- Alerting and on-call routing config\n- Policy-decision logging in the harness\n\n**Needs runtime evidence:** That each rule fires on test traffic, stays quiet on a harmless control, and reaches the responder in time. Recipe R08. Rules often live outside the agent repo.\n\n**N/A when:** never\n\n## R09 · G3 · C7/Obs-T2 — Sequence and context-aware detection\n\n**Lead:** Security. **Shares:** Operations, Agent team.\n\n### Requirement\n\nTest suspicious chains of actions that are each allowed on their own. Also test unusual fan-out and resource use. Compare behavior across context-size ranges, so one fleet-wide baseline doesn't hide failures in long contexts. If the agent could be hijacked while using only allowed tools, check each high-impact action against the original task before it runs. Use a checker that doesn't see the untrusted content in between, and measure what it misses.\n\n  Why this is hard: Real research and data theft can look alike step by step. Real attack examples may be rare. A detector tuned only to test cases can miss real behavior, or flood responders with false alarms.\n\n  If you can't fully do it: Start with a few clear signals. Examples: an unusual number of reads followed by an outside send, repeated denials, too many sub-agents, or a used-up budget. Measure how many seeded attacks you catch, how many false alarms you raise, and how fast alerts arrive, for each context-size range. Use hard rate and cost limits, and limit destinations, while you improve detection. Say exactly which sequences you cover and which you miss. Basic rules don't prove general sequence detection.\n\n  Tradeoffs you can accept: A narrower, written detection scope, and a measured false-alarm rate that responders can handle. For lower-stakes deployments, defer broader coverage under G3 while keeping hard limits. Where G3 blocks release, reduce the real exposure or finish the required coverage.\n\n### Verification recipe\n\nDefine a labeled harmless sequence and a labeled abusive one, made of calls that are each allowed. An example is too many reads followed by an attempt to send data out. Replay both at the set small and large context sizes. Record catches and false alarms for each size range, against thresholds set before the run. If you screen actions against the original task, include a hijacked sequence that uses only allowed tools. Confirm the checker doesn't receive the untrusted content.\n\n**Evidence to keep:** Labeled sequences, context ranges, thresholds, results for each range.\n\n### Checks for likely_pass\n\nRate likely_pass only if every check below is true, with a file and line for each:\n\n- **R09.1** Detection rules evaluate sequences of calls across a run (for example many reads followed by an outside send), not only single calls.\n  - *How to verify:* 1) Read detection rules. 2) Check rules evaluate sequences across a run, such as many reads then an outside send. 3) Answer no if every rule looks at a single call or event.\n- **R09.2** Rules cover unusual fan-out and resource use.\n  - *How to verify:* 1) Read detection rules. 2) Check rules for unusual fan-out (sub-agents, parallel calls) and resource use. 3) Answer no if neither is covered.\n- **R09.3** Thresholds or baselines are set per context-size range, not as one fleet-wide value.\n  - *How to verify:* 1) Read rule thresholds and baselines. 2) Check they are set per context-size range. 3) Answer no if one fleet-wide value is used for all contexts.\n- **R09.4** If hijack through allowed tools alone is possible, each high-impact action is checked against the original task before it runs, by a checker that is not given the untrusted content.\n  - *How to verify:* 1) Check whether allowed tools alone could cause harm if the agent is hijacked. 2) If so, find a task-alignment checker before high-impact actions and see what input it receives. 3) Answer no if no checker exists, it is given the untrusted content, or its errors let the action proceed.\n\n### Not covered here\n\nFile these problems under their owning item, not this one, unless this item's own requirement is also broken:\n\n- Encoded payloads or writes outside scope going undetected → **R08**\n- No test against a deliberately evasive, misaligned agent → **R10**\n- Context size not logged for each action decision → **R07**\n\n### Heuristics\n\n**Provable from code:** partly\n\n**Look for:**\n- Detection over sequences of allowed actions, for example many reads followed by an outside send, repeated denials, unusual fan-out, or a used-up budget.\n- Thresholds or baselines split by context-size range.\n- A pre-action checker that compares high-impact actions with the original task and does not receive the untrusted content in between.\n- Labeled harmless and abusive sequences used to measure catches and false alarms.\n\n**Red flags:**\n- Only per-call rules; nothing looks at chains of actions.\n- One fleet-wide baseline with no split by context size.\n- A task-alignment checker given the full conversation, including untrusted tool output.\n\n**Where to look:**\n- Detection rules and analytics jobs\n- Pre-execution hooks or guardrails\n- Detection test fixtures\n\n**Needs runtime evidence:** Catch and false-alarm rates per context-size range against thresholds set before the run. Recipe R09.\n\n**N/A when:** never\n\n## R10 · G3 · C7 — Misaligned-behavior review\n\n**Lead:** Security. **Shares:** Agent team, Governance.\n\n### Requirement\n\nIf deliberate misalignment is in scope, test a second model or pattern review against realistic evasive behavior. Record what it can and can't catch. Include what the reviewer can see: actions, arguments, and any visible reasoning. Reasoning the reviewer can't see is a stated gap. Include red-team runs by an agent that knows it is being watched. This review must not replace rule-based access and action controls that don't depend on a model's judgment. Deeper checks on intent belong in a separate AI-control program at the model level.\n\n  Why this is hard: Actions don't reliably reveal hidden intent. A second model can share the first model's blind spots. Catching labeled evasive examples doesn't prove you will catch deliberate misalignment.\n\n  If you can't fully do it: Make narrow claims about behavior you can see. An example is catching attempts to split a forbidden operation across several calls. Keep a record of misses and false positives. Rely on separate permission limits, action approvals, and stop controls for containment. Where detection falls short, reduce autonomy or remove high-impact powers. Record an unproven detector as a gap. Never label the model's intent as safe.\n\n  Tradeoffs you can accept: Uncertainty about hidden intent, as long as claims stay limited to measured behavior. This checklist does not certify alignment. Less autonomy and more review are also fine. Missing required behavior detection still falls under G3.\n\n### Verification recipe\n\nBuild a labeled review set. Include harmless traces and traces with attempted evasion, such as splitting a forbidden operation across calls. Run the reviewer or detector, and record misses and false alarms against the pass criteria you set. Include traces from a red-team agent that was told it is being watched. Record what the reviewer could see and which reasoning was hidden from it. This tests catching visible behavior. It doesn't reveal the model's hidden intent.\n\n**Evidence to keep:** Review-set version, detector or model version, results and stated limits.\n\n### Checks for likely_pass\n\nChecks for likely_pass:\n\n- (none) Code can't show this item. Don't rate it likely_pass; use needs_evidence, gap, or na_candidate.\n\n### Not covered here\n\nFile these problems under their owning item, not this one, unless this item's own requirement is also broken:\n\n- Destructive actions not blocked by a rule-based gate → **B05**\n- A swapped, drifting, or failing checker model not treated as a failure → **A07**\n- Chains of individually allowed actions going undetected → **R09**\n\n### Heuristics\n\n**Provable from code:** no\n\n**Look for:**\n- A second-model or pattern reviewer of actions and arguments, with a written record of what it can see, including whether reasoning is visible.\n- A labeled review set with evasive traces (for example a forbidden operation split across calls) and red-team traces from an agent told it is watched.\n- Rule-based access and action controls that stay in place alongside the reviewer.\n\n**Red flags:**\n- A reviewer model used in place of rule-based permission or approval controls.\n- Claims that the model's intent is safe, with no measured misses or false positives.\n- Reviewer sees only the final answer, not actions and arguments.\n\n**Where to look:**\n- Monitor or reviewer agent code and prompts\n- Red-team and evaluation results\n- Threat model stating whether deliberate misalignment is in scope\n\n**Needs runtime evidence:** Measured misses and false alarms on a labeled evasive set, plus red-team runs. Code can only show a reviewer exists. Recipe R10.\n\n**N/A when:** Only when deliberate misalignment is recorded as out of scope for this deployment, with a reason and approval.\n\n## R11 · G1 · C8 — Tested kill switch\n\n**Lead:** Operations. **Shares:** Infrastructure, Agent team.\n\n### Requirement\n\nName who may stop the agent and how. The stop path must not depend on the agent's cooperation. The agent's credentials must not be able to change it. Set a maximum stop time that fits the potential harm. Run a dated drill on the current deployment. It must stop new actions, running work, and open tool streams, subscriptions, and tasks. Include an attempt by the agent to disable or delay the stop. Cover sub-agents and their children in the ecosystem drill (E09).\n\n### Verification recipe\n\nStart a controlled run and trigger the operator's stop control. Record the time of the last allowed action and the final stop. Repeat while a tool call is in progress. Have the agent use its own credentials and tools to try to disable, change, or delay the stop path. It must fail. All work must stop within the time limit. Use E09 for sub-agents and their children.\n\n**Evidence to keep:** Drill trace, operator action, measured stop time.\n\n### Checks for likely_pass\n\nRate likely_pass only if every check below is true, with a file and line for each:\n\n- **R11.1** A stop control halts new model calls and new tool calls when set.\n  - *How to verify:* 1) Find the stop control (kill flag, `stop`, `halt`, feature flag, revoked token). 2) Check the loop checks it before every model call and tool call. 3) Answer no if it is checked only at run start, only between turns, a second loop or worker skips it, or a check error is treated as not stopped.\n- **R11.2** The agent's own credentials and tools cannot set, clear, delete, or delay the stop control.\n  - *How to verify:* 1) Find where the stop flag is stored and who can write it. 2) Compare with the agent's credentials and tools. 3) Answer no if the agent's role can write, delete, or expire the flag, a tool can reach its store, or the agent can delay it (for example by catching the stop signal).\n- **R11.3** Tool calls already running are cancelled or bounded by a timeout when the stop is set.\n  - *How to verify:* 1) Find the stop handler. 2) Check running tool calls are cancelled or bounded by a timeout. 3) Answer no if running calls finish with no timeout, or cancellation errors are swallowed and the call continues.\n- **R11.4** The stop also closes open tool streams, subscriptions, and background tasks.\n  - *How to verify:* 1) Find streams, subscriptions, and background tasks (`asyncio.create_task`, goroutines, websockets, SSE). 2) Check the stop handler closes them. 3) Answer no if any is left open, detached, or untracked.\n- **R11.5** Only named operator identities can set the stop control.\n  - *How to verify:* 1) Find who can set the stop (auth on the endpoint, IAM on the flag store). 2) Answer no if any authenticated user or service can set it with no named operator list. Answer unknown if access lives outside this repo.\n\n### Not covered here\n\nFile these problems under their owning item, not this one, unless this item's own requirement is also broken:\n\n- Stop signal not reaching sub-agents, children, or remote workers → **E09**\n- In-flight writes, queued actions, or unattended restarts after the stop → **R12**\n- No way to cut off the agent's access without cutting off the user → **B04**\n\n### Heuristics\n\n**Provable from code:** partly\n\n**Look for:**\n- A stop path outside the agent's control, for example an operator-only flag in a separate service, scaling the deployment to zero, revoking the agent identity, or a gateway kill rule.\n- The agent's credentials can't write to the stop flag or its config (check IAM and RBAC).\n- The harness checks the stop signal before each action and cancels running tool calls, streams, subscriptions, and background tasks.\n- A written list of who may stop the agent and a maximum stop time.\n\n**Red flags:**\n- Stop works by sending the agent a message or prompt asking it to halt.\n- Stop flag stored where the agent can write, for example the same database, config map, or memory it uses.\n- Stop checked only between turns, with no cancel for in-flight tool calls or streams.\n- Sub-agents or background jobs not reached by the stop signal.\n\n**Where to look:**\n- Runner loop and cancellation handling\n- Ops scripts and runbooks for stopping the agent\n- IAM/RBAC for the stop control\n\n**Needs runtime evidence:** A dated drill on the current deployment with measured stop time, including an in-progress tool call and an agent attempt to block the stop. Recipe R11. Sub-agents are covered by E09.\n\n**N/A when:** never\n\n## R12 · G1 · C8 — Safe state after stopping\n\n**Lead:** Agent team. **Shares:** Operations, Business owner.\n\n### Requirement\n\nDecide what happens to writes in progress, queued actions, and outside side effects. Where you can't cancel something, show how you check and fix the result (reconcile) or undo it another way (compensate). No restart may happen unattended, and no orphaned work may be left able to act.\n\n  Why this is hard: An outside action may already be done when the stop arrives. Emails can't reliably be unsent. Payments, writes, and queued jobs each cancel and reconcile differently.\n\n  If you can't fully do it: For each operation, record whether it can be blocked, canceled, or only reconciled afterward. Stop new work from being sent, turn off retries, check the destination's state, and send unclear results to a named operator. For operations with no workable recovery path, remove autonomous execution or use an approved draft-then-commit flow. Measure stopping new work separately from resolving work already done. Stopping the process alone does not pass this check.\n\n  Tradeoffs you can accept: Slower manual reconciliation and fewer autonomous actions. Some completed effects may need reconciling rather than reversing, following the written safe-state procedure. Untracked effects, or work that keeps its authority past the stop time limit, still block release.\n\n### Verification recipe\n\nStop runs before a write, during a transaction, and after a finished outside test action. Check the queues and the destination's state. Each case must end canceled, completed, or reconciled. Or it must be on a tracked hold for reconciliation, with an owner, no dependent actions, and no blind retries. No unexplained orphaned work or restart is acceptable.\n\n**Evidence to keep:** Outcome of each scenario, queue snapshots, undo and reconciliation records.\n\n### Checks for likely_pass\n\nRate likely_pass only if every check below is true, with a file and line for each:\n\n- **R12.1** For each operation type, code or config states whether it can be cancelled, completed, or only reconciled after a stop.\n  - *How to verify:* 1) Search code and config for a per-operation stop policy (cancel, complete, reconcile). 2) Answer no if any operation type the agent can run has no stated stop behavior.\n- **R12.2** On stop, queued actions are cancelled or held, and automatic retries are turned off.\n  - *How to verify:* 1) Find queue and retry code (`retry`, `backoff`, queue workers). 2) Check stop cancels or holds queued items and turns retries off. 3) Answer no if retries continue after stop or queued jobs still run.\n- **R12.3** Unclear or in-progress outside actions go on a tracked hold with an owner, and dependent actions are blocked until reconciled.\n  - *How to verify:* 1) Find handling of unclear or in-progress outside actions at stop. 2) Check they go on a tracked hold with an owner and block dependent actions. 3) Answer no if they are dropped, retried, or dependents proceed.\n- **R12.4** A stopped run does not restart on its own; restart needs an operator action.\n  - *How to verify:* 1) Find restart logic (supervisors, `restartPolicy`, cron, auto-resume). 2) Answer no if a stopped run restarts automatically (`restartPolicy: Always`, auto-resume from checkpoint) without an operator action.\n- **R12.5** Workers and queued jobs lose their credentials or authority at stop, so no orphaned work can act.\n  - *How to verify:* 1) Find workers and queued jobs and the credentials they hold. 2) Check stop revokes or expires them. 3) Answer no if workers keep long-lived credentials after stop, or jobs carry their own tokens that stay valid.\n\n### Not covered here\n\nFile these problems under their owning item, not this one, unless this item's own requirement is also broken:\n\n- Stop path depends on the agent or has no tested stop time → **R11**\n- Recovery replay repeating irreversible actions or using tampered checkpoints → **R14**\n- Rollback restoring revoked permissions → **C07**\n\n### Heuristics\n\n**Provable from code:** partly\n\n**Look for:**\n- A per-operation record of whether it can be blocked, canceled, or only reconciled afterward.\n- On stop: queues drained or held, retries turned off, and in-flight transactions rolled back.\n- Reconcile or compensate code for outside side effects, and unclear results sent to a named operator.\n- Restart requires an operator; no auto-restart policy resumes agent work.\n\n**Red flags:**\n- Kubernetes `restartPolicy: Always` or a supervisor that resumes the agent's queue after a stop with no human step.\n- Queued jobs or scheduled tasks that keep their credentials and run after the stop.\n- Retries with backoff on outside actions that keep going during or after a stop.\n- Stop handled only by killing the process.\n\n**Where to look:**\n- Job queues, workers, and schedulers\n- Shutdown and signal handlers\n- Retry config and compensation code\n\n**Needs runtime evidence:** Outcomes of stops before a write, mid-transaction, and after an outside action, with queue and destination snapshots. Recipe R12.\n\n**N/A when:** never\n\n## R13 · G1 · C9 — Full execution audit\n\n**Lead:** Agent team. **Shares:** Infrastructure, Operations.\n\n### Requirement\n\nRecord each run as an execution graph: a linked record of every step. It must include proposed actions, tool calls and key arguments, and authorization decisions with the policy version used. It must also include denials, approvals with their IDs, results, errors, and timestamps. Rebuild a typical run from this record, including failed actions. Don't rely on the run's final answer.\n\n  Why this is hard: Agent logs usually show what the agent tried. The destination system knows whether it actually happened. A timeout can mean the call failed, or it worked and the reply was lost. So a local trace can look complete and still have an unknown result.\n\n  If you can't fully do it: Give each operation an ID before you send it. Keep receipts or status checks from the destination where you can. Match attempts against the destination's records, and mark unresolved results clearly. Pause dependent actions and retries until each one is resolved. If an integration can't give you enough evidence to trace and recover, limit or remove that path. Sampling and the agent's own reports can't replace the required action record.\n\n  Tradeoffs you can accept: Pausing dependent work while an unclear result gets resolved. Keep the attempt, the uncertainty, and the final answer in the graph. Less automation is a fair cost. Missing or made-up action evidence is not a G1 pass.\n\n### Verification recipe\n\nRun a known sequence with an allowed call, a denial, an approval, a tool error, and a retry. Confirm each decision record names the policy version, and each approved action names its approval ID. Rebuild the graph using only stored audit records. Compare it with the test runner's and the destination's logs. Every expected action must be accounted for. Unresolved results at the destination must be marked clearly, with dependent work paused until they are reconciled.\n\n**Evidence to keep:** Expected event list, rebuilt graph, query for missing events.\n\n### Checks for likely_pass\n\nRate likely_pass only if every check below is true, with a file and line for each:\n\n- **R13.1** Each step writes a record linked to the run: proposed action, tool call with key arguments, result, error, and timestamp.\n  - *How to verify:* 1) Find step logging (trace spans, audit log writes). 2) Check each record has run ID, proposed action, tool call with key arguments, result, error, and timestamp. 3) Answer no if any field is missing, or logging happens only on the success branch.\n- **R13.2** Authorization decisions are recorded with the policy version, including denials, not only allowed calls.\n  - *How to verify:* 1) Find authorization decision code. 2) Check both allow and deny are logged with the policy version. 3) Answer no if denials are not logged, or no policy version is recorded.\n- **R13.3** Approved actions record their approval ID.\n  - *How to verify:* 1) Find where approved actions are logged. 2) Answer no if the approval ID is not recorded with the action.\n- **R13.4** Tool errors, timeouts, and retries are recorded, and each operation gets an ID before it is sent, so unclear results can be matched to the destination's records.\n  - *How to verify:* 1) Find error, timeout, and retry handling. 2) Check each is logged and each operation gets an ID before sending. 3) Answer no if `except`/`catch` blocks skip logging, retries are not logged, or the ID is created after the response.\n- **R13.5** Records are written by the harness or gateway, not by the model, and do not depend on the run's final answer.\n  - *How to verify:* 1) Find who writes records. 2) Answer no if the model writes them via a tool, records are built from the final answer, or logging runs only if the run completes.\n\n### Not covered here\n\nFile these problems under their owning item, not this one, unless this item's own requirement is also broken:\n\n- Action records missing any of the six identity fields → **A02**\n- Agent able to erase or rewrite its own audit history → **E10**\n- Audit retention, access, redaction, or export rules missing → **E11**\n- Run can't be rebuilt across gateway, tool services, and delegated workers → **E10**\n- Prompt a parent gave a sub-agent not recorded → **A06**\n\n### Heuristics\n\n**Provable from code:** partly\n\n**Look for:**\n- A linked trace per run (for example OpenTelemetry spans with parent links) covering proposed actions, tool calls with key arguments, authorization decisions with policy version, denials, approvals with IDs, results, errors, and timestamps.\n- An operation ID assigned before each outside call, and destination receipts or status checks stored.\n- Unresolved results marked clearly, with dependent actions paused.\n- Audit records written to a store the agent can't change.\n\n**Red flags:**\n- Only the final answer or chat transcript is stored.\n- Denials and approval decisions not logged, or logged without the policy version.\n- Trace sampling (for example `TraceIdRatioBased` below 1.0) applied to the action audit.\n- Timeouts logged as failures with no marker that the result is unknown.\n\n**Where to look:**\n- Tracing and logging setup, span attributes\n- Policy and approval decision code\n- Audit storage config and retention\n\n**Needs runtime evidence:** That a known run with an allowed call, denial, approval, error, and retry can be rebuilt from stored records alone and matches destination logs. Recipe R13.\n\n**N/A when:** never\n\n## R14 · G2 · C9 — Recovery integrity\n\n**Lead:** Agent team. **Shares:** Infrastructure, Operations.\n\n### Requirement\n\nIf logs or checkpoints drive recovery, make them tamper-evident. Before using them, verify them against a separately protected trust anchor or signing key. Test that verification rejects changed checkpoints. It must also catch truncation, rollback to an older valid checkpoint, and replacement of a whole hash chain. For automatic recovery, confirm the destination supports idempotency: sending the same request twice has the effect of sending it once. Replay only within the destination's documented scope and time window. Confirm that actions you can't undo don't happen twice. Where support is missing or the result is unclear, check that replay pauses for reconciliation instead of sending again.\n\n  Why this is hard: Not every outside API supports idempotency keys or a reliable status check. A crash between a successful remote action and the local checkpoint can make replay repeat that action. A local table of past requests doesn't close that gap.\n\n  If you can't fully do it: Use idempotency enforced by the destination where it exists. Write a durable operation record before sending. If the result is unclear, pause replay and reconcile using the destination's evidence or an operator. Don't retry blindly. Turn off automatic replay for side effects that don't support it, and document that limit. Reject checkpoints you can't verify, and recover from a trusted state. Manual recovery doesn't prove automatic replay is safe.\n\n  Tradeoffs you can accept: Manual recovery, slower completion, and no automatic replay for unsupported integrations. Never call blind retries of unclear, permanent actions \"safe replay.\" Remaining recovery-control gaps need allowed G2 acceptance.\n\n### Verification recipe\n\nChange a copy of a checkpoint or audit record. Recovery must reject it. Also cut off, roll back, and replace the chain or checkpoint. Verification must use a separately protected trust anchor. For integrations that support safe automatic replay, replay a good checkpoint twice. The action must happen once, with the duplicate recorded as blocked. For integrations without that support, inject an unclear result. Check that replay pauses for reconciliation with the destination and sends no duplicate.\n\n**Evidence to keep:** Integrity check result, idempotency results enforced by the destination, action counts, manual-recovery hold and outcome.\n\n### Checks for likely_pass\n\nRate likely_pass only if every check below is true, with a file and line for each:\n\n- **R14.1** Checkpoints and audit records used for recovery are hash-chained or signed.\n  - *How to verify:* 1) Find checkpoint and audit storage used for recovery. 2) Check for hash chaining or signatures. 3) Answer no if records are plain rows or files with no chain or signature.\n- **R14.2** Recovery verifies them against a key or trust anchor stored separately from the records, and refuses any that fail.\n  - *How to verify:* 1) Find recovery code that loads checkpoints. 2) Check it verifies against a key stored separately and refuses failures. 3) Answer no if the key sits in the same store, verification is skipped, or failure is logged and recovery continues.\n- **R14.3** Verification catches truncation, rollback to an older valid checkpoint, and replacement of the whole chain, for example with a signed head or sequence number held outside the store.\n  - *How to verify:* 1) Check verification uses a signed head or sequence number held outside the store. 2) Answer no if truncation, rollback to an older valid checkpoint, or replacing the whole chain would still pass.\n- **R14.4** Automatic replay sends idempotency keys that the destination enforces, and only for integrations that support them.\n  - *How to verify:* 1) Find replay code. 2) Check it sends idempotency keys (`Idempotency-Key`, `client_token`) only to integrations that enforce them. 3) Answer no if replay sends without keys, uses a local dedupe table as the only guard, or replays to integrations without support.\n- **R14.5** When idempotency is missing or the result is unclear, replay pauses for reconciliation instead of resending.\n  - *How to verify:* 1) Find replay handling for unclear results or missing idempotency. 2) Answer no if replay resends instead of pausing for reconciliation, or retry is the default on timeout.\n\n### Not covered here\n\nFile these problems under their owning item, not this one, unless this item's own requirement is also broken:\n\n- What happens to in-flight writes and queued actions at stop → **R12**\n- Rollback to an approved earlier release not tested → **C07**\n- Agent able to delete or rewrite its own audit history → **E10**\n\n### Heuristics\n\n**Provable from code:** partly\n\n**Look for:**\n- Checkpoints and recovery logs signed or hash-chained, and verified against a separately protected key or trust anchor before use.\n- Checks that catch truncation, rollback to an older checkpoint, and replacement of a whole chain (for example a signed head or sequence number stored elsewhere).\n- Idempotency keys sent to destinations that support them, for example `Idempotency-Key` headers.\n- A durable operation record written before sending, and replay that pauses for reconciliation when a result is unclear.\n\n**Red flags:**\n- LangGraph or similar checkpoints loaded from a writable store with no integrity check.\n- Hash chain verified only against itself, with no outside anchor.\n- Automatic replay or retry of payments, emails, or other permanent actions with no idempotency key.\n- A local 'already sent' table used as the only duplicate guard.\n\n**Where to look:**\n- Checkpointer and resume code\n- Retry and replay logic in tool clients\n- Audit log storage and signing setup\n\n**Needs runtime evidence:** That changed, truncated, rolled-back, and replaced checkpoints are rejected, and replays happen once or pause. Recipe R14.\n\n**N/A when:** Only when no logs or checkpoints drive recovery and there is no automatic replay.\n","numLines":924,"startLine":1,"totalLines":924}}}
