{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","id":"msg_011Cfdi3S1kTZ2h1oHiyUWkZ","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01AgV9L2mxqGEjoXAGsyFK2W","name":"Read","input":{"file_path":"/private/var/folders/6v/yk9bzvrn5zx0kxll7d4szzb80000gn/T/brace-repeat-80qi2mwl/skill/references/agent.md"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":4084,"cache_read_input_tokens":193954,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":4084},"output_tokens":7,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"29563294-217c-407e-8b87-42521726a071","uuid":"79b76fa3-10bc-46b5-8ac2-1626930b4d2a","timestamp":"2026-10-02T16:15:03.635Z","request_id":"req_011Cfdi3RguFKyEX6xkBtSue","wire_tool_inputs":{"toolu_01AgV9L2mxqGEjoXAGsyFK2W":{"file_path":"/private/var/folders/6v/yk9bzvrn5zx0kxll7d4szzb80000gn/T/brace-repeat-80qi2mwl/skill/references/agent.md"}}}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01AgV9L2mxqGEjoXAGsyFK2W","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t<!-- Generated by skills/gen_references.py from CHECKLIST.md, CHECKLIST-VERIFICATION.md, ROLES.md, and heuristics. Do not edit by hand. -->\n2\t\n3\t# A — Agent\n4\t\n5\tUse this file to rate each item from the code and configuration. The requirement is the checklist text. The heuristics say what to look for. Ratings: likely_pass, gap, needs_evidence, or na_candidate. Never rate pass.\n6\t\n7\t## A01 · G1 · C2/Obs-T1 — Distinct agent identity\n8\t\n9\t**Lead:** Infrastructure. **Shares:** Agent team.\n10\t\n11\t### Requirement\n12\t\n13\tGive the agent its own non-human identity. Keep it separate from the user who launched it and from unrelated agents. Show that permissions and revocation belong to that identity, even when a user starts the run.\n14\t\n15\t### Verification recipe\n16\t\n17\tLaunch the agent from a user account. Look at the identity (principal) the agent was issued, and compare the user's permissions with the agent's. Revoke only the agent and try again. Its identity and access must be separate from the user's and from unrelated agents.\n18\t\n19\t**Evidence to keep:** Identity bindings, permission comparison, revocation results.\n20\t\n21\t### Checks for likely_pass\n22\t\n23\tRate likely_pass only if every check below is true, with a file and line for each:\n24\t\n25\t- **A01.1** The agent runs under its own workload principal (service account, IAM role, SPIFFE ID, or agent ID) that no other service or unrelated agent uses.\n26\t  - *How to verify:* 1) Find the agent's identity: pod `serviceAccountName`, IAM role, SPIFFE ID, or agent ID in IaC or deploy config. 2) Search other deployments, services, and agents in the repo for the same name or role. 3) Answer no if it is shared, `default`, the main backend's identity, or only a string label with no credential. Answer unknown if identity is assigned outside this repo.\n27\t- **A01.2** Tool and API clients get credentials from the agent's own principal, not from the launching user's forwarded token or session cookie.\n28\t  - *How to verify:* 1) Find every place tool and API clients get a token or key. 2) Trace where each comes from: workload identity or token exchange, versus the incoming request's `Authorization` header or session cookie. 3) Check every client, not just the main one. 4) Answer no if any client forwards the user's token or cookie as-is, or uses a shared static key.\n29\t- **A01.3** When a user starts a run, the user is carried as a delegated subject (for example a token exchange with separate act and sub), while the agent keeps its own principal.\n30\t  - *How to verify:* 1) Find the code that starts a run for a user. 2) Look for token exchange (RFC 8693, `subject_token`, `actor_token`, `act`/`sub` claims) or a separate delegated-subject field. 3) Check the agent's own principal is still the actor. 4) Answer no if the user's token is used directly or the user replaces the agent identity. Answer unknown if the exchange happens in an outside identity service.\n31\t- **A01.4** Permissions are bound to the agent principal in IaC or RBAC, so revoking that principal alone removes the agent's access without disabling the user.\n32\t  - *How to verify:* 1) Find IAM policies, RoleBindings, or grants in Terraform, Helm, or Kubernetes YAML. 2) Check they bind to the agent principal, not to a user, group, or shared role. 3) Check that removing that binding alone would cut the agent's access. 4) Answer no if access depends on the user's grants or a shared credential. Answer unknown if permissions are not defined in this repo.\n33\t\n34\t### Not covered here\n35\t\n36\tFile these problems under their owning item, not this one, unless this item's own requirement is also broken:\n37\t\n38\t- Action records missing any of the six identity fields → **A02**\n39\t- Instance or tenant labels taken from what the model supplies → **A04**\n40\t- A credential scoped to more operations, resources, or tenants than needed → **B02**\n41\t- Revocation delay from cached or self-contained tokens not measured → **B04**\n42\t\n43\t### Heuristics\n44\t\n45\t**Provable from code:** partly\n46\t\n47\t**Look for:**\n48\t- The agent runs as its own workload identity: a dedicated Kubernetes ServiceAccount, cloud IAM role (AWS IAM role, GCP service account, Azure managed identity), SPIFFE ID, or Entra Agent ID, not shared with other services.\n49\t- Tool and API clients get credentials from that identity (workload identity federation, IRSA, token exchange per RFC 8693) instead of from the user session.\n50\t- When a user starts a run, the user is passed as a delegated subject (for example an `act`/`sub` pair in an exchanged token), while the agent keeps its own principal.\n51\t- Permissions are attached to the agent principal in IaC (Terraform IAM policies, RBAC RoleBindings) and are narrower than the launching user's.\n52\t- A documented way to revoke or disable the agent principal alone (delete role binding, disable service principal, rotate its key).\n53\t\n54\t**Red flags:**\n55\t- Agent calls downstream APIs with the launching user's OAuth access token or session cookie, forwarded as-is.\n56\t- One service account, API key, or IAM role is shared by several unrelated agents or by the agent and the main app backend.\n57\t- Long-lived static API keys for the agent in env files or code, with no per-agent principal behind them.\n58\t- Agent identity is only a string label in config or a prompt (for example `agent_name: support-bot`) with no credential bound to it.\n59\t- Revoking access would require disabling the user or a shared credential, because there is no agent-only principal.\n60\t\n61\t**Where to look:**\n62\t- Kubernetes ServiceAccount, RoleBinding, and pod spec `serviceAccountName`\n63\t- Terraform/CloudFormation/Bicep IAM roles, policies, and identity federation\n64\t- auth and credential setup in the harness and tool clients (token exchange, OAuth client config)\n65\t- .env files, secret manager references, and Helm values\n66\t\n67\t**Needs runtime evidence:** Code can show how identity is wired, not that the issued principal and its live permissions differ from the user's, or that revoking only the agent works. Needs the A01 recipe: launch from a user account, compare permissions, revoke the agent alone, and retry.\n68\t\n69\t**N/A when:** never\n70\t\n71\t## A02 · G1 · Obs-T1 — All six identity fields\n72\t\n73\t**Lead:** Agent team. **Shares:** Infrastructure, Business owner, Governance.\n74\t\n75\t### Requirement\n76\t\n77\tCheck that every action records the accountable party, operational owner, tenant, agent-type-id, agent-instance-id, and trace context. Look at successful, denied, failed, and background actions, not just the main request path.\n78\t\n79\t### Verification recipe\n80\t\n81\tQuery every record from a controlled run with successful, denied, failed, async, and background actions. Count missing or empty values for each of the six fields. The count must be zero. Confirm the values match the known run and tenant. Confirm tenant and owner values come from the trusted layer, not from baggage or tracestate headers, which have no integrity protection.\n82\t\n83\t**Evidence to keep:** Field-completeness query, counts, sample records.\n84\t\n85\t### Checks for likely_pass\n86\t\n87\tRate likely_pass only if every check below is true, with a file and line for each:\n88\t\n89\t- **A02.1** The audit record schema has all six fields: accountable party, operational owner, tenant, agent-type-id, agent-instance-id, and trace context.\n90\t  - *How to verify:* 1) Find the audit record schema or the function that writes audit entries. 2) Check for each of the six: accountable party, operational owner, tenant, `agent.type.id`, `agent.instance.id`, trace context (`traceparent` or trace and span IDs). 3) Answer no if any one is missing from the schema or never set.\n91\t- **A02.2** All six fields are written on denied, failed, async, and background actions too, not only on the successful main request path.\n92\t  - *How to verify:* 1) List every path that writes an audit record, or should: success, policy deny, `except`/`catch` blocks, retries, queue workers, cron jobs, webhooks, other endpoints such as approvals, and refusals before the run starts (quota, rate limit, auth) that return an error without logging. 2) For each, check all six fields are set, ideally by one shared wrapper. 3) Answer no if any deny, error, async, or background path writes records without them, or writes no record at all.\n93\t- **A02.3** Trace context (trace and span IDs) is stored in the audit record itself, not only in separate tracing spans.\n94\t  - *How to verify:* 1) Open the audit record writer. 2) Check it stores trace and span IDs (or `traceparent`) as fields in the record itself. 3) Answer no if trace context exists only in OpenTelemetry spans, or the record's trace field is never filled.\n95\t- **A02.4** Tenant and owner values come from the trusted layer, not from baggage, tracestate, or other unprotected headers.\n96\t  - *How to verify:* 1) Find where tenant and owner values for audit records are read. 2) Trace each back to its source: token claims, verified session, IAM tags, or SPIFFE path, versus OTel baggage, `tracestate`, headers like `X-Tenant-Id`, request body, or tool arguments. 3) Answer no if any path takes them from an unprotected source.\n97\t\n98\t### Not covered here\n99\t\n100\tFile these problems under their owning item, not this one, unless this item's own requirement is also broken:\n101\t\n102\t- Identity labels taken from what the model supplies → **A04**\n103\t- Agent-type-id not computed as a hash of its identity inputs → **A03**\n104\t- Agent able to erase or rewrite its own audit history → **E10**\n105\t- Sub-agent record missing its parent link or parent prompt → **A06**\n106\t\n107\t### Heuristics\n108\t\n109\t**Provable from code:** partly\n110\t\n111\t**Look for:**\n112\t- Telemetry or audit code sets all six fields on every action: accountable party, operational owner, tenant, `agent.type.id`, `agent.instance.id`, and W3C trace context (`traceparent`).\n113\t- Fields are set in one shared place (span processor, logging middleware, audit wrapper around tool dispatch) so every tool call inherits them.\n114\t- Denied, failed, retried, async, and background paths (queue workers, cron jobs, webhooks) also emit the fields, not just the main request handler.\n115\t- Tenant and owner values come from verified identity (token claims, IAM tags, SPIFFE path) in the trusted layer.\n116\t- A test or query that counts missing or empty identity fields per record.\n117\t\n118\t**Red flags:**\n119\t- Identity attributes are added only on the success path; `except`/`catch` blocks and policy-denial branches log without them.\n120\t- Background jobs or queue consumers start new traces with no tenant, instance, or type ID.\n121\t- Tenant or owner is read from OTel baggage or `tracestate`, which have no integrity protection.\n122\t- `agent.type.id` or `agent.instance.id` never appears anywhere in the code or config.\n123\t- `gen_ai.agent.id` is overwritten with a per-run ID and used as the only agent identifier.\n124\t\n125\t**Where to look:**\n126\t- OpenTelemetry setup: span processors, resource attributes, instrumentation config\n127\t- tool dispatch and policy-check code, including error and deny branches\n128\t- queue workers, schedulers, webhook handlers\n129\t- audit log writer and log schema definitions\n130\t\n131\t**Needs runtime evidence:** Code can't prove every emitted record is complete or that values match the real run and tenant. Needs the A02 recipe: query all records from a controlled run with success, deny, failure, async, and background actions and show zero missing values.\n132\t\n133\t**N/A when:** never\n134\t\n135\t## A03 · G1 · Obs-T1 — Content-derived type identity\n136\t\n137\t**Lead:** Agent team. **Shares:** Model team.\n138\t\n139\t### Requirement\n140\t\n141\tCompute the agent-type-id as a hash of the container digest, harness, system prompt, model ID and version, and configuration. The model part includes checkpoint and fine-tune or adapter references where they apply. Keep the list of inputs (the manifest) and the hashing steps. Check that changing any input changes the ID, and that the same manifest gives the same ID.\n142\t\n143\t  Why this is hard: A hash only covers the inputs you can see. It can't prove that a hosted model's hidden weights or training stayed the same behind the same name.\n144\t\n145\t  If you can't fully do it: Hash the exact local artifacts and the model, checkpoint, and adapter references you have, using a manifest anyone can rebuild. Record the model name you asked for separately from the version the provider says it served. Mark hidden information clearly. State that the ID fingerprints this recorded setup. Handle changes behind remote names under C05. Never use the agent app's release number as the model's identity. Never present a local hash as proof of the provider's hidden state.\n146\t\n147\t  Tradeoffs you can accept: A fingerprint that covers only the recorded setup, with the provider's hidden details clearly outside the claim. Track changes behind remote names under C05. Missing hashes or model references for artifacts you control still fail this G1 item.\n148\t\n149\t### Verification recipe\n150\t\n151\tHash the same frozen manifest twice. The IDs must match. Change the container, harness, prompt, model/checkpoint/adapter reference, and configuration one at a time. Each change must produce a different ID. Confirm the exact input format for hashing is documented and that no secret values are exposed.\n152\t\n153\t**Evidence to keep:** Hashing steps and version, input manifests, expected and actual IDs.\n154\t\n155\t### Checks for likely_pass\n156\t\n157\tRate likely_pass only if every check below is true, with a file and line for each:\n158\t\n159\t- **A03.1** The agent-type-id is a hash over a manifest that includes the container digest, harness, system prompt, model ID and version, and configuration.\n160\t  - *How to verify:* 1) Find the code or CI step that computes `agent.type.id` (search `sha256`, `hashlib`, `createHash`, `type_id`). 2) List its inputs. 3) Check each is there: container digest, harness, system prompt, model ID and version, configuration. 4) Answer no if the ID is a label, version string, or git tag, or any input is missing.\n161\t- **A03.2** The hash covers every file that defines the prompt, tools, and configuration, with no file or directory skipped or excluded.\n162\t  - *How to verify:* 1) Open the function that lists files to hash. 2) Write down every filter it applies: hidden files, names starting with '_' or '__', extensions, ignore lists, glob patterns. 3) For each filter, check whether a file that defines tools, prompts, or config would be skipped (for example `__init__.py`, `index.ts`, `.env`, `config/*.local.*`). 4) Answer no if any such file is skipped.\n163\t- **A03.3** The model input is a pinned version or snapshot (plus checkpoint and adapter references where used), not an alias and not the app's release number.\n164\t  - *How to verify:* 1) Find the model ID that goes into the manifest or hash. 2) Follow env vars and config to the final value, including defaults such as `getenv('MODEL', ...)`. 3) Answer no if it is an alias (`-latest`, no date or version), falls back to one when unset, or is the app release number. Also no if adapters or checkpoints are used but left out.\n165\t- **A03.4** The manifest is put in a fixed, documented order before hashing, so the same manifest always gives the same ID. *(A document can show this.)*\n166\t  - *How to verify:* 1) Open the serialization step before hashing. 2) Check for a fixed order: sorted keys (`sort_keys=True`), sorted file lists, fixed encoding and line endings. 3) Check file lists are not taken straight from directory listing or map iteration order. 4) Answer no if any unsorted dict, map, or filesystem order reaches the hash.\n167\t- **A03.5** Secret values are not placed in the hashed manifest in a form that exposes them.\n168\t  - *How to verify:* 1) List every value fed into the manifest or hash. 2) Check whether any are secrets: API keys, tokens, passwords, private keys, `.env` contents. 3) Answer no if a secret value is written in plain text to the stored manifest, or the whole environment is dumped into it.\n169\t\n170\t### Not covered here\n171\t\n172\tFile these problems under their owning item, not this one, unless this item's own requirement is also broken:\n173\t\n174\t- Changed running config still reporting the old approved ID → **C03**\n175\t- Hosted model changing behind a fixed name → **C05**\n176\t- Release manifest missing training job, dataset, or model provider records → **C01**\n177\t- Action records missing any of the six identity fields → **A02**\n178\t\n179\t### Heuristics\n180\t\n181\t**Provable from code:** partly\n182\t\n183\t**Look for:**\n184\t- A manifest listing the hash inputs: container digest, harness version or files, system prompt, model ID and version, checkpoint/adapter references, and configuration.\n185\t- Code or a CI step that computes `agent.type.id` as a hash (for example `sha256:`) over that manifest with documented, canonical serialization (sorted keys, fixed encoding).\n186\t- Container images referenced by digest (`image@sha256:...`), not only by tag, in the manifest.\n187\t- A test that the same manifest gives the same ID and that changing each input changes it.\n188\t- Requested model ID and provider-reported served version recorded as separate fields.\n189\t\n190\t**Red flags:**\n191\t- `agent.type.id` is a hand-picked label, app version string, or git tag, not a content hash.\n192\t- The hash covers only some inputs (for example code but not the system prompt or model ID).\n193\t- Image referenced as `:latest` or a mutable tag, so the container input is not fixed.\n194\t- Secret values (API keys, tokens) are fed into the hash input or written into the manifest.\n195\t- The app release number is used as the model's identity.\n196\t\n197\t**Where to look:**\n198\t- .brace/manifest.yaml or other release manifest files\n199\t- CI pipelines and build scripts that compute or stamp the type ID\n200\t- Dockerfile, k8s Deployment image fields, Helm values\n201\t- prompt files and model config (where model ID is set)\n202\t\n203\t**Needs runtime evidence:** Code can show the hashing method; proving it is stable and sensitive to each input needs the A03 recipe (hash a frozen manifest twice, then change each input one at a time). A hash can't prove a hosted model's hidden weights stayed the same; that belongs to C05.\n204\t\n205\t**N/A when:** never\n206\t\n207\t## A04 · G1 · C9/Obs-T1 — Trustworthy attribution\n208\t\n209\t**Lead:** Infrastructure. **Shares:** Agent team, Security.\n210\t\n211\t### Requirement\n212\t\n213\tAssign instance and tenant identity in the trusted execution layer. Don't accept labels the model supplies. Record the delegated subject — the user or system whose authority is being used — separately from the agent's identity. Test that the agent can't pose as another instance or tenant in requests or audit records. Test that trace context survives handoffs between async jobs. Treat trace IDs and caller-supplied labels only as a way to link records. They never prove identity or permission.\n214\t\n215\t### Verification recipe\n216\t\n217\tSend fake tenant or instance labels through arguments or headers the agent controls. The trusted layer must reject or replace them using verified identity. Follow an async job and confirm it keeps the right attribution. Run an action on behalf of a user or system, and confirm the record names that delegated subject separately from the agent.\n218\t\n219\t**Evidence to keep:** Fake-input fixtures, trusted identity logs, async trace.\n220\t\n221\t### Checks for likely_pass\n222\t\n223\tRate likely_pass only if every check below is true, with a file and line for each:\n224\t\n225\t- **A04.1** Tenant and instance identity are set by the harness or platform from verified context, never from model-produced text or tool arguments.\n226\t  - *How to verify:* 1) Find every place tenant and instance IDs are set for tool calls and records. 2) Check each comes from verified context (token claims, session bound at launch). 3) Check tool JSON schemas the model fills for `tenant_id`, `user_id`, `instance_id`. 4) Answer no if any function uses the model's value, even if another function uses verified context.\n227\t- **A04.2** Tenant or instance labels in tool arguments or agent-controlled headers are rejected or overwritten with the verified values before they are used or logged.\n228\t  - *How to verify:* 1) Find tool handlers and middleware that receive tool arguments and headers. 2) Check that tenant or instance fields in arguments, or headers like `X-Tenant-Id`, are rejected or overwritten with the verified value before use or logging. 3) Answer no if any handler uses or logs the supplied value, or detects a mismatch and continues.\n229\t- **A04.3** The delegated subject is recorded separately from the agent identity.\n230\t  - *How to verify:* 1) Open the audit record schema and writer. 2) Look for a delegated-subject field (the user or system whose authority is used) separate from the agent principal field. 3) Answer no if only one identity is stored, the user overwrites the agent identity, or the field is never filled.\n231\t- **A04.4** When work is handed to an async job or queue, the trusted layer attaches the verified attribution and trace context, and the job uses them.\n232\t  - *How to verify:* 1) Find queue producers and enqueue calls (Celery, SQS, Pub/Sub, Kafka, cron). 2) Check the trusted layer adds verified identity and trace context to the job. 3) Check the worker uses those values, not ones the agent wrote. 4) Answer no if workers start new traces without parent context or take tenant from agent-written job data.\n233\t- **A04.5** Authorization never treats trace IDs or caller-supplied labels as proof of identity or permission.\n234\t  - *How to verify:* 1) Find authorization and permission-lookup code. 2) Check what each decision is keyed on. 3) Answer no if any check uses a trace ID, span ID, correlation ID, request label, or caller-supplied header as identity or to look up permissions.\n235\t\n236\t### Not covered here\n237\t\n238\tFile these problems under their owning item, not this one, unless this item's own requirement is also broken:\n239\t\n240\t- Agent sharing the launching user's identity → **A01**\n241\t- Prompt a parent gave a sub-agent not recorded → **A06**\n242\t- Sub-agent authorized using an earlier actor's authority → **E08**\n243\t- Action records missing any of the six identity fields → **A02**\n244\t\n245\t### Heuristics\n246\t\n247\t**Provable from code:** yes\n248\t\n249\t**Look for:**\n250\t- Tenant ID and instance ID are taken from verified identity in the harness or gateway (token claims, session bound at launch) and injected into tool calls server-side.\n251\t- Tool handlers ignore or overwrite any tenant, user, or instance field in model-produced arguments.\n252\t- The delegated subject (user or system) is recorded in its own field, separate from the agent principal.\n253\t- Async job payloads carry trace context and verified identity, and workers re-check identity rather than trusting the payload.\n254\t- Authorization checks use verified principals, never trace IDs or request labels.\n255\t\n256\t**Red flags:**\n257\t- Tenant ID taken from a model-produced tool argument (for example `tenant_id` in the tool JSON schema the model fills).\n258\t- Instance or tenant labels read from caller-controlled HTTP headers (for example `X-Tenant-Id`) without checking them against the token.\n259\t- Trace IDs, span IDs, or correlation IDs used for authorization or to look up permissions.\n260\t- Audit records store only the agent identity, or only the user identity, with no separate delegated subject.\n261\t- Queued jobs lose trace context, or workers re-derive tenant from job data the agent wrote.\n262\t\n263\t**Where to look:**\n264\t- tool JSON schemas and function signatures exposed to the model\n265\t- tool handler code and gateway/middleware auth\n266\t- queue producer/consumer code and job payload schemas\n267\t- audit record schema\n268\t\n269\t**Needs runtime evidence:** Code review can usually find the gap; the A04 recipe (fake tenant labels in arguments and headers, async attribution trace) confirms the trusted layer rejects or replaces them at runtime.\n270\t\n271\t**N/A when:** never\n272\t\n273\t## A05 · G1 · C8/C9/Obs-T1 — Operational lookup\n274\t\n275\t**Lead:** Operations. **Shares:** Business owner, Governance, Infrastructure.\n276\t\n277\t### Requirement\n278\t\n279\tStart from one suspicious action. Find the team responsible, the current operator, the deployment manifest, and the live instance. Show that you can contain the right instance or agent type without cutting off the launching user's other access.\n280\t\n281\t### Verification recipe\n282\t\n283\tGive an operator a test action or trace ID with no other context. Have them find the manifest, owners, and live instance, and contain that instance. Confirm the targeted run stops and unrelated test runs keep their access.\n284\t\n285\t**Evidence to keep:** Lookup queries, drill timeline, results for target and non-target runs.\n286\t\n287\t### Checks for likely_pass\n288\t\n289\tRate likely_pass only if every check below is true, with a file and line for each:\n290\t\n291\t- **A05.1** Each audit record carries the agent-type-id and agent-instance-id, which resolve to a stored deployment manifest and a live instance.\n292\t  - *How to verify:* 1) Open the audit record writer. 2) Check `agent.type.id` and `agent.instance.id` are written on every record. 3) Check the type ID can be looked up to a stored manifest (registry, manifest store, file keyed by hash). 4) Answer no if either field is missing or nothing stores manifests by that ID. Answer unknown if the lookup store is outside this repo.\n293\t- **A05.2** An ownership record in code or config maps each agent type to a responsible team and a current operator or on-call rotation.\n294\t  - *How to verify:* 1) Look for CODEOWNERS, `catalog-info.yaml`, `.brace/deployment.yaml`, or another ownership file. 2) Check each agent type maps to a team and to a current operator or on-call rotation. 3) Answer no if an agent type has no owner, or owners exist only in prose with no link. Answer unknown if ownership lives in an outside catalog.\n295\t- **A05.3** A containment control can disable a single instance or a single agent type (for example revoke its principal or scale it to zero) without touching the launching user's credentials.\n296\t  - *How to verify:* 1) Find stop and revoke controls: admin endpoints, CLIs, feature flags, scale-to-zero, per-instance token revocation. 2) Check their scope is one instance or one agent type. 3) Answer no if the only control is a global switch or revoking a shared or user credential. Answer unknown if containment is run by an outside platform.\n297\t\n298\t### Not covered here\n299\t\n300\tFile these problems under their owning item, not this one, unless this item's own requirement is also broken:\n301\t\n302\t- Stop doesn't halt running work or open tool streams within a set time → **R11**\n303\t- No drill for a compromised shared tool or agent type → **E12**\n304\t- Action records missing any of the six identity fields → **A02**\n305\t\n306\t### Heuristics\n307\t\n308\t**Provable from code:** partly\n309\t\n310\t**Look for:**\n311\t- Telemetry links each action to type ID, instance ID, owner, and trace, so one action ID can be traced to a manifest and a live instance.\n312\t- A registry or index mapping `agent.type.id` to manifest, owners, and running instances (service catalog entry, CODEOWNERS, `.brace/deployment.yaml` roles).\n313\t- A kill or quarantine control scoped to one instance or one agent type (per-instance token revocation, per-type feature flag, scaling one deployment to zero).\n314\t- A runbook that starts from a trace or action ID and lists the lookup and containment steps.\n315\t\n316\t**Red flags:**\n317\t- The only stop control is a global switch or revoking a shared credential, which would cut off the launching user or other agents.\n318\t- No mapping from runtime identifiers back to a manifest or owner team.\n319\t- Instances are not individually addressable (no instance ID, no per-run credential).\n320\t- Owner information lives only in a wiki with no link from telemetry.\n321\t\n322\t**Where to look:**\n323\t- runbooks, incident docs, service catalog files (for example `catalog-info.yaml`)\n324\t- admin or ops endpoints and CLIs for stopping runs\n325\t- credential issuing code (per-instance vs shared)\n326\t- CODEOWNERS and deployment records\n327\t\n328\t**Needs runtime evidence:** Code can't show an operator can do the lookup in practice or that containment hits only the target. Needs the A05 drill: start from one action or trace ID, find manifest, owners, and instance, contain it, and confirm unrelated runs keep access.\n329\t\n330\t**N/A when:** never\n331\t\n332\t## A06 · G3 · Obs-T3 — Parent and prompt provenance\n333\t\n334\t**Lead:** Agent team. **Shares:** Data and privacy, Security.\n335\t\n336\t### Requirement\n337\t\n338\tFor every sub-agent, record its own type and instance IDs, a link to its parent, trace links, and the prompt the parent gave it. Trace a nested action back to that prompt. Protect sensitive prompt text with restricted storage and a clear redaction policy. Standard GenAI instrumentation records prompts only when you turn that on, so confirm it is on for this path.\n339\t\n340\t### Verification recipe\n341\t\n342\tStart a child and a grandchild agent, each with a test prompt you can tell apart. From the grandchild's tool action, trace the chain back to the root. Using the approved audit role, pull each prompt a parent passed down. A role without approval must not be able to read protected prompt text. Confirm prompt capture is turned on. Standard top-level agent attributes don't identify the direct parent instance.\n343\t\n344\t**Evidence to keep:** Nested trace, protected prompt references, access test.\n345\t\n346\t### Checks for likely_pass\n347\t\n348\tRate likely_pass only if every check below is true, with a file and line for each:\n349\t\n350\t- **A06.1** Each sub-agent gets its own type ID and instance ID, assigned by the harness when it is started.\n351\t  - *How to verify:* 1) Find sub-agent spawn code (handoffs, subgraphs, subagents, A2A task creation). 2) Check the harness assigns the child a new `agent.instance.id` and a type ID at start. 3) Answer no if any spawn path reuses the parent's IDs, assigns none, or takes the IDs from the model's request.\n352\t- **A06.2** Each sub-agent's records include its direct parent's instance ID and a trace link to the parent.\n353\t  - *How to verify:* 1) Open sub-agent spawn code and the child's record writer. 2) Look for a parent instance field (for example `agent.parent.instance_id`) and trace propagation (span parent, span link, `traceparent` in queued payloads). 3) Answer no if any spawn path, including remote or queued, starts a fresh trace or omits the parent ID.\n354\t- **A06.3** The prompt the parent passed to the child is captured on this path, with prompt capture switched on in config rather than left at the default off.\n355\t  - *How to verify:* 1) Find where the parent's prompt to the child is recorded (for example `agent.parent.prompt`, or a hash plus stored text). 2) Check prompt content capture is turned on in config, for example a GenAI capture-content env var or SDK option. 3) Answer no if capture is left at its default off, or the setting falls back to false when unset.\n356\t- **A06.4** Captured prompt text is stored with restricted access and a redaction policy, and only an approved audit role can read it.\n357\t  - *How to verify:* 1) Find where captured prompt text is stored (bucket, table, log index). 2) Check its IAM or ACL limits reads to an approved audit role. 3) Check a redaction policy is defined and applied. 4) Answer no if prompts go to general logs the whole team can read, or no redaction exists. Answer unknown if storage access is set outside this repo.\n358\t\n359\t### Not covered here\n360\t\n361\tFile these problems under their owning item, not this one, unless this item's own requirement is also broken:\n362\t\n363\t- Sub-agent given powers beyond its task → **E08**\n364\t- Children still running after the parent is stopped → **E09**\n365\t- No limit on how many sub-agents a run can start → **B07**\n366\t\n367\t### Heuristics\n368\t\n369\t**Provable from code:** partly\n370\t\n371\t**Look for:**\n372\t- Sub-agent spawn code assigns the child its own `agent.type.id` and `agent.instance.id` and records a parent link (for example `agent.parent.instance_id`).\n373\t- The prompt the parent passes is recorded as `agent.parent.prompt`, either in full or as a hash reference with the text in restricted storage.\n374\t- Trace context is passed into the child (span parent or span link), including for remote or queued sub-agents.\n375\t- GenAI prompt capture is turned on for this path (for example a content-capture env var or SDK option) and a redaction policy is defined.\n376\t- Restricted storage for prompt text with access limited to an approved audit role.\n377\t\n378\t**Red flags:**\n379\t- Sub-agents reuse the parent's instance ID or have no IDs of their own.\n380\t- Child agents start a fresh trace with no link to the parent span.\n381\t- Prompt content capture is off by default and never turned on, so parent prompts are never recorded.\n382\t- Full parent prompts with customer data are written to general-purpose logs anyone on the team can read.\n383\t- Only top-level `gen_ai.agent.*` attributes are set, which don't identify the direct parent instance.\n384\t\n385\t**Where to look:**\n386\t- sub-agent or handoff code (for example LangGraph subgraphs, OpenAI Agents SDK handoffs, Claude Agent SDK subagents, A2A task creation)\n387\t- OpenTelemetry instrumentation settings and env vars for content capture\n388\t- prompt storage and its access policy (bucket or table IAM)\n389\t\n390\t**Needs runtime evidence:** Needs the A06 recipe: a nested child and grandchild trace back to the root, pulling each parent prompt with the approved role, and a denied read with an unapproved role.\n391\t\n392\t**N/A when:** The agent never starts sub-agents (no child agents, handoffs, or delegated agent tasks).\n393\t\n394\t## A07 · G3 · C7/Obs-T1 — Every model in the loop\n395\t\n396\t**Lead:** Agent team. **Shares:** Model team, Security.\n397\t\n398\t### Requirement\n399\t\n400\tList all helper models, such as verifiers, judges, rerankers, and world models. Give each one an identity based on its available model version or digest, its prompt, and its configuration, and record which decisions it made. Show that a swapped, drifting, or failing checker counts as a failure of the control that depends on it.\n401\t\n402\t### Verification recipe\n403\t\n404\tList every model call in a typical workflow and match each to a model record. Swap a checker's version, and simulate a timeout or an invalid checker decision. The set control-failure response must happen and name the affected model.\n405\t\n406\t**Evidence to keep:** Call list, model provenance records, failure and swap results.\n407\t\n408\t### Checks for likely_pass\n409\t\n410\tRate likely_pass only if every check below is true, with a file and line for each:\n411\t\n412\t- **A07.1** A model inventory in code or config lists every helper model the agent calls (verifiers, judges, rerankers, embedding models, world models).\n413\t  - *How to verify:* 1) Search for every model call: provider SDK clients, model name strings, embedding, rerank, moderation, and judge calls. 2) Compare that list with the model inventory. 3) Answer no if any helper model called in code is missing from the inventory, or no inventory exists.\n414\t- **A07.2** Each helper model is identified by a pinned version or digest plus its prompt and configuration, not by an alias.\n415\t  - *How to verify:* 1) For each helper model, find its model ID and follow env vars to the final value. 2) Check it is a pinned version or digest, and that its prompt and configuration are also recorded. 3) Answer no if any is an alias (`-latest`, undated name), falls back to one by default, or is hard-coded inline outside the inventory.\n416\t- **A07.3** Each helper model's decisions are logged with that model's identity.\n417\t  - *How to verify:* 1) Open each helper or checker call site. 2) Check its decision is logged with that model's ID and version. 3) Answer no if decisions are logged without model identity, or logged only on the allow or success branch.\n418\t- **A07.4** A checker timeout, error, invalid output, or version mismatch makes the control that depends on it fail (block or escalate), not pass by default.\n419\t  - *How to verify:* 1) Open each checker call site (judge, guardrail, moderation, verifier). 2) Read every `try/except`, `catch`, timeout, and parse-error branch. 3) Check each one blocks or escalates. 4) Answer no if any branch returns allow, skips the check, treats unparseable output as safe, or logs and continues. Also no if the returned model version is never compared with the expected one.\n420\t\n421\t### Not covered here\n422\t\n423\tFile these problems under their owning item, not this one, unless this item's own requirement is also broken:\n424\t\n425\t- Main agent model changing behind a fixed name unnoticed → **C05**\n426\t- Second-model review not tested against evasive behavior → **R10**\n427\t- Self-hosted model weights loaded without a signature or format check → **C05**\n428\t\n429\t### Heuristics\n430\t\n431\t**Provable from code:** partly\n432\t\n433\t**Look for:**\n434\t- Every model call site is listed with its role (main, judge, verifier, reranker, embedding, router, fallback) and a model record.\n435\t- Helper model IDs, prompts, and config are pinned in config and covered by the manifest, not hard-coded inline with aliases.\n436\t- Checker decisions are logged with the checker's model ID and version.\n437\t- Code treats a checker timeout, error, or invalid output as a failure of the dependent control (block or escalate), not as a pass.\n438\t\n439\t**Red flags:**\n440\t- Judge or guardrail call wrapped in `try/except` that returns allow or skips the check on error or timeout.\n441\t- Helper models (embedding, reranker, classifier) called with no recorded model version.\n442\t- Checker model set to a moving alias (for example `-latest`) while the main model is pinned.\n443\t- Unparseable checker output is defaulted to safe or approved.\n444\t- Model routing or fallback silently switches to a different model with no record.\n445\t\n446\t**Where to look:**\n447\t- all LLM client call sites (search for provider SDK clients and model name strings)\n448\t- guardrail, judge, moderation, and evaluator modules\n449\t- retrieval pipeline (embedding and reranker config)\n450\t- model routing and fallback config\n451\t\n452\t**Needs runtime evidence:** Code can list call sites and failure handling. The A07 recipe proves the list is complete in a real workflow and that a swapped, timed-out, or invalid checker triggers the set failure response.\n453\t\n454\t**N/A when:** The agent uses no helper models at all (no verifiers, judges, rerankers, embedding models, or world models), and the model inventory shows that.\n455\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"29563294-217c-407e-8b87-42521726a071","uuid":"9dc1dbf3-7e79-4eb4-8697-b2f0543c6c81","timestamp":"2026-10-02T16:15:03.899Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/private/var/folders/6v/yk9bzvrn5zx0kxll7d4szzb80000gn/T/brace-repeat-80qi2mwl/skill/references/agent.md","content":"<!-- Generated by skills/gen_references.py from CHECKLIST.md, CHECKLIST-VERIFICATION.md, ROLES.md, and heuristics. Do not edit by hand. -->\n\n# A — Agent\n\nUse this file to rate each item from the code and configuration. The requirement is the checklist text. The heuristics say what to look for. Ratings: likely_pass, gap, needs_evidence, or na_candidate. Never rate pass.\n\n## A01 · G1 · C2/Obs-T1 — Distinct agent identity\n\n**Lead:** Infrastructure. **Shares:** Agent team.\n\n### Requirement\n\nGive the agent its own non-human identity. Keep it separate from the user who launched it and from unrelated agents. Show that permissions and revocation belong to that identity, even when a user starts the run.\n\n### Verification recipe\n\nLaunch the agent from a user account. Look at the identity (principal) the agent was issued, and compare the user's permissions with the agent's. Revoke only the agent and try again. Its identity and access must be separate from the user's and from unrelated agents.\n\n**Evidence to keep:** Identity bindings, permission comparison, revocation results.\n\n### Checks for likely_pass\n\nRate likely_pass only if every check below is true, with a file and line for each:\n\n- **A01.1** The agent runs under its own workload principal (service account, IAM role, SPIFFE ID, or agent ID) that no other service or unrelated agent uses.\n  - *How to verify:* 1) Find the agent's identity: pod `serviceAccountName`, IAM role, SPIFFE ID, or agent ID in IaC or deploy config. 2) Search other deployments, services, and agents in the repo for the same name or role. 3) Answer no if it is shared, `default`, the main backend's identity, or only a string label with no credential. Answer unknown if identity is assigned outside this repo.\n- **A01.2** Tool and API clients get credentials from the agent's own principal, not from the launching user's forwarded token or session cookie.\n  - *How to verify:* 1) Find every place tool and API clients get a token or key. 2) Trace where each comes from: workload identity or token exchange, versus the incoming request's `Authorization` header or session cookie. 3) Check every client, not just the main one. 4) Answer no if any client forwards the user's token or cookie as-is, or uses a shared static key.\n- **A01.3** When a user starts a run, the user is carried as a delegated subject (for example a token exchange with separate act and sub), while the agent keeps its own principal.\n  - *How to verify:* 1) Find the code that starts a run for a user. 2) Look for token exchange (RFC 8693, `subject_token`, `actor_token`, `act`/`sub` claims) or a separate delegated-subject field. 3) Check the agent's own principal is still the actor. 4) Answer no if the user's token is used directly or the user replaces the agent identity. Answer unknown if the exchange happens in an outside identity service.\n- **A01.4** Permissions are bound to the agent principal in IaC or RBAC, so revoking that principal alone removes the agent's access without disabling the user.\n  - *How to verify:* 1) Find IAM policies, RoleBindings, or grants in Terraform, Helm, or Kubernetes YAML. 2) Check they bind to the agent principal, not to a user, group, or shared role. 3) Check that removing that binding alone would cut the agent's access. 4) Answer no if access depends on the user's grants or a shared credential. Answer unknown if permissions are not defined in this repo.\n\n### Not covered here\n\nFile these problems under their owning item, not this one, unless this item's own requirement is also broken:\n\n- Action records missing any of the six identity fields → **A02**\n- Instance or tenant labels taken from what the model supplies → **A04**\n- A credential scoped to more operations, resources, or tenants than needed → **B02**\n- Revocation delay from cached or self-contained tokens not measured → **B04**\n\n### Heuristics\n\n**Provable from code:** partly\n\n**Look for:**\n- The agent runs as its own workload identity: a dedicated Kubernetes ServiceAccount, cloud IAM role (AWS IAM role, GCP service account, Azure managed identity), SPIFFE ID, or Entra Agent ID, not shared with other services.\n- Tool and API clients get credentials from that identity (workload identity federation, IRSA, token exchange per RFC 8693) instead of from the user session.\n- When a user starts a run, the user is passed as a delegated subject (for example an `act`/`sub` pair in an exchanged token), while the agent keeps its own principal.\n- Permissions are attached to the agent principal in IaC (Terraform IAM policies, RBAC RoleBindings) and are narrower than the launching user's.\n- A documented way to revoke or disable the agent principal alone (delete role binding, disable service principal, rotate its key).\n\n**Red flags:**\n- Agent calls downstream APIs with the launching user's OAuth access token or session cookie, forwarded as-is.\n- One service account, API key, or IAM role is shared by several unrelated agents or by the agent and the main app backend.\n- Long-lived static API keys for the agent in env files or code, with no per-agent principal behind them.\n- Agent identity is only a string label in config or a prompt (for example `agent_name: support-bot`) with no credential bound to it.\n- Revoking access would require disabling the user or a shared credential, because there is no agent-only principal.\n\n**Where to look:**\n- Kubernetes ServiceAccount, RoleBinding, and pod spec `serviceAccountName`\n- Terraform/CloudFormation/Bicep IAM roles, policies, and identity federation\n- auth and credential setup in the harness and tool clients (token exchange, OAuth client config)\n- .env files, secret manager references, and Helm values\n\n**Needs runtime evidence:** Code can show how identity is wired, not that the issued principal and its live permissions differ from the user's, or that revoking only the agent works. Needs the A01 recipe: launch from a user account, compare permissions, revoke the agent alone, and retry.\n\n**N/A when:** never\n\n## A02 · G1 · Obs-T1 — All six identity fields\n\n**Lead:** Agent team. **Shares:** Infrastructure, Business owner, Governance.\n\n### Requirement\n\nCheck that every action records the accountable party, operational owner, tenant, agent-type-id, agent-instance-id, and trace context. Look at successful, denied, failed, and background actions, not just the main request path.\n\n### Verification recipe\n\nQuery every record from a controlled run with successful, denied, failed, async, and background actions. Count missing or empty values for each of the six fields. The count must be zero. Confirm the values match the known run and tenant. Confirm tenant and owner values come from the trusted layer, not from baggage or tracestate headers, which have no integrity protection.\n\n**Evidence to keep:** Field-completeness query, counts, sample records.\n\n### Checks for likely_pass\n\nRate likely_pass only if every check below is true, with a file and line for each:\n\n- **A02.1** The audit record schema has all six fields: accountable party, operational owner, tenant, agent-type-id, agent-instance-id, and trace context.\n  - *How to verify:* 1) Find the audit record schema or the function that writes audit entries. 2) Check for each of the six: accountable party, operational owner, tenant, `agent.type.id`, `agent.instance.id`, trace context (`traceparent` or trace and span IDs). 3) Answer no if any one is missing from the schema or never set.\n- **A02.2** All six fields are written on denied, failed, async, and background actions too, not only on the successful main request path.\n  - *How to verify:* 1) List every path that writes an audit record, or should: success, policy deny, `except`/`catch` blocks, retries, queue workers, cron jobs, webhooks, other endpoints such as approvals, and refusals before the run starts (quota, rate limit, auth) that return an error without logging. 2) For each, check all six fields are set, ideally by one shared wrapper. 3) Answer no if any deny, error, async, or background path writes records without them, or writes no record at all.\n- **A02.3** Trace context (trace and span IDs) is stored in the audit record itself, not only in separate tracing spans.\n  - *How to verify:* 1) Open the audit record writer. 2) Check it stores trace and span IDs (or `traceparent`) as fields in the record itself. 3) Answer no if trace context exists only in OpenTelemetry spans, or the record's trace field is never filled.\n- **A02.4** Tenant and owner values come from the trusted layer, not from baggage, tracestate, or other unprotected headers.\n  - *How to verify:* 1) Find where tenant and owner values for audit records are read. 2) Trace each back to its source: token claims, verified session, IAM tags, or SPIFFE path, versus OTel baggage, `tracestate`, headers like `X-Tenant-Id`, request body, or tool arguments. 3) Answer no if any path takes them from an unprotected source.\n\n### Not covered here\n\nFile these problems under their owning item, not this one, unless this item's own requirement is also broken:\n\n- Identity labels taken from what the model supplies → **A04**\n- Agent-type-id not computed as a hash of its identity inputs → **A03**\n- Agent able to erase or rewrite its own audit history → **E10**\n- Sub-agent record missing its parent link or parent prompt → **A06**\n\n### Heuristics\n\n**Provable from code:** partly\n\n**Look for:**\n- Telemetry or audit code sets all six fields on every action: accountable party, operational owner, tenant, `agent.type.id`, `agent.instance.id`, and W3C trace context (`traceparent`).\n- Fields are set in one shared place (span processor, logging middleware, audit wrapper around tool dispatch) so every tool call inherits them.\n- Denied, failed, retried, async, and background paths (queue workers, cron jobs, webhooks) also emit the fields, not just the main request handler.\n- Tenant and owner values come from verified identity (token claims, IAM tags, SPIFFE path) in the trusted layer.\n- A test or query that counts missing or empty identity fields per record.\n\n**Red flags:**\n- Identity attributes are added only on the success path; `except`/`catch` blocks and policy-denial branches log without them.\n- Background jobs or queue consumers start new traces with no tenant, instance, or type ID.\n- Tenant or owner is read from OTel baggage or `tracestate`, which have no integrity protection.\n- `agent.type.id` or `agent.instance.id` never appears anywhere in the code or config.\n- `gen_ai.agent.id` is overwritten with a per-run ID and used as the only agent identifier.\n\n**Where to look:**\n- OpenTelemetry setup: span processors, resource attributes, instrumentation config\n- tool dispatch and policy-check code, including error and deny branches\n- queue workers, schedulers, webhook handlers\n- audit log writer and log schema definitions\n\n**Needs runtime evidence:** Code can't prove every emitted record is complete or that values match the real run and tenant. Needs the A02 recipe: query all records from a controlled run with success, deny, failure, async, and background actions and show zero missing values.\n\n**N/A when:** never\n\n## A03 · G1 · Obs-T1 — Content-derived type identity\n\n**Lead:** Agent team. **Shares:** Model team.\n\n### Requirement\n\nCompute the agent-type-id as a hash of the container digest, harness, system prompt, model ID and version, and configuration. The model part includes checkpoint and fine-tune or adapter references where they apply. Keep the list of inputs (the manifest) and the hashing steps. Check that changing any input changes the ID, and that the same manifest gives the same ID.\n\n  Why this is hard: A hash only covers the inputs you can see. It can't prove that a hosted model's hidden weights or training stayed the same behind the same name.\n\n  If you can't fully do it: Hash the exact local artifacts and the model, checkpoint, and adapter references you have, using a manifest anyone can rebuild. Record the model name you asked for separately from the version the provider says it served. Mark hidden information clearly. State that the ID fingerprints this recorded setup. Handle changes behind remote names under C05. Never use the agent app's release number as the model's identity. Never present a local hash as proof of the provider's hidden state.\n\n  Tradeoffs you can accept: A fingerprint that covers only the recorded setup, with the provider's hidden details clearly outside the claim. Track changes behind remote names under C05. Missing hashes or model references for artifacts you control still fail this G1 item.\n\n### Verification recipe\n\nHash the same frozen manifest twice. The IDs must match. Change the container, harness, prompt, model/checkpoint/adapter reference, and configuration one at a time. Each change must produce a different ID. Confirm the exact input format for hashing is documented and that no secret values are exposed.\n\n**Evidence to keep:** Hashing steps and version, input manifests, expected and actual IDs.\n\n### Checks for likely_pass\n\nRate likely_pass only if every check below is true, with a file and line for each:\n\n- **A03.1** The agent-type-id is a hash over a manifest that includes the container digest, harness, system prompt, model ID and version, and configuration.\n  - *How to verify:* 1) Find the code or CI step that computes `agent.type.id` (search `sha256`, `hashlib`, `createHash`, `type_id`). 2) List its inputs. 3) Check each is there: container digest, harness, system prompt, model ID and version, configuration. 4) Answer no if the ID is a label, version string, or git tag, or any input is missing.\n- **A03.2** The hash covers every file that defines the prompt, tools, and configuration, with no file or directory skipped or excluded.\n  - *How to verify:* 1) Open the function that lists files to hash. 2) Write down every filter it applies: hidden files, names starting with '_' or '__', extensions, ignore lists, glob patterns. 3) For each filter, check whether a file that defines tools, prompts, or config would be skipped (for example `__init__.py`, `index.ts`, `.env`, `config/*.local.*`). 4) Answer no if any such file is skipped.\n- **A03.3** The model input is a pinned version or snapshot (plus checkpoint and adapter references where used), not an alias and not the app's release number.\n  - *How to verify:* 1) Find the model ID that goes into the manifest or hash. 2) Follow env vars and config to the final value, including defaults such as `getenv('MODEL', ...)`. 3) Answer no if it is an alias (`-latest`, no date or version), falls back to one when unset, or is the app release number. Also no if adapters or checkpoints are used but left out.\n- **A03.4** The manifest is put in a fixed, documented order before hashing, so the same manifest always gives the same ID. *(A document can show this.)*\n  - *How to verify:* 1) Open the serialization step before hashing. 2) Check for a fixed order: sorted keys (`sort_keys=True`), sorted file lists, fixed encoding and line endings. 3) Check file lists are not taken straight from directory listing or map iteration order. 4) Answer no if any unsorted dict, map, or filesystem order reaches the hash.\n- **A03.5** Secret values are not placed in the hashed manifest in a form that exposes them.\n  - *How to verify:* 1) List every value fed into the manifest or hash. 2) Check whether any are secrets: API keys, tokens, passwords, private keys, `.env` contents. 3) Answer no if a secret value is written in plain text to the stored manifest, or the whole environment is dumped into it.\n\n### Not covered here\n\nFile these problems under their owning item, not this one, unless this item's own requirement is also broken:\n\n- Changed running config still reporting the old approved ID → **C03**\n- Hosted model changing behind a fixed name → **C05**\n- Release manifest missing training job, dataset, or model provider records → **C01**\n- Action records missing any of the six identity fields → **A02**\n\n### Heuristics\n\n**Provable from code:** partly\n\n**Look for:**\n- A manifest listing the hash inputs: container digest, harness version or files, system prompt, model ID and version, checkpoint/adapter references, and configuration.\n- Code or a CI step that computes `agent.type.id` as a hash (for example `sha256:`) over that manifest with documented, canonical serialization (sorted keys, fixed encoding).\n- Container images referenced by digest (`image@sha256:...`), not only by tag, in the manifest.\n- A test that the same manifest gives the same ID and that changing each input changes it.\n- Requested model ID and provider-reported served version recorded as separate fields.\n\n**Red flags:**\n- `agent.type.id` is a hand-picked label, app version string, or git tag, not a content hash.\n- The hash covers only some inputs (for example code but not the system prompt or model ID).\n- Image referenced as `:latest` or a mutable tag, so the container input is not fixed.\n- Secret values (API keys, tokens) are fed into the hash input or written into the manifest.\n- The app release number is used as the model's identity.\n\n**Where to look:**\n- .brace/manifest.yaml or other release manifest files\n- CI pipelines and build scripts that compute or stamp the type ID\n- Dockerfile, k8s Deployment image fields, Helm values\n- prompt files and model config (where model ID is set)\n\n**Needs runtime evidence:** Code can show the hashing method; proving it is stable and sensitive to each input needs the A03 recipe (hash a frozen manifest twice, then change each input one at a time). A hash can't prove a hosted model's hidden weights stayed the same; that belongs to C05.\n\n**N/A when:** never\n\n## A04 · G1 · C9/Obs-T1 — Trustworthy attribution\n\n**Lead:** Infrastructure. **Shares:** Agent team, Security.\n\n### Requirement\n\nAssign instance and tenant identity in the trusted execution layer. Don't accept labels the model supplies. Record the delegated subject — the user or system whose authority is being used — separately from the agent's identity. Test that the agent can't pose as another instance or tenant in requests or audit records. Test that trace context survives handoffs between async jobs. Treat trace IDs and caller-supplied labels only as a way to link records. They never prove identity or permission.\n\n### Verification recipe\n\nSend fake tenant or instance labels through arguments or headers the agent controls. The trusted layer must reject or replace them using verified identity. Follow an async job and confirm it keeps the right attribution. Run an action on behalf of a user or system, and confirm the record names that delegated subject separately from the agent.\n\n**Evidence to keep:** Fake-input fixtures, trusted identity logs, async trace.\n\n### Checks for likely_pass\n\nRate likely_pass only if every check below is true, with a file and line for each:\n\n- **A04.1** Tenant and instance identity are set by the harness or platform from verified context, never from model-produced text or tool arguments.\n  - *How to verify:* 1) Find every place tenant and instance IDs are set for tool calls and records. 2) Check each comes from verified context (token claims, session bound at launch). 3) Check tool JSON schemas the model fills for `tenant_id`, `user_id`, `instance_id`. 4) Answer no if any function uses the model's value, even if another function uses verified context.\n- **A04.2** Tenant or instance labels in tool arguments or agent-controlled headers are rejected or overwritten with the verified values before they are used or logged.\n  - *How to verify:* 1) Find tool handlers and middleware that receive tool arguments and headers. 2) Check that tenant or instance fields in arguments, or headers like `X-Tenant-Id`, are rejected or overwritten with the verified value before use or logging. 3) Answer no if any handler uses or logs the supplied value, or detects a mismatch and continues.\n- **A04.3** The delegated subject is recorded separately from the agent identity.\n  - *How to verify:* 1) Open the audit record schema and writer. 2) Look for a delegated-subject field (the user or system whose authority is used) separate from the agent principal field. 3) Answer no if only one identity is stored, the user overwrites the agent identity, or the field is never filled.\n- **A04.4** When work is handed to an async job or queue, the trusted layer attaches the verified attribution and trace context, and the job uses them.\n  - *How to verify:* 1) Find queue producers and enqueue calls (Celery, SQS, Pub/Sub, Kafka, cron). 2) Check the trusted layer adds verified identity and trace context to the job. 3) Check the worker uses those values, not ones the agent wrote. 4) Answer no if workers start new traces without parent context or take tenant from agent-written job data.\n- **A04.5** Authorization never treats trace IDs or caller-supplied labels as proof of identity or permission.\n  - *How to verify:* 1) Find authorization and permission-lookup code. 2) Check what each decision is keyed on. 3) Answer no if any check uses a trace ID, span ID, correlation ID, request label, or caller-supplied header as identity or to look up permissions.\n\n### Not covered here\n\nFile these problems under their owning item, not this one, unless this item's own requirement is also broken:\n\n- Agent sharing the launching user's identity → **A01**\n- Prompt a parent gave a sub-agent not recorded → **A06**\n- Sub-agent authorized using an earlier actor's authority → **E08**\n- Action records missing any of the six identity fields → **A02**\n\n### Heuristics\n\n**Provable from code:** yes\n\n**Look for:**\n- Tenant ID and instance ID are taken from verified identity in the harness or gateway (token claims, session bound at launch) and injected into tool calls server-side.\n- Tool handlers ignore or overwrite any tenant, user, or instance field in model-produced arguments.\n- The delegated subject (user or system) is recorded in its own field, separate from the agent principal.\n- Async job payloads carry trace context and verified identity, and workers re-check identity rather than trusting the payload.\n- Authorization checks use verified principals, never trace IDs or request labels.\n\n**Red flags:**\n- Tenant ID taken from a model-produced tool argument (for example `tenant_id` in the tool JSON schema the model fills).\n- Instance or tenant labels read from caller-controlled HTTP headers (for example `X-Tenant-Id`) without checking them against the token.\n- Trace IDs, span IDs, or correlation IDs used for authorization or to look up permissions.\n- Audit records store only the agent identity, or only the user identity, with no separate delegated subject.\n- Queued jobs lose trace context, or workers re-derive tenant from job data the agent wrote.\n\n**Where to look:**\n- tool JSON schemas and function signatures exposed to the model\n- tool handler code and gateway/middleware auth\n- queue producer/consumer code and job payload schemas\n- audit record schema\n\n**Needs runtime evidence:** Code review can usually find the gap; the A04 recipe (fake tenant labels in arguments and headers, async attribution trace) confirms the trusted layer rejects or replaces them at runtime.\n\n**N/A when:** never\n\n## A05 · G1 · C8/C9/Obs-T1 — Operational lookup\n\n**Lead:** Operations. **Shares:** Business owner, Governance, Infrastructure.\n\n### Requirement\n\nStart from one suspicious action. Find the team responsible, the current operator, the deployment manifest, and the live instance. Show that you can contain the right instance or agent type without cutting off the launching user's other access.\n\n### Verification recipe\n\nGive an operator a test action or trace ID with no other context. Have them find the manifest, owners, and live instance, and contain that instance. Confirm the targeted run stops and unrelated test runs keep their access.\n\n**Evidence to keep:** Lookup queries, drill timeline, results for target and non-target runs.\n\n### Checks for likely_pass\n\nRate likely_pass only if every check below is true, with a file and line for each:\n\n- **A05.1** Each audit record carries the agent-type-id and agent-instance-id, which resolve to a stored deployment manifest and a live instance.\n  - *How to verify:* 1) Open the audit record writer. 2) Check `agent.type.id` and `agent.instance.id` are written on every record. 3) Check the type ID can be looked up to a stored manifest (registry, manifest store, file keyed by hash). 4) Answer no if either field is missing or nothing stores manifests by that ID. Answer unknown if the lookup store is outside this repo.\n- **A05.2** An ownership record in code or config maps each agent type to a responsible team and a current operator or on-call rotation.\n  - *How to verify:* 1) Look for CODEOWNERS, `catalog-info.yaml`, `.brace/deployment.yaml`, or another ownership file. 2) Check each agent type maps to a team and to a current operator or on-call rotation. 3) Answer no if an agent type has no owner, or owners exist only in prose with no link. Answer unknown if ownership lives in an outside catalog.\n- **A05.3** A containment control can disable a single instance or a single agent type (for example revoke its principal or scale it to zero) without touching the launching user's credentials.\n  - *How to verify:* 1) Find stop and revoke controls: admin endpoints, CLIs, feature flags, scale-to-zero, per-instance token revocation. 2) Check their scope is one instance or one agent type. 3) Answer no if the only control is a global switch or revoking a shared or user credential. Answer unknown if containment is run by an outside platform.\n\n### Not covered here\n\nFile these problems under their owning item, not this one, unless this item's own requirement is also broken:\n\n- Stop doesn't halt running work or open tool streams within a set time → **R11**\n- No drill for a compromised shared tool or agent type → **E12**\n- Action records missing any of the six identity fields → **A02**\n\n### Heuristics\n\n**Provable from code:** partly\n\n**Look for:**\n- Telemetry links each action to type ID, instance ID, owner, and trace, so one action ID can be traced to a manifest and a live instance.\n- A registry or index mapping `agent.type.id` to manifest, owners, and running instances (service catalog entry, CODEOWNERS, `.brace/deployment.yaml` roles).\n- A kill or quarantine control scoped to one instance or one agent type (per-instance token revocation, per-type feature flag, scaling one deployment to zero).\n- A runbook that starts from a trace or action ID and lists the lookup and containment steps.\n\n**Red flags:**\n- The only stop control is a global switch or revoking a shared credential, which would cut off the launching user or other agents.\n- No mapping from runtime identifiers back to a manifest or owner team.\n- Instances are not individually addressable (no instance ID, no per-run credential).\n- Owner information lives only in a wiki with no link from telemetry.\n\n**Where to look:**\n- runbooks, incident docs, service catalog files (for example `catalog-info.yaml`)\n- admin or ops endpoints and CLIs for stopping runs\n- credential issuing code (per-instance vs shared)\n- CODEOWNERS and deployment records\n\n**Needs runtime evidence:** Code can't show an operator can do the lookup in practice or that containment hits only the target. Needs the A05 drill: start from one action or trace ID, find manifest, owners, and instance, contain it, and confirm unrelated runs keep access.\n\n**N/A when:** never\n\n## A06 · G3 · Obs-T3 — Parent and prompt provenance\n\n**Lead:** Agent team. **Shares:** Data and privacy, Security.\n\n### Requirement\n\nFor every sub-agent, record its own type and instance IDs, a link to its parent, trace links, and the prompt the parent gave it. Trace a nested action back to that prompt. Protect sensitive prompt text with restricted storage and a clear redaction policy. Standard GenAI instrumentation records prompts only when you turn that on, so confirm it is on for this path.\n\n### Verification recipe\n\nStart a child and a grandchild agent, each with a test prompt you can tell apart. From the grandchild's tool action, trace the chain back to the root. Using the approved audit role, pull each prompt a parent passed down. A role without approval must not be able to read protected prompt text. Confirm prompt capture is turned on. Standard top-level agent attributes don't identify the direct parent instance.\n\n**Evidence to keep:** Nested trace, protected prompt references, access test.\n\n### Checks for likely_pass\n\nRate likely_pass only if every check below is true, with a file and line for each:\n\n- **A06.1** Each sub-agent gets its own type ID and instance ID, assigned by the harness when it is started.\n  - *How to verify:* 1) Find sub-agent spawn code (handoffs, subgraphs, subagents, A2A task creation). 2) Check the harness assigns the child a new `agent.instance.id` and a type ID at start. 3) Answer no if any spawn path reuses the parent's IDs, assigns none, or takes the IDs from the model's request.\n- **A06.2** Each sub-agent's records include its direct parent's instance ID and a trace link to the parent.\n  - *How to verify:* 1) Open sub-agent spawn code and the child's record writer. 2) Look for a parent instance field (for example `agent.parent.instance_id`) and trace propagation (span parent, span link, `traceparent` in queued payloads). 3) Answer no if any spawn path, including remote or queued, starts a fresh trace or omits the parent ID.\n- **A06.3** The prompt the parent passed to the child is captured on this path, with prompt capture switched on in config rather than left at the default off.\n  - *How to verify:* 1) Find where the parent's prompt to the child is recorded (for example `agent.parent.prompt`, or a hash plus stored text). 2) Check prompt content capture is turned on in config, for example a GenAI capture-content env var or SDK option. 3) Answer no if capture is left at its default off, or the setting falls back to false when unset.\n- **A06.4** Captured prompt text is stored with restricted access and a redaction policy, and only an approved audit role can read it.\n  - *How to verify:* 1) Find where captured prompt text is stored (bucket, table, log index). 2) Check its IAM or ACL limits reads to an approved audit role. 3) Check a redaction policy is defined and applied. 4) Answer no if prompts go to general logs the whole team can read, or no redaction exists. Answer unknown if storage access is set outside this repo.\n\n### Not covered here\n\nFile these problems under their owning item, not this one, unless this item's own requirement is also broken:\n\n- Sub-agent given powers beyond its task → **E08**\n- Children still running after the parent is stopped → **E09**\n- No limit on how many sub-agents a run can start → **B07**\n\n### Heuristics\n\n**Provable from code:** partly\n\n**Look for:**\n- Sub-agent spawn code assigns the child its own `agent.type.id` and `agent.instance.id` and records a parent link (for example `agent.parent.instance_id`).\n- The prompt the parent passes is recorded as `agent.parent.prompt`, either in full or as a hash reference with the text in restricted storage.\n- Trace context is passed into the child (span parent or span link), including for remote or queued sub-agents.\n- GenAI prompt capture is turned on for this path (for example a content-capture env var or SDK option) and a redaction policy is defined.\n- Restricted storage for prompt text with access limited to an approved audit role.\n\n**Red flags:**\n- Sub-agents reuse the parent's instance ID or have no IDs of their own.\n- Child agents start a fresh trace with no link to the parent span.\n- Prompt content capture is off by default and never turned on, so parent prompts are never recorded.\n- Full parent prompts with customer data are written to general-purpose logs anyone on the team can read.\n- Only top-level `gen_ai.agent.*` attributes are set, which don't identify the direct parent instance.\n\n**Where to look:**\n- sub-agent or handoff code (for example LangGraph subgraphs, OpenAI Agents SDK handoffs, Claude Agent SDK subagents, A2A task creation)\n- OpenTelemetry instrumentation settings and env vars for content capture\n- prompt storage and its access policy (bucket or table IAM)\n\n**Needs runtime evidence:** Needs the A06 recipe: a nested child and grandchild trace back to the root, pulling each parent prompt with the approved role, and a denied read with an unapproved role.\n\n**N/A when:** The agent never starts sub-agents (no child agents, handoffs, or delegated agent tasks).\n\n## A07 · G3 · C7/Obs-T1 — Every model in the loop\n\n**Lead:** Agent team. **Shares:** Model team, Security.\n\n### Requirement\n\nList all helper models, such as verifiers, judges, rerankers, and world models. Give each one an identity based on its available model version or digest, its prompt, and its configuration, and record which decisions it made. Show that a swapped, drifting, or failing checker counts as a failure of the control that depends on it.\n\n### Verification recipe\n\nList every model call in a typical workflow and match each to a model record. Swap a checker's version, and simulate a timeout or an invalid checker decision. The set control-failure response must happen and name the affected model.\n\n**Evidence to keep:** Call list, model provenance records, failure and swap results.\n\n### Checks for likely_pass\n\nRate likely_pass only if every check below is true, with a file and line for each:\n\n- **A07.1** A model inventory in code or config lists every helper model the agent calls (verifiers, judges, rerankers, embedding models, world models).\n  - *How to verify:* 1) Search for every model call: provider SDK clients, model name strings, embedding, rerank, moderation, and judge calls. 2) Compare that list with the model inventory. 3) Answer no if any helper model called in code is missing from the inventory, or no inventory exists.\n- **A07.2** Each helper model is identified by a pinned version or digest plus its prompt and configuration, not by an alias.\n  - *How to verify:* 1) For each helper model, find its model ID and follow env vars to the final value. 2) Check it is a pinned version or digest, and that its prompt and configuration are also recorded. 3) Answer no if any is an alias (`-latest`, undated name), falls back to one by default, or is hard-coded inline outside the inventory.\n- **A07.3** Each helper model's decisions are logged with that model's identity.\n  - *How to verify:* 1) Open each helper or checker call site. 2) Check its decision is logged with that model's ID and version. 3) Answer no if decisions are logged without model identity, or logged only on the allow or success branch.\n- **A07.4** A checker timeout, error, invalid output, or version mismatch makes the control that depends on it fail (block or escalate), not pass by default.\n  - *How to verify:* 1) Open each checker call site (judge, guardrail, moderation, verifier). 2) Read every `try/except`, `catch`, timeout, and parse-error branch. 3) Check each one blocks or escalates. 4) Answer no if any branch returns allow, skips the check, treats unparseable output as safe, or logs and continues. Also no if the returned model version is never compared with the expected one.\n\n### Not covered here\n\nFile these problems under their owning item, not this one, unless this item's own requirement is also broken:\n\n- Main agent model changing behind a fixed name unnoticed → **C05**\n- Second-model review not tested against evasive behavior → **R10**\n- Self-hosted model weights loaded without a signature or format check → **C05**\n\n### Heuristics\n\n**Provable from code:** partly\n\n**Look for:**\n- Every model call site is listed with its role (main, judge, verifier, reranker, embedding, router, fallback) and a model record.\n- Helper model IDs, prompts, and config are pinned in config and covered by the manifest, not hard-coded inline with aliases.\n- Checker decisions are logged with the checker's model ID and version.\n- Code treats a checker timeout, error, or invalid output as a failure of the dependent control (block or escalate), not as a pass.\n\n**Red flags:**\n- Judge or guardrail call wrapped in `try/except` that returns allow or skips the check on error or timeout.\n- Helper models (embedding, reranker, classifier) called with no recorded model version.\n- Checker model set to a moving alias (for example `-latest`) while the main model is pinned.\n- Unparseable checker output is defaulted to safe or approved.\n- Model routing or fallback silently switches to a different model with no record.\n\n**Where to look:**\n- all LLM client call sites (search for provider SDK clients and model name strings)\n- guardrail, judge, moderation, and evaluator modules\n- retrieval pipeline (embedding and reranker config)\n- model routing and fallback config\n\n**Needs runtime evidence:** Code can list call sites and failure handling. The A07 recipe proves the list is complete in a real workflow and that a swapped, timed-out, or invalid checker triggers the set failure response.\n\n**N/A when:** The agent uses no helper models at all (no verifiers, judges, rerankers, embedding models, or world models), and the model inventory shows that.\n","numLines":455,"startLine":1,"totalLines":455}}}
