{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01AUediStio8YhfbCupvGd2B","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t<!-- Generated by skills/gen_references.py from CHECKLIST.md, CHECKLIST-VERIFICATION.md, ROLES.md, and heuristics. Do not edit by hand. -->\n2\t\n3\t# E — Ecosystem\n4\t\n5\tUse this file to rate each item from the code and configuration. The requirement is the checklist text. The heuristics say what to look for. Ratings: likely_pass, gap, needs_evidence, or na_candidate. Never rate pass.\n6\t\n7\t## E01 · G2 · C1–C9 — Map shared responsibilities\n8\t\n9\t**Lead:** Governance. **Shares:** Agent team, Infrastructure, Vendors.\n10\t\n11\t### Requirement\n12\t\n13\tList the outside tools, MCP servers, peer agents, sub-agents, registries, identity services, gateways, stop channels, and audit services. For each control that applies, name the owner on the agent team and the owner on the shared platform or vendor side. Keep evidence for both halves. Using an upstream service doesn't make its controls N/A.\n14\t\n15\t### Verification recipe\n16\t\n17\tMap the real calls and services from the configuration and a test trace. For each control that applies, name the agent-team owner and the platform or vendor owner, and link their evidence. Assign an owner to every unowned boundary. Mark vendor evidence you can't get as a gap.\n18\t\n19\t**Evidence to keep:** Dependency map, responsibility table, evidence links.\n20\t\n21\t### Checks for likely_pass\n22\t\n23\tRate likely_pass only if every check below is true, with a file and line for each:\n24\t\n25\t- **E01.1** An inventory file lists the outside tools, MCP servers, peer agents, sub-agents, registries, identity services, gateways, stop channels, and audit services. *(A document can show this.)*\n26\t  - *How to verify:* 1) Find the inventory file. 2) Check each category the agent uses is listed: outside tools, MCP servers, peer agents, sub-agents, registries, identity services, gateways, stop channels, audit services. 3) Answer no if no inventory exists or a category the agent uses is missing.\n27\t- **E01.2** Each inventory entry names both an agent-team owner and a platform or vendor owner. *(A document can show this.)*\n28\t  - *How to verify:* 1) Open each inventory entry. 2) Check it names an agent-team owner and a platform or vendor owner. 3) Answer no if any entry is missing either owner.\n29\t- **E01.3** Every outside service the code or config calls appears in the inventory. *(A document can show this.)*\n30\t  - *How to verify:* 1) Search code and config for outside calls: HTTP clients and base URLs, SDK clients, `*.mcp.json` servers, queue and webhook endpoints, identity providers, telemetry exporters. 2) Compare each with the inventory. 3) Answer no if any called service is not listed.\n31\t\n32\t### Not covered here\n33\t\n34\tFile these problems under their owning item, not this one, unless this item's own requirement is also broken:\n35\t\n36\t- A tool call that skips the MCP gateway or reaches a tool server directly → **E06**\n37\t- A vendor service dropping agent or tenant identity from its records → **E10**\n38\t- Tool or MCP server approved without vetting or pinning → **E02**\n39\t\n40\t### Heuristics\n41\t\n42\t**Provable from code:** partly\n43\t\n44\t**Look for:**\n45\t- A dependency map listing outside tools, MCP servers, peer agents, sub-agents, registries, identity services, gateways, stop channels, and audit services.\n46\t- A responsibility table naming the agent-team owner and the platform or vendor owner for each applicable control, with evidence links for both.\n47\t- Owner records in machine-readable form (service catalog, `.brace/deployment.yaml` roles, CODEOWNERS).\n48\t\n49\t**Red flags:**\n50\t- Outside services in config (MCP servers, API base URLs, webhooks) that do not appear in any dependency or ownership list.\n51\t- Controls marked N/A because an upstream vendor provides the service.\n52\t- Vendor evidence assumed rather than linked, with no gap recorded.\n53\t\n54\t**Where to look:**\n55\t- *.mcp.json, agent config, env vars with service URLs\n56\t- service catalog files, architecture docs, `.brace/` records\n57\t- IaC for gateways, identity providers, and log sinks\n58\t\n59\t**Needs runtime evidence:** Code shows declared dependencies; the E01 recipe compares them with a test trace of real calls and links evidence for each owner.\n60\t\n61\t**N/A when:** never\n62\t\n63\t## E02 · G2 · C3/C5 — Vet and pin dependencies\n64\t\n65\t**Lead:** Security. **Shares:** Agent team, Model team, Vendors.\n66\t\n67\t### Requirement\n68\t\n69\tBefore approving a tool or server, review its source code or available provenance, publisher, permissions, update path, and known vulnerabilities. Include model files, adapters, and datasets. Pin versions or digests, and verify signatures where they exist. A registry's namespace check shows who controls a publisher account, not that the code is safe. Record the limits of remote services and unsigned artifacts, and the extra checks you use instead.\n70\t\n71\t### Verification recipe\n72\t\n73\tFor each approved dependency, including model files, adapters, and datasets, find the publisher, artifact and version, permissions, and update source. Verify signatures where they exist. Scan the pinned list for known vulnerabilities, and judge the findings under the team's release policy. Record missing provenance and how you reduce the risk.\n74\t\n75\t**Evidence to keep:** Dependency list, signature and scan results, review and exception records.\n76\t\n77\t### Checks for likely_pass\n78\t\n79\tRate likely_pass only if every check below is true, with a file and line for each:\n80\t\n81\t- **E02.1** Each tool, MCP server, package, model file, adapter, and dataset is pinned by exact version or digest, with no floating tags, ranges, or 'latest'.\n82\t  - *How to verify:* 1) Open lockfiles, `*.mcp.json`, Dockerfiles, and model or dataset download code. 2) Check each tool, MCP server, package, model file, adapter, and dataset uses an exact version or digest. 3) Answer no if any uses a range, `latest`, a branch, `npx` or `uvx` without a version, or an unpinned download URL.\n83\t- **E02.2** Signature checks run in the build or load path where the publisher provides signatures, and a failed check stops the install or load.\n84\t  - *How to verify:* 1) Find install and load steps in CI and code. 2) Check signature checks (`cosign verify`, `npm audit signatures`, sigstore, GPG) run where the publisher signs. 3) Answer no if a check is missing, its failure is ignored (`|| true`, `continue-on-error`, `try/except` that continues), or a second install path skips it.\n85\t- **E02.3** A vulnerability scan of the pinned dependency list runs in CI and gates on the team's release policy.\n86\t  - *How to verify:* 1) Open CI workflows. 2) Find a vulnerability scan (`pip-audit`, `npm audit`, `osv-scanner`, `trivy`, `grype`) of the pinned dependency list. 3) Check it fails the build per the release policy. 4) Answer no if no scan exists, it is set not to fail (`--exit-zero`, `|| true`, `continue-on-error`), or it scans a different file than the lockfile actually used.\n87\t- **E02.4** Each approved dependency's record names its publisher, permissions, and update source, and remote services or unsigned artifacts have a recorded exception. *(A document can show this.)*\n88\t  - *How to verify:* 1) Find the dependency approval records. 2) Check each names publisher, permissions, and update source. 3) Check each remote service and unsigned artifact has a recorded exception. 4) Answer no if any approved dependency lacks these fields or the needed exception.\n89\t\n90\t### Not covered here\n91\t\n92\tFile these problems under their owning item, not this one, unless this item's own requirement is also broken:\n93\t\n94\t- A tool's description or schema changing after it was approved → **E03**\n95\t- Hidden instructions inside a tool's metadata → **E04**\n96\t- Lookalike or duplicate tool names across servers → **E05**\n97\t- Container image not pinned by digest or signature-checked → **B10**\n98\t\n99\t### Heuristics\n100\t\n101\t**Provable from code:** partly\n102\t\n103\t**Look for:**\n104\t- MCP servers, packages, and images pinned to exact versions or digests (`pkg@1.4.2`, `image@sha256:...`).\n105\t- Dependency scanning in CI (for example OSV-Scanner, Trivy, Grype, Dependabot) with a policy for findings.\n106\t- Signature or provenance checks (Sigstore/cosign, SLSA attestations, npm provenance) where available.\n107\t- Model files, adapters, and datasets included in the pinned list with revisions or digests.\n108\t- Review records for each approved tool or server: publisher, permissions, update source.\n109\t\n110\t**Red flags:**\n111\t- MCP server launched with `npx -y package` or `uvx package` without a pinned version.\n112\t- Remote MCP server URLs with no record of publisher review or limits.\n113\t- Docker images pulled by `:latest` for tools or servers.\n114\t- Hub models or datasets loaded by name with no revision pin.\n115\t- Registry namespace verification treated as proof the code is safe.\n116\t\n117\t**Where to look:**\n118\t- *.mcp.json, `claude_desktop_config.json`, agent tool registries\n119\t- lockfiles, Dockerfiles, compose and k8s manifests\n120\t- CI scanning and signing steps\n121\t- model and dataset download code\n122\t\n123\t**Needs runtime evidence:** Signature verification results and scan outputs come from the E02 run; code shows only whether pins and checks are configured.\n124\t\n125\t**N/A when:** never\n126\t\n127\t## E03 · G2 · C5 — Re-check tools on load\n128\t\n129\t**Lead:** Infrastructure. **Shares:** Security, Vendors.\n130\t\n131\t### Requirement\n132\t\n133\tTake a fingerprint of each approved tool's description and schema. Compare it every time the tool loads, the cache refreshes, or the server says its tool list changed. Don't use a cached definition when the re-check fails. Test a changed description, schema, or implementation version, and block unexpected changes until they are reviewed. A fingerprint of the description and schema catches changes to that metadata, not hidden changes to remote code. Pin or attest the implementation where you can see it. For remote implementations, record what you can't inspect and when you'll review them again. Track vulnerability notices, and find which deployments use an affected dependency.\n134\t\n135\t### Verification recipe\n136\t\n137\tLoad an approved tool. Then, on a test server, change its description, schema, or implementation version, and refresh. Unexpected changes must block use until reviewed. That includes when a cached definition would otherwise be used after a failed re-check. Confirm that a metadata hash doesn't claim to catch changed remote code when the metadata stays the same. Record implementation pinning and visibility separately. Query which deployments use the affected dependency.\n138\t\n139\t**Evidence to keep:** Approved and new fingerprints, load denials, affected-deployment query.\n140\t\n141\t### Checks for likely_pass\n142\t\n143\tRate likely_pass only if every check below is true, with a file and line for each:\n144\t\n145\t- **E03.1** A fingerprint of each approved tool's description and schema is stored.\n146\t  - *How to verify:* 1) Find where tool definitions are loaded (MCP `tools/list`, tool registry). 2) Look for a stored hash of each approved tool's description and schema. 3) Answer no if no fingerprint is stored, or it covers only the name and not the description and schema.\n147\t- **E03.2** The fingerprint is re-checked on every tool load, cache refresh, and list-changed notice from the server.\n148\t  - *How to verify:* 1) List every path that loads tool definitions: startup, cache refresh, reconnect, and the `notifications/tools/list_changed` handler. 2) Check each recomputes the fingerprint and compares it with the stored one. 3) Answer no if any path skips the compare, or both sides of the compare are computed from the new definition.\n149\t- **E03.3** When the re-check fails, the tool is blocked and the cached definition is not used.\n150\t  - *How to verify:* 1) Open the fingerprint mismatch branch. 2) Check it blocks the tool and does not fall back to the cached definition. 3) Answer no if it only logs, uses the cached or new definition anyway, or an error during the check lets the tool load.\n151\t- **E03.4** The implementation version is pinned or attested where it can be seen, and recorded separately from the metadata fingerprint.\n152\t  - *How to verify:* 1) Check how each tool server implementation is referenced: package version, image digest, or attestation. 2) Check that version is recorded in its own field, apart from the metadata fingerprint. 3) Answer no if a visible implementation is unpinned, or only the metadata hash is recorded.\n153\t\n154\t### Not covered here\n155\t\n156\tFile these problems under their owning item, not this one, unless this item's own requirement is also broken:\n157\t\n158\t- Hidden instructions in a tool's metadata when first approved → **E04**\n159\t- Tool approved without reviewing its publisher and permissions → **E02**\n160\t- A new server quietly swapping its tool in for an approved one → **E05**\n161\t- Cached tool list shared across authorization contexts → **E06**\n162\t\n163\t### Heuristics\n164\t\n165\t**Provable from code:** yes\n166\t\n167\t**Look for:**\n168\t- Stored fingerprints (hashes) of each approved tool's description and schema, compared on every load, cache refresh, and `notifications/tools/list_changed`.\n169\t- Load is blocked when the fingerprint doesn't match or the re-check fails; no fallback to a cached definition.\n170\t- Implementation version pinned or attested separately from the metadata fingerprint where visible.\n171\t- A query or inventory showing which deployments use each tool or server version.\n172\t\n173\t**Red flags:**\n174\t- Tool lists fetched from MCP servers and passed to the model with no fingerprint comparison.\n175\t- `list_changed` notifications handled by silently reloading tools.\n176\t- On re-check error, the code falls back to the cached tool definition.\n177\t- Docs or code claim a metadata hash detects changes to remote server code.\n178\t\n179\t**Where to look:**\n180\t- MCP client code and tool registry or loader\n181\t- gateway tool-catalog config\n182\t- approved tool lists or lock files for tools\n183\t\n184\t**Needs runtime evidence:** Code usually shows whether pinning and re-checks exist; the E03 recipe confirms that a changed description, schema, or version on a test server blocks use, including after a failed re-check.\n185\t\n186\t**N/A when:** The agent loads no external tools or MCP servers; all tools are built in and fixed in the release.\n187\t\n188\t## E04 · G2 · C5 — Inspect tool metadata\n189\t\n190\t**Lead:** Security. **Shares:** Infrastructure.\n191\t\n192\t### Requirement\n193\t\n194\tBefore the model sees them, scan server instructions, tool names, titles, descriptions, input and output schemas, annotations, and prompt and resource templates. Look for hidden instructions, references to other servers' tools, invisible or control characters, role overrides, encoded payloads, and places data could be sent. Limit lengths, and clean or reject anything suspicious. Check that a scanner error doesn't quietly let unchecked metadata through.\n195\t\n196\t  Why this is hard: Hidden instructions can be written in plain language, with no strange encoding or control characters. Scanners can also flag honest descriptions. A clean scan doesn't prove a tool is trustworthy.\n197\t\n198\t  If you can't fully do it: Use a small approved tool catalog, with reviewed, versioned descriptions and schemas. Block unexpected changes. Keep rule-based limits and known-pattern checks even without advanced scanning. Limit each tool's powers regardless of what its metadata says. Turn a tool off when the required inspection isn't available. Record what the scanner covers and misses. Manual review and pattern scans are useful layers. They don't prove every poisoned tool is caught.\n199\t\n200\t  Tradeoffs you can accept: A smaller catalog, review delays, and some honest metadata getting rejected. Limited scanning for attacks hidden in meaning can get G2 acceptance, as long as tool powers are limited separately. Known-pattern tests don't prove you can catch every poisoned tool.\n201\t\n202\t### Verification recipe\n203\t\n204\tAdd test fixtures with hidden or encoded instructions, and overlong descriptions, to tool metadata. Put them in the server instructions field, annotations, and prompt and resource templates too. Add a description that tells the model how to use another server's tool. Confirm the set rejection or cleaning happens before the model sees them. Turn off the scanner. Unchecked metadata must not get through.\n205\t\n206\t**Evidence to keep:** Metadata fixtures, scan results, check of what the model saw.\n207\t\n208\t### Checks for likely_pass\n209\t\n210\tRate likely_pass only if every check below is true, with a file and line for each:\n211\t\n212\t- **E04.1** A scanner runs on server instructions, tool names, titles, descriptions, input and output schemas, annotations, and prompt and resource templates before the model sees them.\n213\t  - *How to verify:* 1) Find where server instructions and tool metadata reach the model's context. 2) Check a scanner runs before that on every field: server instructions, names, titles, descriptions, input and output schemas, annotations, prompt and resource templates. 3) Answer no if any field, or a second load path, skips the scanner.\n214\t- **E04.2** The scanner checks for hidden instructions, references to other servers' tools, invisible or control characters, role overrides, encoded payloads, and data-sending targets, and enforces length limits.\n215\t  - *How to verify:* 1) Open the scanner rules. 2) Check for each: hidden instructions, references to other servers' tools, invisible or control characters (zero-width, bidi), role overrides, encoded payloads (base64), data-sending targets (URLs, emails), and length limits. 3) Answer no if any one is missing.\n216\t- **E04.3** Metadata the scanner flags is cleaned or rejected, not just logged.\n217\t  - *How to verify:* 1) Open the scanner's result handling. 2) Check flagged metadata is cleaned, or the tool rejected, before the model sees it. 3) Answer no if flagged items are only logged or warned about and passed through.\n218\t- **E04.4** A scanner error or outage blocks the metadata instead of letting it through unchecked.\n219\t  - *How to verify:* 1) Read every `try/except`, `catch`, and timeout around the scanner call. 2) Check an error blocks the metadata. 3) Answer no if any error branch passes metadata through, returns clean, or skips the scan, or a config flag disables scanning by default.\n220\t\n221\t### Not covered here\n222\t\n223\tFile these problems under their owning item, not this one, unless this item's own requirement is also broken:\n224\t\n225\t- A tool's description or schema changing after it was approved → **E03**\n226\t- Hidden instructions in tool call results → **R01**\n227\t- Lookalike or duplicate tool names across servers → **E05**\n228\t- Allow decisions based on server hints such as read-only → **B01**\n229\t\n230\t### Heuristics\n231\t\n232\t**Provable from code:** yes\n233\t\n234\t**Look for:**\n235\t- A scanner or filter that runs on server instructions, tool names, titles, descriptions, schemas, annotations, and prompt and resource templates before they reach the model.\n236\t- Checks for hidden or control characters, encoded payloads, role overrides, references to other servers' tools, and length limits.\n237\t- Scanner errors cause rejection (fail closed), not pass-through.\n238\t- Where scanning is limited, a small approved catalog with reviewed, versioned metadata.\n239\t\n240\t**Red flags:**\n241\t- Tool descriptions and server `instructions` inserted into the system prompt unchanged.\n242\t- Only descriptions are scanned; schemas, annotations, or templates skip the scanner.\n243\t- Scanner wrapped in `try/except` that logs and continues on error.\n244\t- No length limits on tool metadata.\n245\t\n246\t**Where to look:**\n247\t- MCP client or gateway code that builds the tool list for the model\n248\t- metadata scanning or sanitizing modules\n249\t- gateway policy config\n250\t\n251\t**Needs runtime evidence:** The E04 recipe with poisoned metadata fixtures confirms what the model actually saw, including with the scanner turned off.\n252\t\n253\t**N/A when:** The agent loads no external tools or MCP servers; all tool metadata is written in-house and fixed in the release.\n254\t\n255\t## E05 · G2 · C2/C5 — Verified, namespaced tools\n256\t\n257\t**Lead:** Infrastructure. **Shares:** Security.\n258\t\n259\t### Requirement\n260\t\n261\tIdentify each tool by a verified server identity plus the tool name. Base server identity on the verified endpoint, credential, or registry namespace. A server's self-reported name is neither unique nor trustworthy. Flag lookalike and duplicate names across servers. Test that a new server can't quietly swap its tool in for an approved one.\n262\t\n263\t### Verification recipe\n264\t\n265\tRegister a second test server with a duplicate or lookalike tool name. The registry must flag the conflict, and calls must go to the approved server identity. A matching bare name must not change the target. Register a server whose self-reported name copies an approved server's name. It must not inherit that server's approval.\n266\t\n267\t**Evidence to keep:** Registration warning, resolved tool identity, destination log.\n268\t\n269\t### Checks for likely_pass\n270\t\n271\tRate likely_pass only if every check below is true, with a file and line for each:\n272\t\n273\t- **E05.1** Tools are keyed by verified server identity (endpoint, credential, or registry namespace) plus tool name, never by bare tool name or a server's self-reported name.\n274\t  - *How to verify:* 1) Find the tool registry or lookup map. 2) Check its key is a verified endpoint, credential, or registry namespace plus tool name. 3) Answer no if tools are keyed by bare tool name or the server's self-reported name (`serverInfo.name`), or a later server overwrites an earlier entry with the same tool name.\n275\t- **E05.2** The registry flags duplicate and lookalike tool names across servers.\n276\t  - *How to verify:* 1) Open tool registration code. 2) Look for a check that compares new tool names across servers for duplicates and lookalikes (case, homoglyphs, separators). 3) Answer no if registration silently overwrites or accepts duplicates.\n277\t- **E05.3** Approval is bound to the verified server identity, so a new server with a matching tool name or self-reported name does not inherit approval or replace the approved tool.\n278\t  - *How to verify:* 1) Find where tool approval is stored and checked. 2) Check approval is keyed by verified server identity, not tool name or self-reported server name. 3) Answer no if a new server with a matching tool name or server name would count as approved or replace the approved tool.\n279\t\n280\t### Not covered here\n281\t\n282\tFile these problems under their owning item, not this one, unless this item's own requirement is also broken:\n283\t\n284\t- Hidden instructions inside a tool's metadata → **E04**\n285\t- A tool's description or schema changing after it was approved → **E03**\n286\t- Peer agent identity not authenticated on each message → **E07**\n287\t- Gateway not authenticating callers or authorizing each action → **E06**\n288\t\n289\t### Heuristics\n290\t\n291\t**Provable from code:** yes\n292\t\n293\t**Look for:**\n294\t- Tools keyed by verified server identity plus tool name (for example a namespaced `server-id/tool`), where server identity comes from the endpoint, credential, or registry namespace.\n295\t- Registration code that flags duplicate and lookalike tool names across servers.\n296\t- Approvals bound to verified server identity, not to the server's self-reported name.\n297\t\n298\t**Red flags:**\n299\t- Tools merged into one flat dict by bare name, so a later server overwrites an earlier one.\n300\t- Server identity taken from the `serverInfo.name` the server reports about itself.\n301\t- No warning when two servers expose the same or similar tool name.\n302\t\n303\t**Where to look:**\n304\t- tool registry and MCP client multiplexing code\n305\t- gateway routing config\n306\t- *.mcp.json server entries\n307\t\n308\t**Needs runtime evidence:** The E05 recipe registers a duplicate or lookalike server and confirms calls still go to the approved server identity.\n309\t\n310\t**N/A when:** The agent loads no external tools or MCP servers.\n311\t\n312\t## E06 · G2 · C1/C2/C4/C5/C9 — Enforced gateway boundary\n313\t\n314\t**Lead:** Infrastructure. **Shares:** Security, Agent team, Vendors.\n315\t\n316\t### Requirement\n317\t\n318\tSend MCP calls through a gateway, or a similar enforcement layer, that the agent can't get around. Authenticate each caller (prove who they are), authorize each action, and validate calls and responses. Enforce rate limits and log every decision. For HTTP and OAuth, check the token's issuer, intended audience, expiry, and scopes. Use properly authorized credentials for the services behind the gateway. Never pass an unchecked token through. For local tools that talk over standard input/output (stdio), control process launch, file access, credentials, and networking in the host or sandbox. An HTTP gateway alone doesn't cover these. Test going around the gateway straight to the server, bad credentials, broken responses, and outages of the policy engine or scanner. MCP copies some request details into headers. If routing or policy uses those headers, reject any request whose headers don't match its body, before checking policy. Store any cached tool list or response separately for each authorization context. When the gateway is itself an OAuth client, check the issuer (`iss`, RFC 9207). Also protect discovery URLs against SSRF (tricking a server into calling places it shouldn't). Required checks must fail closed: if a required check can't run, block the action.\n319\t\n320\t### Verification recipe\n321\t\n322\tMake allowed calls through the gateway. Then try going around it straight to the server, tokens for the wrong audience or that have expired, broken responses, and a scanner or policy outage. Send a `tools/call` whose `Mcp-Name` header names a different tool than the body. Request a cached tool list using a second caller's token. Test that downstream credentials are separate, that the issuer (`iss`) is checked where the gateway is an OAuth client, and that local stdio launches are restricted, where these apply. Unapproved and unchecked traffic must fail closed. Allowed traffic must still be logged, and rate limits must work.\n323\t\n324\t**Evidence to keep:** Route and policy settings, request and response fixtures, gateway decisions.\n325\t\n326\t### Checks for likely_pass\n327\t\n328\tRate likely_pass only if every check below is true, with a file and line for each:\n329\t\n330\t- **E06.1** The agent can reach MCP servers only through the gateway: network policy blocks direct routes, the agent holds no server credentials, and any local stdio servers are launched by the host or sandbox with limited files, credentials, and network.\n331\t  - *How to verify:* 1) Find how the agent reaches MCP servers: URLs in `*.mcp.json` and client config. 2) Check all go to the gateway, and NetworkPolicy or egress rules block direct routes. 3) Check the agent holds no server credentials and stdio servers run in a restricted sandbox. 4) Answer no if any tool call bypasses the gateway. Answer unknown if network policy lives outside this repo.\n332\t- **E06.2** The gateway checks each token's issuer, audience, expiry, and scopes, and uses its own separately authorized credentials downstream instead of passing the caller's token through.\n333\t  - *How to verify:* 1) Open the gateway's token validation. 2) Check issuer, audience, expiry, and scopes are each verified. 3) Check downstream calls use the gateway's own credentials. 4) Answer no if any claim is unchecked, signature verification is disabled, or the caller's `Authorization` header is forwarded downstream.\n334\t- **E06.3** The gateway authorizes each action, validates requests and responses, enforces rate limits, and logs every decision.\n335\t  - *How to verify:* 1) Open the gateway's request handling. 2) Check per-action authorization, request and response validation, rate limits, and a log entry for every decision. 3) Answer no if any is missing, authorization happens only once per connection, or denials are not logged.\n336\t- **E06.4** If a required check (policy engine, scanner, token check) errors or times out, the call is blocked.\n337\t  - *How to verify:* 1) Read every `try/except`, `catch`, and timeout around the policy engine, scanner, and token check. 2) Check each error blocks the call. 3) Answer no if any error branch allows the call or logs and continues, or a config flag defaults to fail-open.\n338\t- **E06.5** Requests whose routing headers (such as Mcp-Name) don't match the body are rejected before policy, and cached tool lists and responses are stored per authorization context.\n339\t  - *How to verify:* 1) Find where routing or policy reads headers such as `Mcp-Name`. 2) Check the header is compared with the body before policy runs, and mismatches are rejected. 3) Check tool-list and response caches are keyed by authorization context. 4) Answer no if headers are trusted without the compare, or a cache is shared across callers.\n340\t- **E06.6** If the gateway is itself an OAuth client, it checks the authorization server's issuer (iss, RFC 9207) and blocks private and metadata addresses in discovery URLs. Answer yes with the code line, or yes citing the config that shows it isn't an OAuth client.\n341\t  - *How to verify:* 1) Check whether the gateway acts as an OAuth client (gets tokens, follows discovery or metadata URLs). 2) If so, check it verifies the `iss` parameter (RFC 9207) and blocks private, loopback, and metadata addresses (169.254.169.254) in discovery URLs. 3) Answer no if either is missing. If it is not an OAuth client, answer yes citing the config that shows that.\n342\t\n343\t### Not covered here\n344\t\n345\tFile these problems under their owning item, not this one, unless this item's own requirement is also broken:\n346\t\n347\t- Tool inventory missing a task-based reason for each tool → **B01**\n348\t- Agent credential granting wildcard or admin permissions → **B02**\n349\t- Destructive actions running without a higher-authority approval → **B05**\n350\t- Hidden instructions inside a tool's metadata → **E04**\n351\t- Outbound traffic to unapproved internet destinations → **B09**\n352\t\n353\t### Heuristics\n354\t\n355\t**Provable from code:** partly\n356\t\n357\t**Look for:**\n358\t- All MCP traffic routed through a gateway or enforcement layer; network policy blocks direct paths from the agent to servers.\n359\t- Gateway checks token issuer, audience, expiry, and scopes; uses separate downstream credentials (token exchange) rather than passing the caller's token through.\n360\t- Per-call authorization, request and response validation, rate limits, and decision logging.\n361\t- Rejection of requests whose MCP headers (for example `Mcp-Name`) don't match the body, before policy runs; tool-list caches keyed by authorization context.\n362\t- `iss` checked (RFC 9207) where the gateway is an OAuth client; SSRF protection on discovery URLs; stdio servers launched in a sandbox with limited files, credentials, and network.\n363\t- Fail-closed settings when the policy engine or scanner is down.\n364\t\n365\t**Red flags:**\n366\t- Agent config lists MCP server URLs directly, bypassing the gateway, with egress open.\n367\t- Gateway forwards the incoming bearer token unchanged to downstream servers.\n368\t- Policy or scanner outage handled with fail-open (`on_error: allow`, catch and continue).\n369\t- Cached tool lists shared across callers or tenants.\n370\t- stdio servers launched on the host with full environment variables and network access.\n371\t\n372\t**Where to look:**\n373\t- gateway config (route, auth, policy, rate-limit rules)\n374\t- *.mcp.json and agent MCP client settings\n375\t- k8s NetworkPolicy, egress proxy, firewall rules\n376\t- OAuth client and token-exchange config; sandbox or container settings for stdio servers\n377\t\n378\t**Needs runtime evidence:** Needs the E06 tests: bypass attempts, wrong-audience and expired tokens, broken responses, header-body mismatch, cross-caller cache reads, and policy or scanner outages must all fail closed.\n379\t\n380\t**N/A when:** The agent uses no MCP servers or similar external tool servers.\n381\t\n382\t## E07 · G2 · C2/C5 — Untrusted peer messages\n383\t\n384\t**Lead:** Agent team. **Shares:** Security.\n385\t\n386\t### Requirement\n387\t\n388\tAuthenticate the sending agent (prove its identity) on every message, even validly signed ones. Validate message types and schemas, and check what that peer is allowed to ask for. Where a replay could repeat an action, check freshness and track nonces or request IDs. Checking the sender alone doesn't stop replays. Test faked identity, replayed requests, requests across tenants, and instructions passed along by a compromised peer. Verify signed agent cards where they exist. A card says who an agent is, not what it may do. Check callback and webhook URLs against SSRF. A valid sender doesn't make a message's content trusted.\n389\t\n390\t### Verification recipe\n391\t\n392\tSend valid, broken, fake-sender, replayed, and cross-tenant peer requests. Reused action requests must not repeat side effects without approval. Authenticate the sender of every request, even validly signed ones, and check its scope. Deny requests that aren't allowed, and keep hostile content as untrusted data. Include a schema-valid injected instruction, a tampered signed agent card, and a callback URL that points at a private or metadata address.\n393\t\n394\t**Evidence to keep:** Peer policy, message fixtures, authorization and validation logs.\n395\t\n396\t### Checks for likely_pass\n397\t\n398\tRate likely_pass only if every check below is true, with a file and line for each:\n399\t\n400\t- **E07.1** Every peer message is authenticated to a specific sending agent, even when the message is validly signed.\n401\t  - *How to verify:* 1) Find the handler for incoming peer messages (A2A server, peer endpoint). 2) Check each message is authenticated to a specific sending agent (mTLS identity, verified token subject). 3) Answer no if any route accepts unauthenticated messages, trusts a self-declared sender field, or accepts any valid signature without mapping it to a known agent.\n402\t- **E07.2** Message types and schemas are validated, and each request is checked against what that peer is allowed to ask for, including tenant.\n403\t  - *How to verify:* 1) Open the peer message handler. 2) Check message type and schema validation. 3) Check a per-peer allowlist of request types and tenants, keyed on the authenticated sender. 4) Answer no if schemas are unchecked, any authenticated peer can ask for anything, or tenant is taken from the message body.\n404\t- **E07.3** Action requests carry a nonce or request ID and a freshness check, and replays are rejected or do not repeat side effects.\n405\t  - *How to verify:* 1) Find action request handling. 2) Check for a nonce or request ID plus a freshness (timestamp) check, with seen IDs stored. 3) Answer no if no replay check exists, the nonce is never stored or compared, or a repeated ID repeats side effects.\n406\t- **E07.4** Peer message content is handled as untrusted data and is not placed into system or instruction prompts.\n407\t  - *How to verify:* 1) Trace peer message content to the prompt builder. 2) Check it goes into user or tool-result content as untrusted data. 3) Answer no if any path places it into the system prompt or an instruction template.\n408\t- **E07.5** Callback and webhook URLs are checked against SSRF, with private and metadata addresses blocked.\n409\t  - *How to verify:* 1) Find code that calls callback or webhook URLs from peers. 2) Check URL validation blocks private, loopback, link-local, and metadata addresses (169.254.169.254). 3) Answer no if any callback is fetched without the check, or only the hostname text is checked and not the resolved address.\n410\t\n411\t### Not covered here\n412\t\n413\tFile these problems under their owning item, not this one, unless this item's own requirement is also broken:\n414\t\n415\t- Tool server identified only by its self-reported name → **E05**\n416\t- Missing schema or size checks on API responses → **R01**\n417\t- Peers or children still running after a stop → **E09**\n418\t- Run can't be rebuilt across gateway, tool services, and delegated workers → **E10**\n419\t\n420\t### Heuristics\n421\t\n422\t**Provable from code:** partly\n423\t\n424\t**Look for:**\n425\t- Every peer message authenticated (mTLS, signed requests, verified tokens) and the sender's scope checked.\n426\t- Message type and schema validation for incoming A2A or peer requests.\n427\t- Nonce, request ID, or timestamp tracking to stop replays where an action could repeat.\n428\t- Signed agent cards verified, and card claims not used as authorization.\n429\t- Callback and webhook URLs checked against private, link-local, and metadata addresses (SSRF).\n430\t- Peer message content passed to the model as untrusted data.\n431\t\n432\t**Red flags:**\n433\t- Peer requests accepted based on a valid signature alone, with no per-peer authorization.\n434\t- Agent card skills or capabilities treated as permission grants.\n435\t- Callback URLs fetched without SSRF checks (for example to `169.254.169.254`).\n436\t- Retried peer requests re-run side effects with no idempotency key.\n437\t- Peer message text inserted into the system prompt as instructions.\n438\t\n439\t**Where to look:**\n440\t- A2A server handlers, agent card files (`/.well-known/agent-card.json`)\n441\t- peer auth middleware\n442\t- webhook and callback handlers\n443\t- idempotency and replay protection code\n444\t\n445\t**Needs runtime evidence:** Needs the E07 fixtures: fake-sender, replayed, cross-tenant, tampered-card, and private-callback requests must be denied.\n446\t\n447\t**N/A when:** The agent receives no messages from other agents (no A2A server, peer endpoint, or agent-to-agent channel).\n448\t\n449\t## E08 · G1 · C2/C4 — Bounded delegated authority\n450\t\n451\t**Lead:** Agent team. **Shares:** Infrastructure, Security.\n452\t\n453\t### Requirement\n454\t\n455\tGive sub-agents only the powers approved for their task. Enforce each child's role and any explicit approval from a higher authority. A parent must not gain a power it was denied just by asking a more powerful peer to act. Narrow credentials at each hop, for example with token exchange or transaction tokens. Authorize based on the original subject (the user or system whose authority is used) and the current actor. Earlier actors in a delegation chain are for the record only, not a reason to grant access. Test that escalation attempt.\n456\t\n457\t  Why this is hard: A powerful peer may treat a low-privilege agent's request as a normal task. Knowing who sent the last message doesn't prove what the original requester was allowed to do. Delegation limits can also get lost across several hops.\n458\t\n459\t  If you can't fully do it: Carry the verified original identity, tenant, and allowed scope all the way to where the action runs. If a peer can't enforce those limits, delegate only to peers with no more relevant authority. Or use a narrow task credential, or require separate approval for that exact privileged action. If none of these can be enforced, turn off that delegation path. Trusting a peer's prompt does not pass this blocking check.\n460\t\n461\t  Tradeoffs you can accept: Less flexible delegation, narrower child roles, or separate approval for each action. These keep the authorization boundary intact. Quiet privilege gains can never pass this G1 check. Turn off the path if you can't enforce the boundary.\n462\t\n463\t### Verification recipe\n464\t\n465\tGive parent A a read-only task, and ask it to hand a forbidden write to a more powerful peer. The handed-off write must be denied without its own valid approval. A legitimate handed-off task that is clearly approved must still work. Present the parent's token to the child's service. It must be rejected.\n466\t\n467\t**Evidence to keep:** Parent and child grants, delegation decisions, destination state.\n468\t\n469\t### Checks for likely_pass\n470\t\n471\tRate likely_pass only if every check below is true, with a file and line for each:\n472\t\n473\t- **E08.1** Each child gets a credential narrowed to its approved task (for example by token exchange or transaction tokens), never the parent's full credential.\n474\t  - *How to verify:* 1) Find where a child agent gets its credential. 2) Check it is a narrowed token for the task (token exchange, transaction token, scoped-down role). 3) Answer no if any spawn path passes the parent's token, API key, or full role, or the child inherits the parent's environment credentials.\n475\t- **E08.2** Authorization at the action uses the original subject and the current actor, and earlier actors in the delegation chain never add access.\n476\t  - *How to verify:* 1) Open authorization code for delegated actions. 2) Check it decides on the original subject and the current actor. 3) Answer no if it grants access because of any earlier actor in the chain (nested `act` claims, parent identity) or a union of chain permissions.\n477\t- **E08.3** An action the parent was denied is also denied when routed through a more powerful peer, unless it carries its own explicit approval.\n478\t  - *How to verify:* 1) Find how a parent asks a peer or child to act. 2) Check the request carries the parent's limits and the peer re-checks them, or requires its own explicit approval. 3) Answer no if a peer acts with its own wider permissions on a parent's request without that check.\n479\t- **E08.4** Downstream services reject the parent's token when the child presents it (audience-bound tokens).\n480\t  - *How to verify:* 1) Find how child tokens are issued and how downstream services validate tokens. 2) Check tokens are audience-bound and services verify `aud`. 3) Answer no if audience is unset, a wildcard, or not checked. Answer unknown if downstream services are outside this repo.\n481\t\n482\t### Not covered here\n483\t\n484\tFile these problems under their owning item, not this one, unless this item's own requirement is also broken:\n485\t\n486\t- Approvals that can be replayed or aren't tied to exact arguments → **B06**\n487\t- Prompt a parent gave a sub-agent not recorded → **A06**\n488\t- Children still running after the parent is stopped → **E09**\n489\t- Replayed or faked peer-agent messages → **E07**\n490\t- No limit on how many sub-agents a run can start → **B07**\n491\t\n492\t### Heuristics\n493\t\n494\t**Provable from code:** partly\n495\t\n496\t**Look for:**\n497\t- Each sub-agent gets its own narrowed role or credential (token exchange per RFC 8693, transaction tokens, down-scoped tokens) at each hop.\n498\t- Authorization uses the original subject and the current actor; earlier actors in the chain are recorded only.\n499\t- Children cannot be given powers the parent lacks unless there is an explicit, separate approval.\n500\t- Audience-restricted tokens, so a parent's token is rejected by a child's service.\n501\t\n502\t**Red flags:**\n503\t- Parent passes its own full credentials or API keys to sub-agents.\n504\t- A low-privilege agent can call a more powerful peer that acts with the peer's own broad credentials.\n505\t- Authorization decided from the `act` chain or a peer-supplied delegator claim instead of the original subject.\n506\t- Tokens with no audience restriction reused across services.\n507\t\n508\t**Where to look:**\n509\t- sub-agent spawn and handoff code\n510\t- token exchange and credential issuing code\n511\t- peer agent authorization policy\n512\t- IAM role trust policies for agent roles\n513\t\n514\t**Needs runtime evidence:** Needs the E08 test: a read-only parent asking a more powerful peer for a forbidden write must be denied, a properly approved handoff must work, and the parent's token must be rejected at the child's service.\n515\t\n516\t**N/A when:** The agent never delegates to sub-agents or peer agents.\n517\t\n518\t## E09 · G1 · C8 — Recursive shutdown\n519\t\n520\t**Lead:** Operations. **Shares:** Agent team, Infrastructure.\n521\t\n522\t### Requirement\n523\t\n524\tRun a drill across a parent, nested children, remote workers, queued work, and open tool streams, subscriptions, and tasks, where they exist. Check that the stop reaches every descendant within the set time. It must revoke access as needed and prevent orphaned retries or restarts. Record how outside effects that already happened are reconciled.\n525\t\n526\t  Why this is hard: Remote workers, disconnected children, queued work, and open tool streams, subscriptions, and tasks can outlive the parent. A stop message can arrive late or get lost. Remote effects that already happened may not be cancelable.\n527\t\n528\t  If you can't fully do it: Combine stop signals that spread down the tree with revocation, time limits on tasks, and short-lived leases that must be renewed to keep working. Block new work from being sent. Test that disconnected workers lose the ability to act within the stop time limit. Decide separately how long-running calls get canceled or reconciled. Leave out remote execution paths that can't meet the time limit. Record completed effects separately and reconcile them under R12.\n529\t\n530\t  Tradeoffs you can accept: Fewer remote workers, the overhead of renewing leases, and slower completion. A set stop time is fine when it is tested and fits the potential harm. A worker that keeps its authority past that time is a blocking gap.\n531\t\n532\t### Verification recipe\n533\t\n534\tBuild a test tree with nested agents, a remote worker, queued work, and open tool streams, subscriptions, and tasks. Stop the root. Try delayed retries and new calls from descendants. Confirm every descendant is stopped or revoked within the time limit, and finished effects are reconciled.\n535\t\n536\t**Evidence to keep:** Full tree, stop and revocation times, retry denials, reconciliation log.\n537\t\n538\t### Checks for likely_pass\n539\t\n540\tRate likely_pass only if every check below is true, with a file and line for each:\n541\t\n542\t- **E09.1** A stop signal from the root reaches every descendant: nested children, remote workers, queued work, and open tool streams, subscriptions, and tasks.\n543\t  - *How to verify:* 1) Find the stop handler. 2) Check it reaches nested children, remote workers, queued jobs, and open tool streams, subscriptions, and tasks (cancel calls, MCP `notifications/cancelled`). 3) Answer no if any of these that exists in the code is not reached, or stop only sets an in-process flag that remote workers never read.\n544\t- **E09.2** Stopping revokes descendants' credentials or leases, and leases or tokens are short-lived, so a disconnected worker loses authority within the stop time limit.\n545\t  - *How to verify:* 1) Open the stop path and credential issuing code. 2) Check stopping revokes descendants' tokens or leases. 3) Check token or lease lifetime fits within the stop time limit. 4) Answer no if no revocation exists, or tokens are long-lived so a disconnected worker keeps authority.\n546\t- **E09.3** Queues and schedulers refuse new work, retries, and restarts for a stopped tree.\n547\t  - *How to verify:* 1) Find queue consumers, schedulers, and retry logic. 2) Check each looks up the stopped state before running new work, retries, or restarts. 3) Answer no if any consumer, retry decorator, or restart policy runs without that check.\n548\t- **E09.4** Outside effects already completed are recorded for reconciliation.\n549\t  - *How to verify:* 1) Open the stop path. 2) Check it records outside effects already completed (tool calls, writes, sent messages) for reconciliation. 3) Answer no if nothing is recorded, or only in-flight work is listed.\n550\t\n551\t### Not covered here\n552\t\n553\tFile these problems under their owning item, not this one, unless this item's own requirement is also broken:\n554\t\n555\t- Agent able to disable or delay its own stop → **R11**\n556\t- No way to cut off the agent's access without cutting off the user → **B04**\n557\t- No drill for a compromised shared tool or agent type → **E12**\n558\t- Sub-agent given powers beyond its task → **E08**\n559\t\n560\t### Heuristics\n561\t\n562\t**Provable from code:** partly\n563\t\n564\t**Look for:**\n565\t- A stop signal that spreads from root to all descendants (cancellation tokens, propagated stop messages).\n566\t- Short-lived leases or credentials that children must renew, so disconnected workers lose access.\n567\t- Queued work for a stopped tree is dropped or blocked; retries check a stop flag before running.\n568\t- Open tool streams, subscriptions, and remote tasks are canceled on stop; a reconciliation step for effects already done.\n569\t\n570\t**Red flags:**\n571\t- Stop only kills the parent process; children, queue workers, or remote tasks keep running.\n572\t- Long-lived credentials given to remote workers.\n573\t- Retry loops and schedulers with no check for a stopped run.\n574\t- No time limit on tasks or subscriptions.\n575\t\n576\t**Where to look:**\n577\t- orchestration and sub-agent lifecycle code\n578\t- queue consumers and retry config\n579\t- credential TTLs and lease logic\n580\t- stop or kill endpoints and runbooks\n581\t\n582\t**Needs runtime evidence:** Needs the E09 drill across nested agents, a remote worker, queued work, and open streams, with stop and revocation times and denied retries.\n583\t\n584\t**N/A when:** never\n585\t\n586\t## E10 · G1 · C9/Obs-T1 — Audit across service boundaries\n587\t\n588\t**Lead:** Infrastructure. **Shares:** Operations, Security, Vendors.\n589\t\n590\t### Requirement\n591\t\n592\tRebuild a run across the gateway, tool services, and delegated workers, with all six identity fields kept. Check that the agent can't erase or rewrite its audit history, either directly or by asking a more powerful tool or peer. Check that a vendor boundary doesn't leave any action untraceable to the agent and instance that did it.\n593\t\n594\t  Why this is hard: Vendor services may drop parent or tenant identity, or return only request receipts. That leaves gaps between your gateway trace and what happened downstream. Shared credentials make attribution even weaker.\n595\t\n596\t  If you can't fully do it: Keep verified identity and correlation IDs at your gateway. Map them to vendor job or receipt IDs, and match them against any destination records you can get. Where identity can't be passed downstream, use separate, narrow credentials. Record which parts of the history you can't inspect. Gateway logs alone can't prove what happened inside those services. Limit or replace integrations whose evidence can't meet the attribution requirement. Don't mark the whole graph complete.\n597\t\n598\t  Tradeoffs you can accept: Extra integration work, slower reconciliation, and leaving out services you can't see into. Mapping verified identity to vendor receipts can support attribution once you've shown it works end to end. Actions that can never be traced still block release.\n599\t\n600\t### Verification recipe\n601\t\n602\tRun a task across the gateway, tools, and workers. Merge their logs into one graph with the six identity fields. Try to delete or edit records with the agent's credentials, and by asking a more powerful tool or peer to do it. Both must be denied, or held for approval under B05. Record any vendor segment you can't access as a gap.\n603\t\n604\t**Evidence to keep:** Cross-service graph, completeness query, audit-write denial.\n605\t\n606\t### Checks for likely_pass\n607\t\n608\tRate likely_pass only if every check below is true, with a file and line for each:\n609\t\n610\t- **E10.1** The gateway, tool services, and delegated workers each write audit records with all six identity fields and the shared trace context.\n611\t  - *How to verify:* 1) Find audit writers in the gateway, tool services, and delegated workers. 2) Check each writes all six identity fields and the shared trace context. 3) Answer no if any component writes records without them or starts a new trace. Answer unknown if a component's code is outside this repo.\n612\t- **E10.2** The agent's credentials have no permission to delete, update, or overwrite audit records (for example an append-only or WORM store with IAM denies).\n613\t  - *How to verify:* 1) Find the agent's IAM role or database grants and the audit store config. 2) Check the agent has no delete, update, or overwrite rights (`DeleteObject`, overwriting `PutObject`, `UPDATE`, `DELETE`, `TRUNCATE`) and the store is append-only or WORM (Object Lock). 3) Answer no if the agent's credentials can delete or rewrite records, or grant `*`. Answer unknown if these are set outside this repo.\n614\t- **E10.3** No tool or peer the agent can call is able to delete or rewrite audit records on its behalf, or such requests are held for approval under B05.\n615\t  - *How to verify:* 1) List the tools and peers the agent can call. 2) For each, check whether it can delete or rewrite audit records (database admin, log management, storage tools). 3) Answer no if any can and such requests are not held for approval under B05.\n616\t- **E10.4** Calls into vendor services are mapped from the gateway's verified identity and correlation ID to the vendor's job or receipt ID.\n617\t  - *How to verify:* 1) Find calls into vendor services. 2) Check the gateway stores its verified identity and correlation ID together with the vendor's job or receipt ID. 3) Answer no if the vendor ID is not stored, or stored without the link to identity and correlation ID.\n618\t\n619\t### Not covered here\n620\t\n621\tFile these problems under their owning item, not this one, unless this item's own requirement is also broken:\n622\t\n623\t- Audit retention, access, redaction, or export rules missing → **E11**\n624\t- Authorization decisions missing the policy version in the run record → **R13**\n625\t- Instance or tenant labels taken from what the model supplies → **A04**\n626\t- Recovery checkpoints not tamper-evident → **R14**\n627\t\n628\t### Heuristics\n629\t\n630\t**Provable from code:** partly\n631\t\n632\t**Look for:**\n633\t- Gateway, tool services, and workers all emit the six identity fields and propagate W3C trace context.\n634\t- Gateway maps verified identity to vendor job or receipt IDs where identity can't pass downstream.\n635\t- Audit logs go to a sink the agent can only append to (separate write-only role, WORM storage, S3 Object Lock in compliance mode).\n636\t- No tool or peer the agent can call has delete or edit rights on audit storage.\n637\t\n638\t**Red flags:**\n639\t- Audit logs written by the agent's own credentials to a bucket it can delete from.\n640\t- A tool or admin API the agent can call that can delete or rewrite logs.\n641\t- Vendor calls made with shared credentials and no mapping from receipts back to agent and instance.\n642\t- Trace context dropped at the gateway or at queue boundaries.\n643\t\n644\t**Where to look:**\n645\t- audit log sink config and its IAM policy\n646\t- bucket or storage settings (object lock, retention mode)\n647\t- gateway logging and trace propagation\n648\t- tool definitions with admin or log-management powers\n649\t\n650\t**Needs runtime evidence:** Needs the E10 test: merge logs across services into one graph with all six fields, and show deletes or edits by the agent, directly or through a more powerful tool or peer, are denied or held for approval.\n651\t\n652\t**N/A when:** never\n653\t\n654\t## E11 · G2 · C9 — Protect shared evidence\n655\t\n656\t**Lead:** Infrastructure. **Shares:** Governance, Data and privacy, Security, Vendors.\n657\t\n658\t### Requirement\n659\t\n660\tSet rules for how long audit records are kept, who can see them, what is redacted, how their integrity is checked, and how they are exported and restored. Test pulling up a past run, and test that missing records or a broken pipeline get noticed. Keep records long enough for investigations and for the law. For example, the EU AI Act requires providers and deployers of high-risk systems to keep automatically generated logs for at least six months (Articles 19 and 26). High-impact actions must fail closed when required audit capture isn't working.\n661\t\n662\t  Why this is hard: Audit delivery can fail while the agent keeps working. Buffers can fill up, records can arrive late, and a vendor may keep records for less time than you need. Exported logs may also hold sensitive data.\n663\t\n664\t  If you can't fully do it: Use durable local buffering, or another protected audit path, with delivery receipts, backlog limits, and alerts for missing records. Export vendor evidence before the vendor deletes it, and limit who can see it. When buffering runs out or isn't available, pause affected actions before required evidence is lost. Record outages and any evidence you can't get back. A fixed pipeline doesn't bring back missing history.\n665\t\n666\t  Tradeoffs you can accept: Short delivery delays while records stay safely stored, extra storage and export costs, and paused work during outages. Limits on retention or delivery can get G2 acceptance only if the required G1 action capture and attribution still hold. Disclose any lost evidence.\n667\t\n668\t### Verification recipe\n669\t\n670\tPull up an old test trace, check its integrity, and run the export and recovery procedure. Interrupt audit delivery and inject a missing event in the sequence. The gap must be caught, and high-impact actions must fail closed while capture is down. Check the retention period against the investigation window and the law that applies.\n671\t\n672\t**Evidence to keep:** Retention and access policy, integrity and export results, outage alerts and response.\n673\t\n674\t### Checks for likely_pass\n675\t\n676\tRate likely_pass only if every check below is true, with a file and line for each:\n677\t\n678\t- **E11.1** Audit retention is set in config to cover the investigation window and the legal minimum (for example at least six months for EU AI Act high-risk systems).\n679\t  - *How to verify:* 1) Find retention settings for audit storage: bucket lifecycle rules, log retention days, TTLs. 2) Check the value covers the investigation window and the legal minimum (for example six months for EU AI Act high-risk systems). 3) Answer no if retention is shorter or unset. Answer unknown if audit storage is configured outside this repo.\n680\t- **E11.2** Access to audit records is limited by role, and redaction rules apply to sensitive fields.\n681\t  - *How to verify:* 1) Find IAM policies or ACLs on audit storage. 2) Check reads are limited by role. 3) Check redaction rules apply to sensitive fields. 4) Answer no if read access is broad (`*`, all engineers) or no redaction exists.\n682\t- **E11.3** Integrity protection (hash chain, signatures, or WORM) plus sequence numbers or delivery receipts let missing records be detected, with an alert on gaps or pipeline failure.\n683\t  - *How to verify:* 1) Open the audit writer and store config. 2) Check for integrity protection (hash chain, signatures, or WORM) plus sequence numbers or delivery receipts. 3) Check an alert fires on gaps or pipeline failure. 4) Answer no if any piece is missing, or a hash is computed but never verified.\n684\t- **E11.4** High-impact actions are blocked when required audit capture is down.\n685\t  - *How to verify:* 1) Find high-impact action paths. 2) Check they confirm audit capture is working before the action runs. 3) Answer no if audit write errors are caught and the action proceeds, or the audit record is written only after success.\n686\t\n687\t### Not covered here\n688\t\n689\tFile these problems under their owning item, not this one, unless this item's own requirement is also broken:\n690\t\n691\t- Agent able to erase or rewrite its own audit history → **E10**\n692\t- Run record missing denials, approvals, or results → **R13**\n693\t- Secrets left in tool output, responses, or normal logs → **R03**\n694\t\n695\t### Heuristics\n696\t\n697\t**Provable from code:** partly\n698\t\n699\t**Look for:**\n700\t- Retention set to cover the investigation window and legal needs (for example at least six months for EU AI Act high-risk logs).\n701\t- Integrity checks on audit records (hash chains, signed batches, sequence numbers).\n702\t- Durable local buffering with delivery receipts, backlog limits, and alerts on gaps.\n703\t- High-impact actions fail closed when audit capture is down.\n704\t- Documented export, restore, redaction, and access rules.\n705\t\n706\t**Red flags:**\n707\t- Audit writes are fire-and-forget; failures are logged and the action continues.\n708\t- Default backend retention (for example 7 or 30 days) with no longer archive.\n709\t- No alert when log delivery stops or sequence numbers skip.\n710\t- Exported logs hold unredacted sensitive data with broad read access.\n711\t\n712\t**Where to look:**\n713\t- audit logging client and buffering code\n714\t- log backend and bucket lifecycle settings\n715\t- monitoring and alert rules for log pipelines\n716\t- data retention and redaction policy docs\n717\t\n718\t**Needs runtime evidence:** Needs the E11 recipe: restore an old trace, check integrity, interrupt delivery and inject a missing event, and confirm the gap is caught and high-impact actions stop.\n719\t\n720\t**N/A when:** never\n721\t\n722\t## E12 · G3 · C7/C8 — Fleet incident response\n723\t\n724\t**Lead:** Security. **Shares:** Operations, Governance, Business owner.\n725\t\n726\t### Requirement\n727\t\n728\tRun a drill where a shared tool or agent type is compromised. Find the affected instances and tenants. Quarantine the dependency, revoke the access involved, stop affected work, and alert the named responders. Check that alerts about abuse across agents, and about budgets, reach the team that can act. Name who decides whether to report outside the organization and share information.\n729\t\n730\t### Verification recipe\n731\t\n732\tMark a shared test tool or version as compromised. Query which instances and tenants it affects, quarantine it, revoke the access involved, and trigger response alerts. Affected work must stop within the set targets. Responders must get identities and traces they can act on. Record who decided on outside reporting and information sharing.\n733\t\n734\t**Evidence to keep:** Fleet impact query, quarantine and revocation records, alert delivery and response times.\n735\t\n736\t### Checks for likely_pass\n737\t\n738\tChecks for likely_pass:\n739\t\n740\t- (none) Code can't show this item. Don't rate it likely_pass; use needs_evidence, gap, or na_candidate.\n741\t\n742\t### Not covered here\n743\t\n744\tFile these problems under their owning item, not this one, unless this item's own requirement is also broken:\n745\t\n746\t- Kill switch for a single agent not drilled → **R11**\n747\t- No owner named on each side of a shared control → **E01**\n748\t- Tool or MCP server approved without vetting or pinning → **E02**\n749\t- Budget and rate limits not enforced outside the model → **B07**\n750\t\n751\t### Heuristics\n752\t\n753\t**Provable from code:** no\n754\t\n755\t**Look for:**\n756\t- An inventory or query that maps tool and dependency versions to instances and tenants.\n757\t- Quarantine controls for a shared tool or agent type (gateway deny rule, registry block, feature flag).\n758\t- Alert routes for cross-agent abuse and budget alerts to a named responder team.\n759\t- An incident runbook that names who decides on outside reporting and information sharing.\n760\t\n761\t**Red flags:**\n762\t- No way to find which deployments or tenants use a given tool version.\n763\t- Quarantine requires a code change and full redeploy.\n764\t- Alerts go to an unowned channel or a single person.\n765\t- No named decision-maker for outside reporting.\n766\t\n767\t**Where to look:**\n768\t- incident runbooks and on-call config\n769\t- alert routing config (PagerDuty, Opsgenie, Alertmanager)\n770\t- gateway and tool registry block rules\n771\t- SBOM or dependency inventory systems\n772\t\n773\t**Needs runtime evidence:** Almost all of this item is shown by the E12 drill: mark a shared tool compromised, query the impact, quarantine, revoke, alert, and measure stop times. Code only shows that the controls exist.\n774\t\n775\t**N/A when:** never\n776\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"29563294-217c-407e-8b87-42521726a071","uuid":"47e4a31e-a18d-4e3e-b631-300c0263de2b","timestamp":"2026-10-02T16:18:07.748Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/private/var/folders/6v/yk9bzvrn5zx0kxll7d4szzb80000gn/T/brace-repeat-80qi2mwl/skill/references/ecosystem.md","content":"<!-- Generated by skills/gen_references.py from CHECKLIST.md, CHECKLIST-VERIFICATION.md, ROLES.md, and heuristics. Do not edit by hand. -->\n\n# E — Ecosystem\n\nUse this file to rate each item from the code and configuration. The requirement is the checklist text. The heuristics say what to look for. Ratings: likely_pass, gap, needs_evidence, or na_candidate. Never rate pass.\n\n## E01 · G2 · C1–C9 — Map shared responsibilities\n\n**Lead:** Governance. **Shares:** Agent team, Infrastructure, Vendors.\n\n### Requirement\n\nList the outside tools, MCP servers, peer agents, sub-agents, registries, identity services, gateways, stop channels, and audit services. For each control that applies, name the owner on the agent team and the owner on the shared platform or vendor side. Keep evidence for both halves. Using an upstream service doesn't make its controls N/A.\n\n### Verification recipe\n\nMap the real calls and services from the configuration and a test trace. For each control that applies, name the agent-team owner and the platform or vendor owner, and link their evidence. Assign an owner to every unowned boundary. Mark vendor evidence you can't get as a gap.\n\n**Evidence to keep:** Dependency map, responsibility table, evidence links.\n\n### Checks for likely_pass\n\nRate likely_pass only if every check below is true, with a file and line for each:\n\n- **E01.1** An inventory file lists the outside tools, MCP servers, peer agents, sub-agents, registries, identity services, gateways, stop channels, and audit services. *(A document can show this.)*\n  - *How to verify:* 1) Find the inventory file. 2) Check each category the agent uses is listed: outside tools, MCP servers, peer agents, sub-agents, registries, identity services, gateways, stop channels, audit services. 3) Answer no if no inventory exists or a category the agent uses is missing.\n- **E01.2** Each inventory entry names both an agent-team owner and a platform or vendor owner. *(A document can show this.)*\n  - *How to verify:* 1) Open each inventory entry. 2) Check it names an agent-team owner and a platform or vendor owner. 3) Answer no if any entry is missing either owner.\n- **E01.3** Every outside service the code or config calls appears in the inventory. *(A document can show this.)*\n  - *How to verify:* 1) Search code and config for outside calls: HTTP clients and base URLs, SDK clients, `*.mcp.json` servers, queue and webhook endpoints, identity providers, telemetry exporters. 2) Compare each with the inventory. 3) Answer no if any called service is not listed.\n\n### Not covered here\n\nFile these problems under their owning item, not this one, unless this item's own requirement is also broken:\n\n- A tool call that skips the MCP gateway or reaches a tool server directly → **E06**\n- A vendor service dropping agent or tenant identity from its records → **E10**\n- Tool or MCP server approved without vetting or pinning → **E02**\n\n### Heuristics\n\n**Provable from code:** partly\n\n**Look for:**\n- A dependency map listing outside tools, MCP servers, peer agents, sub-agents, registries, identity services, gateways, stop channels, and audit services.\n- A responsibility table naming the agent-team owner and the platform or vendor owner for each applicable control, with evidence links for both.\n- Owner records in machine-readable form (service catalog, `.brace/deployment.yaml` roles, CODEOWNERS).\n\n**Red flags:**\n- Outside services in config (MCP servers, API base URLs, webhooks) that do not appear in any dependency or ownership list.\n- Controls marked N/A because an upstream vendor provides the service.\n- Vendor evidence assumed rather than linked, with no gap recorded.\n\n**Where to look:**\n- *.mcp.json, agent config, env vars with service URLs\n- service catalog files, architecture docs, `.brace/` records\n- IaC for gateways, identity providers, and log sinks\n\n**Needs runtime evidence:** Code shows declared dependencies; the E01 recipe compares them with a test trace of real calls and links evidence for each owner.\n\n**N/A when:** never\n\n## E02 · G2 · C3/C5 — Vet and pin dependencies\n\n**Lead:** Security. **Shares:** Agent team, Model team, Vendors.\n\n### Requirement\n\nBefore approving a tool or server, review its source code or available provenance, publisher, permissions, update path, and known vulnerabilities. Include model files, adapters, and datasets. Pin versions or digests, and verify signatures where they exist. A registry's namespace check shows who controls a publisher account, not that the code is safe. Record the limits of remote services and unsigned artifacts, and the extra checks you use instead.\n\n### Verification recipe\n\nFor each approved dependency, including model files, adapters, and datasets, find the publisher, artifact and version, permissions, and update source. Verify signatures where they exist. Scan the pinned list for known vulnerabilities, and judge the findings under the team's release policy. Record missing provenance and how you reduce the risk.\n\n**Evidence to keep:** Dependency list, signature and scan results, review and exception records.\n\n### Checks for likely_pass\n\nRate likely_pass only if every check below is true, with a file and line for each:\n\n- **E02.1** Each tool, MCP server, package, model file, adapter, and dataset is pinned by exact version or digest, with no floating tags, ranges, or 'latest'.\n  - *How to verify:* 1) Open lockfiles, `*.mcp.json`, Dockerfiles, and model or dataset download code. 2) Check each tool, MCP server, package, model file, adapter, and dataset uses an exact version or digest. 3) Answer no if any uses a range, `latest`, a branch, `npx` or `uvx` without a version, or an unpinned download URL.\n- **E02.2** Signature checks run in the build or load path where the publisher provides signatures, and a failed check stops the install or load.\n  - *How to verify:* 1) Find install and load steps in CI and code. 2) Check signature checks (`cosign verify`, `npm audit signatures`, sigstore, GPG) run where the publisher signs. 3) Answer no if a check is missing, its failure is ignored (`|| true`, `continue-on-error`, `try/except` that continues), or a second install path skips it.\n- **E02.3** A vulnerability scan of the pinned dependency list runs in CI and gates on the team's release policy.\n  - *How to verify:* 1) Open CI workflows. 2) Find a vulnerability scan (`pip-audit`, `npm audit`, `osv-scanner`, `trivy`, `grype`) of the pinned dependency list. 3) Check it fails the build per the release policy. 4) Answer no if no scan exists, it is set not to fail (`--exit-zero`, `|| true`, `continue-on-error`), or it scans a different file than the lockfile actually used.\n- **E02.4** Each approved dependency's record names its publisher, permissions, and update source, and remote services or unsigned artifacts have a recorded exception. *(A document can show this.)*\n  - *How to verify:* 1) Find the dependency approval records. 2) Check each names publisher, permissions, and update source. 3) Check each remote service and unsigned artifact has a recorded exception. 4) Answer no if any approved dependency lacks these fields or the needed exception.\n\n### Not covered here\n\nFile these problems under their owning item, not this one, unless this item's own requirement is also broken:\n\n- A tool's description or schema changing after it was approved → **E03**\n- Hidden instructions inside a tool's metadata → **E04**\n- Lookalike or duplicate tool names across servers → **E05**\n- Container image not pinned by digest or signature-checked → **B10**\n\n### Heuristics\n\n**Provable from code:** partly\n\n**Look for:**\n- MCP servers, packages, and images pinned to exact versions or digests (`pkg@1.4.2`, `image@sha256:...`).\n- Dependency scanning in CI (for example OSV-Scanner, Trivy, Grype, Dependabot) with a policy for findings.\n- Signature or provenance checks (Sigstore/cosign, SLSA attestations, npm provenance) where available.\n- Model files, adapters, and datasets included in the pinned list with revisions or digests.\n- Review records for each approved tool or server: publisher, permissions, update source.\n\n**Red flags:**\n- MCP server launched with `npx -y package` or `uvx package` without a pinned version.\n- Remote MCP server URLs with no record of publisher review or limits.\n- Docker images pulled by `:latest` for tools or servers.\n- Hub models or datasets loaded by name with no revision pin.\n- Registry namespace verification treated as proof the code is safe.\n\n**Where to look:**\n- *.mcp.json, `claude_desktop_config.json`, agent tool registries\n- lockfiles, Dockerfiles, compose and k8s manifests\n- CI scanning and signing steps\n- model and dataset download code\n\n**Needs runtime evidence:** Signature verification results and scan outputs come from the E02 run; code shows only whether pins and checks are configured.\n\n**N/A when:** never\n\n## E03 · G2 · C5 — Re-check tools on load\n\n**Lead:** Infrastructure. **Shares:** Security, Vendors.\n\n### Requirement\n\nTake a fingerprint of each approved tool's description and schema. Compare it every time the tool loads, the cache refreshes, or the server says its tool list changed. Don't use a cached definition when the re-check fails. Test a changed description, schema, or implementation version, and block unexpected changes until they are reviewed. A fingerprint of the description and schema catches changes to that metadata, not hidden changes to remote code. Pin or attest the implementation where you can see it. For remote implementations, record what you can't inspect and when you'll review them again. Track vulnerability notices, and find which deployments use an affected dependency.\n\n### Verification recipe\n\nLoad an approved tool. Then, on a test server, change its description, schema, or implementation version, and refresh. Unexpected changes must block use until reviewed. That includes when a cached definition would otherwise be used after a failed re-check. Confirm that a metadata hash doesn't claim to catch changed remote code when the metadata stays the same. Record implementation pinning and visibility separately. Query which deployments use the affected dependency.\n\n**Evidence to keep:** Approved and new fingerprints, load denials, affected-deployment query.\n\n### Checks for likely_pass\n\nRate likely_pass only if every check below is true, with a file and line for each:\n\n- **E03.1** A fingerprint of each approved tool's description and schema is stored.\n  - *How to verify:* 1) Find where tool definitions are loaded (MCP `tools/list`, tool registry). 2) Look for a stored hash of each approved tool's description and schema. 3) Answer no if no fingerprint is stored, or it covers only the name and not the description and schema.\n- **E03.2** The fingerprint is re-checked on every tool load, cache refresh, and list-changed notice from the server.\n  - *How to verify:* 1) List every path that loads tool definitions: startup, cache refresh, reconnect, and the `notifications/tools/list_changed` handler. 2) Check each recomputes the fingerprint and compares it with the stored one. 3) Answer no if any path skips the compare, or both sides of the compare are computed from the new definition.\n- **E03.3** When the re-check fails, the tool is blocked and the cached definition is not used.\n  - *How to verify:* 1) Open the fingerprint mismatch branch. 2) Check it blocks the tool and does not fall back to the cached definition. 3) Answer no if it only logs, uses the cached or new definition anyway, or an error during the check lets the tool load.\n- **E03.4** The implementation version is pinned or attested where it can be seen, and recorded separately from the metadata fingerprint.\n  - *How to verify:* 1) Check how each tool server implementation is referenced: package version, image digest, or attestation. 2) Check that version is recorded in its own field, apart from the metadata fingerprint. 3) Answer no if a visible implementation is unpinned, or only the metadata hash is recorded.\n\n### Not covered here\n\nFile these problems under their owning item, not this one, unless this item's own requirement is also broken:\n\n- Hidden instructions in a tool's metadata when first approved → **E04**\n- Tool approved without reviewing its publisher and permissions → **E02**\n- A new server quietly swapping its tool in for an approved one → **E05**\n- Cached tool list shared across authorization contexts → **E06**\n\n### Heuristics\n\n**Provable from code:** yes\n\n**Look for:**\n- Stored fingerprints (hashes) of each approved tool's description and schema, compared on every load, cache refresh, and `notifications/tools/list_changed`.\n- Load is blocked when the fingerprint doesn't match or the re-check fails; no fallback to a cached definition.\n- Implementation version pinned or attested separately from the metadata fingerprint where visible.\n- A query or inventory showing which deployments use each tool or server version.\n\n**Red flags:**\n- Tool lists fetched from MCP servers and passed to the model with no fingerprint comparison.\n- `list_changed` notifications handled by silently reloading tools.\n- On re-check error, the code falls back to the cached tool definition.\n- Docs or code claim a metadata hash detects changes to remote server code.\n\n**Where to look:**\n- MCP client code and tool registry or loader\n- gateway tool-catalog config\n- approved tool lists or lock files for tools\n\n**Needs runtime evidence:** Code usually shows whether pinning and re-checks exist; the E03 recipe confirms that a changed description, schema, or version on a test server blocks use, including after a failed re-check.\n\n**N/A when:** The agent loads no external tools or MCP servers; all tools are built in and fixed in the release.\n\n## E04 · G2 · C5 — Inspect tool metadata\n\n**Lead:** Security. **Shares:** Infrastructure.\n\n### Requirement\n\nBefore the model sees them, scan server instructions, tool names, titles, descriptions, input and output schemas, annotations, and prompt and resource templates. Look for hidden instructions, references to other servers' tools, invisible or control characters, role overrides, encoded payloads, and places data could be sent. Limit lengths, and clean or reject anything suspicious. Check that a scanner error doesn't quietly let unchecked metadata through.\n\n  Why this is hard: Hidden instructions can be written in plain language, with no strange encoding or control characters. Scanners can also flag honest descriptions. A clean scan doesn't prove a tool is trustworthy.\n\n  If you can't fully do it: Use a small approved tool catalog, with reviewed, versioned descriptions and schemas. Block unexpected changes. Keep rule-based limits and known-pattern checks even without advanced scanning. Limit each tool's powers regardless of what its metadata says. Turn a tool off when the required inspection isn't available. Record what the scanner covers and misses. Manual review and pattern scans are useful layers. They don't prove every poisoned tool is caught.\n\n  Tradeoffs you can accept: A smaller catalog, review delays, and some honest metadata getting rejected. Limited scanning for attacks hidden in meaning can get G2 acceptance, as long as tool powers are limited separately. Known-pattern tests don't prove you can catch every poisoned tool.\n\n### Verification recipe\n\nAdd test fixtures with hidden or encoded instructions, and overlong descriptions, to tool metadata. Put them in the server instructions field, annotations, and prompt and resource templates too. Add a description that tells the model how to use another server's tool. Confirm the set rejection or cleaning happens before the model sees them. Turn off the scanner. Unchecked metadata must not get through.\n\n**Evidence to keep:** Metadata fixtures, scan results, check of what the model saw.\n\n### Checks for likely_pass\n\nRate likely_pass only if every check below is true, with a file and line for each:\n\n- **E04.1** A scanner runs on server instructions, tool names, titles, descriptions, input and output schemas, annotations, and prompt and resource templates before the model sees them.\n  - *How to verify:* 1) Find where server instructions and tool metadata reach the model's context. 2) Check a scanner runs before that on every field: server instructions, names, titles, descriptions, input and output schemas, annotations, prompt and resource templates. 3) Answer no if any field, or a second load path, skips the scanner.\n- **E04.2** The scanner checks for hidden instructions, references to other servers' tools, invisible or control characters, role overrides, encoded payloads, and data-sending targets, and enforces length limits.\n  - *How to verify:* 1) Open the scanner rules. 2) Check for each: hidden instructions, references to other servers' tools, invisible or control characters (zero-width, bidi), role overrides, encoded payloads (base64), data-sending targets (URLs, emails), and length limits. 3) Answer no if any one is missing.\n- **E04.3** Metadata the scanner flags is cleaned or rejected, not just logged.\n  - *How to verify:* 1) Open the scanner's result handling. 2) Check flagged metadata is cleaned, or the tool rejected, before the model sees it. 3) Answer no if flagged items are only logged or warned about and passed through.\n- **E04.4** A scanner error or outage blocks the metadata instead of letting it through unchecked.\n  - *How to verify:* 1) Read every `try/except`, `catch`, and timeout around the scanner call. 2) Check an error blocks the metadata. 3) Answer no if any error branch passes metadata through, returns clean, or skips the scan, or a config flag disables scanning by default.\n\n### Not covered here\n\nFile these problems under their owning item, not this one, unless this item's own requirement is also broken:\n\n- A tool's description or schema changing after it was approved → **E03**\n- Hidden instructions in tool call results → **R01**\n- Lookalike or duplicate tool names across servers → **E05**\n- Allow decisions based on server hints such as read-only → **B01**\n\n### Heuristics\n\n**Provable from code:** yes\n\n**Look for:**\n- A scanner or filter that runs on server instructions, tool names, titles, descriptions, schemas, annotations, and prompt and resource templates before they reach the model.\n- Checks for hidden or control characters, encoded payloads, role overrides, references to other servers' tools, and length limits.\n- Scanner errors cause rejection (fail closed), not pass-through.\n- Where scanning is limited, a small approved catalog with reviewed, versioned metadata.\n\n**Red flags:**\n- Tool descriptions and server `instructions` inserted into the system prompt unchanged.\n- Only descriptions are scanned; schemas, annotations, or templates skip the scanner.\n- Scanner wrapped in `try/except` that logs and continues on error.\n- No length limits on tool metadata.\n\n**Where to look:**\n- MCP client or gateway code that builds the tool list for the model\n- metadata scanning or sanitizing modules\n- gateway policy config\n\n**Needs runtime evidence:** The E04 recipe with poisoned metadata fixtures confirms what the model actually saw, including with the scanner turned off.\n\n**N/A when:** The agent loads no external tools or MCP servers; all tool metadata is written in-house and fixed in the release.\n\n## E05 · G2 · C2/C5 — Verified, namespaced tools\n\n**Lead:** Infrastructure. **Shares:** Security.\n\n### Requirement\n\nIdentify each tool by a verified server identity plus the tool name. Base server identity on the verified endpoint, credential, or registry namespace. A server's self-reported name is neither unique nor trustworthy. Flag lookalike and duplicate names across servers. Test that a new server can't quietly swap its tool in for an approved one.\n\n### Verification recipe\n\nRegister a second test server with a duplicate or lookalike tool name. The registry must flag the conflict, and calls must go to the approved server identity. A matching bare name must not change the target. Register a server whose self-reported name copies an approved server's name. It must not inherit that server's approval.\n\n**Evidence to keep:** Registration warning, resolved tool identity, destination log.\n\n### Checks for likely_pass\n\nRate likely_pass only if every check below is true, with a file and line for each:\n\n- **E05.1** Tools are keyed by verified server identity (endpoint, credential, or registry namespace) plus tool name, never by bare tool name or a server's self-reported name.\n  - *How to verify:* 1) Find the tool registry or lookup map. 2) Check its key is a verified endpoint, credential, or registry namespace plus tool name. 3) Answer no if tools are keyed by bare tool name or the server's self-reported name (`serverInfo.name`), or a later server overwrites an earlier entry with the same tool name.\n- **E05.2** The registry flags duplicate and lookalike tool names across servers.\n  - *How to verify:* 1) Open tool registration code. 2) Look for a check that compares new tool names across servers for duplicates and lookalikes (case, homoglyphs, separators). 3) Answer no if registration silently overwrites or accepts duplicates.\n- **E05.3** Approval is bound to the verified server identity, so a new server with a matching tool name or self-reported name does not inherit approval or replace the approved tool.\n  - *How to verify:* 1) Find where tool approval is stored and checked. 2) Check approval is keyed by verified server identity, not tool name or self-reported server name. 3) Answer no if a new server with a matching tool name or server name would count as approved or replace the approved tool.\n\n### Not covered here\n\nFile these problems under their owning item, not this one, unless this item's own requirement is also broken:\n\n- Hidden instructions inside a tool's metadata → **E04**\n- A tool's description or schema changing after it was approved → **E03**\n- Peer agent identity not authenticated on each message → **E07**\n- Gateway not authenticating callers or authorizing each action → **E06**\n\n### Heuristics\n\n**Provable from code:** yes\n\n**Look for:**\n- Tools keyed by verified server identity plus tool name (for example a namespaced `server-id/tool`), where server identity comes from the endpoint, credential, or registry namespace.\n- Registration code that flags duplicate and lookalike tool names across servers.\n- Approvals bound to verified server identity, not to the server's self-reported name.\n\n**Red flags:**\n- Tools merged into one flat dict by bare name, so a later server overwrites an earlier one.\n- Server identity taken from the `serverInfo.name` the server reports about itself.\n- No warning when two servers expose the same or similar tool name.\n\n**Where to look:**\n- tool registry and MCP client multiplexing code\n- gateway routing config\n- *.mcp.json server entries\n\n**Needs runtime evidence:** The E05 recipe registers a duplicate or lookalike server and confirms calls still go to the approved server identity.\n\n**N/A when:** The agent loads no external tools or MCP servers.\n\n## E06 · G2 · C1/C2/C4/C5/C9 — Enforced gateway boundary\n\n**Lead:** Infrastructure. **Shares:** Security, Agent team, Vendors.\n\n### Requirement\n\nSend MCP calls through a gateway, or a similar enforcement layer, that the agent can't get around. Authenticate each caller (prove who they are), authorize each action, and validate calls and responses. Enforce rate limits and log every decision. For HTTP and OAuth, check the token's issuer, intended audience, expiry, and scopes. Use properly authorized credentials for the services behind the gateway. Never pass an unchecked token through. For local tools that talk over standard input/output (stdio), control process launch, file access, credentials, and networking in the host or sandbox. An HTTP gateway alone doesn't cover these. Test going around the gateway straight to the server, bad credentials, broken responses, and outages of the policy engine or scanner. MCP copies some request details into headers. If routing or policy uses those headers, reject any request whose headers don't match its body, before checking policy. Store any cached tool list or response separately for each authorization context. When the gateway is itself an OAuth client, check the issuer (`iss`, RFC 9207). Also protect discovery URLs against SSRF (tricking a server into calling places it shouldn't). Required checks must fail closed: if a required check can't run, block the action.\n\n### Verification recipe\n\nMake allowed calls through the gateway. Then try going around it straight to the server, tokens for the wrong audience or that have expired, broken responses, and a scanner or policy outage. Send a `tools/call` whose `Mcp-Name` header names a different tool than the body. Request a cached tool list using a second caller's token. Test that downstream credentials are separate, that the issuer (`iss`) is checked where the gateway is an OAuth client, and that local stdio launches are restricted, where these apply. Unapproved and unchecked traffic must fail closed. Allowed traffic must still be logged, and rate limits must work.\n\n**Evidence to keep:** Route and policy settings, request and response fixtures, gateway decisions.\n\n### Checks for likely_pass\n\nRate likely_pass only if every check below is true, with a file and line for each:\n\n- **E06.1** The agent can reach MCP servers only through the gateway: network policy blocks direct routes, the agent holds no server credentials, and any local stdio servers are launched by the host or sandbox with limited files, credentials, and network.\n  - *How to verify:* 1) Find how the agent reaches MCP servers: URLs in `*.mcp.json` and client config. 2) Check all go to the gateway, and NetworkPolicy or egress rules block direct routes. 3) Check the agent holds no server credentials and stdio servers run in a restricted sandbox. 4) Answer no if any tool call bypasses the gateway. Answer unknown if network policy lives outside this repo.\n- **E06.2** The gateway checks each token's issuer, audience, expiry, and scopes, and uses its own separately authorized credentials downstream instead of passing the caller's token through.\n  - *How to verify:* 1) Open the gateway's token validation. 2) Check issuer, audience, expiry, and scopes are each verified. 3) Check downstream calls use the gateway's own credentials. 4) Answer no if any claim is unchecked, signature verification is disabled, or the caller's `Authorization` header is forwarded downstream.\n- **E06.3** The gateway authorizes each action, validates requests and responses, enforces rate limits, and logs every decision.\n  - *How to verify:* 1) Open the gateway's request handling. 2) Check per-action authorization, request and response validation, rate limits, and a log entry for every decision. 3) Answer no if any is missing, authorization happens only once per connection, or denials are not logged.\n- **E06.4** If a required check (policy engine, scanner, token check) errors or times out, the call is blocked.\n  - *How to verify:* 1) Read every `try/except`, `catch`, and timeout around the policy engine, scanner, and token check. 2) Check each error blocks the call. 3) Answer no if any error branch allows the call or logs and continues, or a config flag defaults to fail-open.\n- **E06.5** Requests whose routing headers (such as Mcp-Name) don't match the body are rejected before policy, and cached tool lists and responses are stored per authorization context.\n  - *How to verify:* 1) Find where routing or policy reads headers such as `Mcp-Name`. 2) Check the header is compared with the body before policy runs, and mismatches are rejected. 3) Check tool-list and response caches are keyed by authorization context. 4) Answer no if headers are trusted without the compare, or a cache is shared across callers.\n- **E06.6** If the gateway is itself an OAuth client, it checks the authorization server's issuer (iss, RFC 9207) and blocks private and metadata addresses in discovery URLs. Answer yes with the code line, or yes citing the config that shows it isn't an OAuth client.\n  - *How to verify:* 1) Check whether the gateway acts as an OAuth client (gets tokens, follows discovery or metadata URLs). 2) If so, check it verifies the `iss` parameter (RFC 9207) and blocks private, loopback, and metadata addresses (169.254.169.254) in discovery URLs. 3) Answer no if either is missing. If it is not an OAuth client, answer yes citing the config that shows that.\n\n### Not covered here\n\nFile these problems under their owning item, not this one, unless this item's own requirement is also broken:\n\n- Tool inventory missing a task-based reason for each tool → **B01**\n- Agent credential granting wildcard or admin permissions → **B02**\n- Destructive actions running without a higher-authority approval → **B05**\n- Hidden instructions inside a tool's metadata → **E04**\n- Outbound traffic to unapproved internet destinations → **B09**\n\n### Heuristics\n\n**Provable from code:** partly\n\n**Look for:**\n- All MCP traffic routed through a gateway or enforcement layer; network policy blocks direct paths from the agent to servers.\n- Gateway checks token issuer, audience, expiry, and scopes; uses separate downstream credentials (token exchange) rather than passing the caller's token through.\n- Per-call authorization, request and response validation, rate limits, and decision logging.\n- Rejection of requests whose MCP headers (for example `Mcp-Name`) don't match the body, before policy runs; tool-list caches keyed by authorization context.\n- `iss` checked (RFC 9207) where the gateway is an OAuth client; SSRF protection on discovery URLs; stdio servers launched in a sandbox with limited files, credentials, and network.\n- Fail-closed settings when the policy engine or scanner is down.\n\n**Red flags:**\n- Agent config lists MCP server URLs directly, bypassing the gateway, with egress open.\n- Gateway forwards the incoming bearer token unchanged to downstream servers.\n- Policy or scanner outage handled with fail-open (`on_error: allow`, catch and continue).\n- Cached tool lists shared across callers or tenants.\n- stdio servers launched on the host with full environment variables and network access.\n\n**Where to look:**\n- gateway config (route, auth, policy, rate-limit rules)\n- *.mcp.json and agent MCP client settings\n- k8s NetworkPolicy, egress proxy, firewall rules\n- OAuth client and token-exchange config; sandbox or container settings for stdio servers\n\n**Needs runtime evidence:** Needs the E06 tests: bypass attempts, wrong-audience and expired tokens, broken responses, header-body mismatch, cross-caller cache reads, and policy or scanner outages must all fail closed.\n\n**N/A when:** The agent uses no MCP servers or similar external tool servers.\n\n## E07 · G2 · C2/C5 — Untrusted peer messages\n\n**Lead:** Agent team. **Shares:** Security.\n\n### Requirement\n\nAuthenticate the sending agent (prove its identity) on every message, even validly signed ones. Validate message types and schemas, and check what that peer is allowed to ask for. Where a replay could repeat an action, check freshness and track nonces or request IDs. Checking the sender alone doesn't stop replays. Test faked identity, replayed requests, requests across tenants, and instructions passed along by a compromised peer. Verify signed agent cards where they exist. A card says who an agent is, not what it may do. Check callback and webhook URLs against SSRF. A valid sender doesn't make a message's content trusted.\n\n### Verification recipe\n\nSend valid, broken, fake-sender, replayed, and cross-tenant peer requests. Reused action requests must not repeat side effects without approval. Authenticate the sender of every request, even validly signed ones, and check its scope. Deny requests that aren't allowed, and keep hostile content as untrusted data. Include a schema-valid injected instruction, a tampered signed agent card, and a callback URL that points at a private or metadata address.\n\n**Evidence to keep:** Peer policy, message fixtures, authorization and validation logs.\n\n### Checks for likely_pass\n\nRate likely_pass only if every check below is true, with a file and line for each:\n\n- **E07.1** Every peer message is authenticated to a specific sending agent, even when the message is validly signed.\n  - *How to verify:* 1) Find the handler for incoming peer messages (A2A server, peer endpoint). 2) Check each message is authenticated to a specific sending agent (mTLS identity, verified token subject). 3) Answer no if any route accepts unauthenticated messages, trusts a self-declared sender field, or accepts any valid signature without mapping it to a known agent.\n- **E07.2** Message types and schemas are validated, and each request is checked against what that peer is allowed to ask for, including tenant.\n  - *How to verify:* 1) Open the peer message handler. 2) Check message type and schema validation. 3) Check a per-peer allowlist of request types and tenants, keyed on the authenticated sender. 4) Answer no if schemas are unchecked, any authenticated peer can ask for anything, or tenant is taken from the message body.\n- **E07.3** Action requests carry a nonce or request ID and a freshness check, and replays are rejected or do not repeat side effects.\n  - *How to verify:* 1) Find action request handling. 2) Check for a nonce or request ID plus a freshness (timestamp) check, with seen IDs stored. 3) Answer no if no replay check exists, the nonce is never stored or compared, or a repeated ID repeats side effects.\n- **E07.4** Peer message content is handled as untrusted data and is not placed into system or instruction prompts.\n  - *How to verify:* 1) Trace peer message content to the prompt builder. 2) Check it goes into user or tool-result content as untrusted data. 3) Answer no if any path places it into the system prompt or an instruction template.\n- **E07.5** Callback and webhook URLs are checked against SSRF, with private and metadata addresses blocked.\n  - *How to verify:* 1) Find code that calls callback or webhook URLs from peers. 2) Check URL validation blocks private, loopback, link-local, and metadata addresses (169.254.169.254). 3) Answer no if any callback is fetched without the check, or only the hostname text is checked and not the resolved address.\n\n### Not covered here\n\nFile these problems under their owning item, not this one, unless this item's own requirement is also broken:\n\n- Tool server identified only by its self-reported name → **E05**\n- Missing schema or size checks on API responses → **R01**\n- Peers or children still running after a stop → **E09**\n- Run can't be rebuilt across gateway, tool services, and delegated workers → **E10**\n\n### Heuristics\n\n**Provable from code:** partly\n\n**Look for:**\n- Every peer message authenticated (mTLS, signed requests, verified tokens) and the sender's scope checked.\n- Message type and schema validation for incoming A2A or peer requests.\n- Nonce, request ID, or timestamp tracking to stop replays where an action could repeat.\n- Signed agent cards verified, and card claims not used as authorization.\n- Callback and webhook URLs checked against private, link-local, and metadata addresses (SSRF).\n- Peer message content passed to the model as untrusted data.\n\n**Red flags:**\n- Peer requests accepted based on a valid signature alone, with no per-peer authorization.\n- Agent card skills or capabilities treated as permission grants.\n- Callback URLs fetched without SSRF checks (for example to `169.254.169.254`).\n- Retried peer requests re-run side effects with no idempotency key.\n- Peer message text inserted into the system prompt as instructions.\n\n**Where to look:**\n- A2A server handlers, agent card files (`/.well-known/agent-card.json`)\n- peer auth middleware\n- webhook and callback handlers\n- idempotency and replay protection code\n\n**Needs runtime evidence:** Needs the E07 fixtures: fake-sender, replayed, cross-tenant, tampered-card, and private-callback requests must be denied.\n\n**N/A when:** The agent receives no messages from other agents (no A2A server, peer endpoint, or agent-to-agent channel).\n\n## E08 · G1 · C2/C4 — Bounded delegated authority\n\n**Lead:** Agent team. **Shares:** Infrastructure, Security.\n\n### Requirement\n\nGive sub-agents only the powers approved for their task. Enforce each child's role and any explicit approval from a higher authority. A parent must not gain a power it was denied just by asking a more powerful peer to act. Narrow credentials at each hop, for example with token exchange or transaction tokens. Authorize based on the original subject (the user or system whose authority is used) and the current actor. Earlier actors in a delegation chain are for the record only, not a reason to grant access. Test that escalation attempt.\n\n  Why this is hard: A powerful peer may treat a low-privilege agent's request as a normal task. Knowing who sent the last message doesn't prove what the original requester was allowed to do. Delegation limits can also get lost across several hops.\n\n  If you can't fully do it: Carry the verified original identity, tenant, and allowed scope all the way to where the action runs. If a peer can't enforce those limits, delegate only to peers with no more relevant authority. Or use a narrow task credential, or require separate approval for that exact privileged action. If none of these can be enforced, turn off that delegation path. Trusting a peer's prompt does not pass this blocking check.\n\n  Tradeoffs you can accept: Less flexible delegation, narrower child roles, or separate approval for each action. These keep the authorization boundary intact. Quiet privilege gains can never pass this G1 check. Turn off the path if you can't enforce the boundary.\n\n### Verification recipe\n\nGive parent A a read-only task, and ask it to hand a forbidden write to a more powerful peer. The handed-off write must be denied without its own valid approval. A legitimate handed-off task that is clearly approved must still work. Present the parent's token to the child's service. It must be rejected.\n\n**Evidence to keep:** Parent and child grants, delegation decisions, destination state.\n\n### Checks for likely_pass\n\nRate likely_pass only if every check below is true, with a file and line for each:\n\n- **E08.1** Each child gets a credential narrowed to its approved task (for example by token exchange or transaction tokens), never the parent's full credential.\n  - *How to verify:* 1) Find where a child agent gets its credential. 2) Check it is a narrowed token for the task (token exchange, transaction token, scoped-down role). 3) Answer no if any spawn path passes the parent's token, API key, or full role, or the child inherits the parent's environment credentials.\n- **E08.2** Authorization at the action uses the original subject and the current actor, and earlier actors in the delegation chain never add access.\n  - *How to verify:* 1) Open authorization code for delegated actions. 2) Check it decides on the original subject and the current actor. 3) Answer no if it grants access because of any earlier actor in the chain (nested `act` claims, parent identity) or a union of chain permissions.\n- **E08.3** An action the parent was denied is also denied when routed through a more powerful peer, unless it carries its own explicit approval.\n  - *How to verify:* 1) Find how a parent asks a peer or child to act. 2) Check the request carries the parent's limits and the peer re-checks them, or requires its own explicit approval. 3) Answer no if a peer acts with its own wider permissions on a parent's request without that check.\n- **E08.4** Downstream services reject the parent's token when the child presents it (audience-bound tokens).\n  - *How to verify:* 1) Find how child tokens are issued and how downstream services validate tokens. 2) Check tokens are audience-bound and services verify `aud`. 3) Answer no if audience is unset, a wildcard, or not checked. Answer unknown if downstream services are outside this repo.\n\n### Not covered here\n\nFile these problems under their owning item, not this one, unless this item's own requirement is also broken:\n\n- Approvals that can be replayed or aren't tied to exact arguments → **B06**\n- Prompt a parent gave a sub-agent not recorded → **A06**\n- Children still running after the parent is stopped → **E09**\n- Replayed or faked peer-agent messages → **E07**\n- No limit on how many sub-agents a run can start → **B07**\n\n### Heuristics\n\n**Provable from code:** partly\n\n**Look for:**\n- Each sub-agent gets its own narrowed role or credential (token exchange per RFC 8693, transaction tokens, down-scoped tokens) at each hop.\n- Authorization uses the original subject and the current actor; earlier actors in the chain are recorded only.\n- Children cannot be given powers the parent lacks unless there is an explicit, separate approval.\n- Audience-restricted tokens, so a parent's token is rejected by a child's service.\n\n**Red flags:**\n- Parent passes its own full credentials or API keys to sub-agents.\n- A low-privilege agent can call a more powerful peer that acts with the peer's own broad credentials.\n- Authorization decided from the `act` chain or a peer-supplied delegator claim instead of the original subject.\n- Tokens with no audience restriction reused across services.\n\n**Where to look:**\n- sub-agent spawn and handoff code\n- token exchange and credential issuing code\n- peer agent authorization policy\n- IAM role trust policies for agent roles\n\n**Needs runtime evidence:** Needs the E08 test: a read-only parent asking a more powerful peer for a forbidden write must be denied, a properly approved handoff must work, and the parent's token must be rejected at the child's service.\n\n**N/A when:** The agent never delegates to sub-agents or peer agents.\n\n## E09 · G1 · C8 — Recursive shutdown\n\n**Lead:** Operations. **Shares:** Agent team, Infrastructure.\n\n### Requirement\n\nRun a drill across a parent, nested children, remote workers, queued work, and open tool streams, subscriptions, and tasks, where they exist. Check that the stop reaches every descendant within the set time. It must revoke access as needed and prevent orphaned retries or restarts. Record how outside effects that already happened are reconciled.\n\n  Why this is hard: Remote workers, disconnected children, queued work, and open tool streams, subscriptions, and tasks can outlive the parent. A stop message can arrive late or get lost. Remote effects that already happened may not be cancelable.\n\n  If you can't fully do it: Combine stop signals that spread down the tree with revocation, time limits on tasks, and short-lived leases that must be renewed to keep working. Block new work from being sent. Test that disconnected workers lose the ability to act within the stop time limit. Decide separately how long-running calls get canceled or reconciled. Leave out remote execution paths that can't meet the time limit. Record completed effects separately and reconcile them under R12.\n\n  Tradeoffs you can accept: Fewer remote workers, the overhead of renewing leases, and slower completion. A set stop time is fine when it is tested and fits the potential harm. A worker that keeps its authority past that time is a blocking gap.\n\n### Verification recipe\n\nBuild a test tree with nested agents, a remote worker, queued work, and open tool streams, subscriptions, and tasks. Stop the root. Try delayed retries and new calls from descendants. Confirm every descendant is stopped or revoked within the time limit, and finished effects are reconciled.\n\n**Evidence to keep:** Full tree, stop and revocation times, retry denials, reconciliation log.\n\n### Checks for likely_pass\n\nRate likely_pass only if every check below is true, with a file and line for each:\n\n- **E09.1** A stop signal from the root reaches every descendant: nested children, remote workers, queued work, and open tool streams, subscriptions, and tasks.\n  - *How to verify:* 1) Find the stop handler. 2) Check it reaches nested children, remote workers, queued jobs, and open tool streams, subscriptions, and tasks (cancel calls, MCP `notifications/cancelled`). 3) Answer no if any of these that exists in the code is not reached, or stop only sets an in-process flag that remote workers never read.\n- **E09.2** Stopping revokes descendants' credentials or leases, and leases or tokens are short-lived, so a disconnected worker loses authority within the stop time limit.\n  - *How to verify:* 1) Open the stop path and credential issuing code. 2) Check stopping revokes descendants' tokens or leases. 3) Check token or lease lifetime fits within the stop time limit. 4) Answer no if no revocation exists, or tokens are long-lived so a disconnected worker keeps authority.\n- **E09.3** Queues and schedulers refuse new work, retries, and restarts for a stopped tree.\n  - *How to verify:* 1) Find queue consumers, schedulers, and retry logic. 2) Check each looks up the stopped state before running new work, retries, or restarts. 3) Answer no if any consumer, retry decorator, or restart policy runs without that check.\n- **E09.4** Outside effects already completed are recorded for reconciliation.\n  - *How to verify:* 1) Open the stop path. 2) Check it records outside effects already completed (tool calls, writes, sent messages) for reconciliation. 3) Answer no if nothing is recorded, or only in-flight work is listed.\n\n### Not covered here\n\nFile these problems under their owning item, not this one, unless this item's own requirement is also broken:\n\n- Agent able to disable or delay its own stop → **R11**\n- No way to cut off the agent's access without cutting off the user → **B04**\n- No drill for a compromised shared tool or agent type → **E12**\n- Sub-agent given powers beyond its task → **E08**\n\n### Heuristics\n\n**Provable from code:** partly\n\n**Look for:**\n- A stop signal that spreads from root to all descendants (cancellation tokens, propagated stop messages).\n- Short-lived leases or credentials that children must renew, so disconnected workers lose access.\n- Queued work for a stopped tree is dropped or blocked; retries check a stop flag before running.\n- Open tool streams, subscriptions, and remote tasks are canceled on stop; a reconciliation step for effects already done.\n\n**Red flags:**\n- Stop only kills the parent process; children, queue workers, or remote tasks keep running.\n- Long-lived credentials given to remote workers.\n- Retry loops and schedulers with no check for a stopped run.\n- No time limit on tasks or subscriptions.\n\n**Where to look:**\n- orchestration and sub-agent lifecycle code\n- queue consumers and retry config\n- credential TTLs and lease logic\n- stop or kill endpoints and runbooks\n\n**Needs runtime evidence:** Needs the E09 drill across nested agents, a remote worker, queued work, and open streams, with stop and revocation times and denied retries.\n\n**N/A when:** never\n\n## E10 · G1 · C9/Obs-T1 — Audit across service boundaries\n\n**Lead:** Infrastructure. **Shares:** Operations, Security, Vendors.\n\n### Requirement\n\nRebuild a run across the gateway, tool services, and delegated workers, with all six identity fields kept. Check that the agent can't erase or rewrite its audit history, either directly or by asking a more powerful tool or peer. Check that a vendor boundary doesn't leave any action untraceable to the agent and instance that did it.\n\n  Why this is hard: Vendor services may drop parent or tenant identity, or return only request receipts. That leaves gaps between your gateway trace and what happened downstream. Shared credentials make attribution even weaker.\n\n  If you can't fully do it: Keep verified identity and correlation IDs at your gateway. Map them to vendor job or receipt IDs, and match them against any destination records you can get. Where identity can't be passed downstream, use separate, narrow credentials. Record which parts of the history you can't inspect. Gateway logs alone can't prove what happened inside those services. Limit or replace integrations whose evidence can't meet the attribution requirement. Don't mark the whole graph complete.\n\n  Tradeoffs you can accept: Extra integration work, slower reconciliation, and leaving out services you can't see into. Mapping verified identity to vendor receipts can support attribution once you've shown it works end to end. Actions that can never be traced still block release.\n\n### Verification recipe\n\nRun a task across the gateway, tools, and workers. Merge their logs into one graph with the six identity fields. Try to delete or edit records with the agent's credentials, and by asking a more powerful tool or peer to do it. Both must be denied, or held for approval under B05. Record any vendor segment you can't access as a gap.\n\n**Evidence to keep:** Cross-service graph, completeness query, audit-write denial.\n\n### Checks for likely_pass\n\nRate likely_pass only if every check below is true, with a file and line for each:\n\n- **E10.1** The gateway, tool services, and delegated workers each write audit records with all six identity fields and the shared trace context.\n  - *How to verify:* 1) Find audit writers in the gateway, tool services, and delegated workers. 2) Check each writes all six identity fields and the shared trace context. 3) Answer no if any component writes records without them or starts a new trace. Answer unknown if a component's code is outside this repo.\n- **E10.2** The agent's credentials have no permission to delete, update, or overwrite audit records (for example an append-only or WORM store with IAM denies).\n  - *How to verify:* 1) Find the agent's IAM role or database grants and the audit store config. 2) Check the agent has no delete, update, or overwrite rights (`DeleteObject`, overwriting `PutObject`, `UPDATE`, `DELETE`, `TRUNCATE`) and the store is append-only or WORM (Object Lock). 3) Answer no if the agent's credentials can delete or rewrite records, or grant `*`. Answer unknown if these are set outside this repo.\n- **E10.3** No tool or peer the agent can call is able to delete or rewrite audit records on its behalf, or such requests are held for approval under B05.\n  - *How to verify:* 1) List the tools and peers the agent can call. 2) For each, check whether it can delete or rewrite audit records (database admin, log management, storage tools). 3) Answer no if any can and such requests are not held for approval under B05.\n- **E10.4** Calls into vendor services are mapped from the gateway's verified identity and correlation ID to the vendor's job or receipt ID.\n  - *How to verify:* 1) Find calls into vendor services. 2) Check the gateway stores its verified identity and correlation ID together with the vendor's job or receipt ID. 3) Answer no if the vendor ID is not stored, or stored without the link to identity and correlation ID.\n\n### Not covered here\n\nFile these problems under their owning item, not this one, unless this item's own requirement is also broken:\n\n- Audit retention, access, redaction, or export rules missing → **E11**\n- Authorization decisions missing the policy version in the run record → **R13**\n- Instance or tenant labels taken from what the model supplies → **A04**\n- Recovery checkpoints not tamper-evident → **R14**\n\n### Heuristics\n\n**Provable from code:** partly\n\n**Look for:**\n- Gateway, tool services, and workers all emit the six identity fields and propagate W3C trace context.\n- Gateway maps verified identity to vendor job or receipt IDs where identity can't pass downstream.\n- Audit logs go to a sink the agent can only append to (separate write-only role, WORM storage, S3 Object Lock in compliance mode).\n- No tool or peer the agent can call has delete or edit rights on audit storage.\n\n**Red flags:**\n- Audit logs written by the agent's own credentials to a bucket it can delete from.\n- A tool or admin API the agent can call that can delete or rewrite logs.\n- Vendor calls made with shared credentials and no mapping from receipts back to agent and instance.\n- Trace context dropped at the gateway or at queue boundaries.\n\n**Where to look:**\n- audit log sink config and its IAM policy\n- bucket or storage settings (object lock, retention mode)\n- gateway logging and trace propagation\n- tool definitions with admin or log-management powers\n\n**Needs runtime evidence:** Needs the E10 test: merge logs across services into one graph with all six fields, and show deletes or edits by the agent, directly or through a more powerful tool or peer, are denied or held for approval.\n\n**N/A when:** never\n\n## E11 · G2 · C9 — Protect shared evidence\n\n**Lead:** Infrastructure. **Shares:** Governance, Data and privacy, Security, Vendors.\n\n### Requirement\n\nSet rules for how long audit records are kept, who can see them, what is redacted, how their integrity is checked, and how they are exported and restored. Test pulling up a past run, and test that missing records or a broken pipeline get noticed. Keep records long enough for investigations and for the law. For example, the EU AI Act requires providers and deployers of high-risk systems to keep automatically generated logs for at least six months (Articles 19 and 26). High-impact actions must fail closed when required audit capture isn't working.\n\n  Why this is hard: Audit delivery can fail while the agent keeps working. Buffers can fill up, records can arrive late, and a vendor may keep records for less time than you need. Exported logs may also hold sensitive data.\n\n  If you can't fully do it: Use durable local buffering, or another protected audit path, with delivery receipts, backlog limits, and alerts for missing records. Export vendor evidence before the vendor deletes it, and limit who can see it. When buffering runs out or isn't available, pause affected actions before required evidence is lost. Record outages and any evidence you can't get back. A fixed pipeline doesn't bring back missing history.\n\n  Tradeoffs you can accept: Short delivery delays while records stay safely stored, extra storage and export costs, and paused work during outages. Limits on retention or delivery can get G2 acceptance only if the required G1 action capture and attribution still hold. Disclose any lost evidence.\n\n### Verification recipe\n\nPull up an old test trace, check its integrity, and run the export and recovery procedure. Interrupt audit delivery and inject a missing event in the sequence. The gap must be caught, and high-impact actions must fail closed while capture is down. Check the retention period against the investigation window and the law that applies.\n\n**Evidence to keep:** Retention and access policy, integrity and export results, outage alerts and response.\n\n### Checks for likely_pass\n\nRate likely_pass only if every check below is true, with a file and line for each:\n\n- **E11.1** Audit retention is set in config to cover the investigation window and the legal minimum (for example at least six months for EU AI Act high-risk systems).\n  - *How to verify:* 1) Find retention settings for audit storage: bucket lifecycle rules, log retention days, TTLs. 2) Check the value covers the investigation window and the legal minimum (for example six months for EU AI Act high-risk systems). 3) Answer no if retention is shorter or unset. Answer unknown if audit storage is configured outside this repo.\n- **E11.2** Access to audit records is limited by role, and redaction rules apply to sensitive fields.\n  - *How to verify:* 1) Find IAM policies or ACLs on audit storage. 2) Check reads are limited by role. 3) Check redaction rules apply to sensitive fields. 4) Answer no if read access is broad (`*`, all engineers) or no redaction exists.\n- **E11.3** Integrity protection (hash chain, signatures, or WORM) plus sequence numbers or delivery receipts let missing records be detected, with an alert on gaps or pipeline failure.\n  - *How to verify:* 1) Open the audit writer and store config. 2) Check for integrity protection (hash chain, signatures, or WORM) plus sequence numbers or delivery receipts. 3) Check an alert fires on gaps or pipeline failure. 4) Answer no if any piece is missing, or a hash is computed but never verified.\n- **E11.4** High-impact actions are blocked when required audit capture is down.\n  - *How to verify:* 1) Find high-impact action paths. 2) Check they confirm audit capture is working before the action runs. 3) Answer no if audit write errors are caught and the action proceeds, or the audit record is written only after success.\n\n### Not covered here\n\nFile these problems under their owning item, not this one, unless this item's own requirement is also broken:\n\n- Agent able to erase or rewrite its own audit history → **E10**\n- Run record missing denials, approvals, or results → **R13**\n- Secrets left in tool output, responses, or normal logs → **R03**\n\n### Heuristics\n\n**Provable from code:** partly\n\n**Look for:**\n- Retention set to cover the investigation window and legal needs (for example at least six months for EU AI Act high-risk logs).\n- Integrity checks on audit records (hash chains, signed batches, sequence numbers).\n- Durable local buffering with delivery receipts, backlog limits, and alerts on gaps.\n- High-impact actions fail closed when audit capture is down.\n- Documented export, restore, redaction, and access rules.\n\n**Red flags:**\n- Audit writes are fire-and-forget; failures are logged and the action continues.\n- Default backend retention (for example 7 or 30 days) with no longer archive.\n- No alert when log delivery stops or sequence numbers skip.\n- Exported logs hold unredacted sensitive data with broad read access.\n\n**Where to look:**\n- audit logging client and buffering code\n- log backend and bucket lifecycle settings\n- monitoring and alert rules for log pipelines\n- data retention and redaction policy docs\n\n**Needs runtime evidence:** Needs the E11 recipe: restore an old trace, check integrity, interrupt delivery and inject a missing event, and confirm the gap is caught and high-impact actions stop.\n\n**N/A when:** never\n\n## E12 · G3 · C7/C8 — Fleet incident response\n\n**Lead:** Security. **Shares:** Operations, Governance, Business owner.\n\n### Requirement\n\nRun a drill where a shared tool or agent type is compromised. Find the affected instances and tenants. Quarantine the dependency, revoke the access involved, stop affected work, and alert the named responders. Check that alerts about abuse across agents, and about budgets, reach the team that can act. Name who decides whether to report outside the organization and share information.\n\n### Verification recipe\n\nMark a shared test tool or version as compromised. Query which instances and tenants it affects, quarantine it, revoke the access involved, and trigger response alerts. Affected work must stop within the set targets. Responders must get identities and traces they can act on. Record who decided on outside reporting and information sharing.\n\n**Evidence to keep:** Fleet impact query, quarantine and revocation records, alert delivery and response times.\n\n### Checks for likely_pass\n\nChecks for likely_pass:\n\n- (none) Code can't show this item. Don't rate it likely_pass; use needs_evidence, gap, or na_candidate.\n\n### Not covered here\n\nFile these problems under their owning item, not this one, unless this item's own requirement is also broken:\n\n- Kill switch for a single agent not drilled → **R11**\n- No owner named on each side of a shared control → **E01**\n- Tool or MCP server approved without vetting or pinning → **E02**\n- Budget and rate limits not enforced outside the model → **B07**\n\n### Heuristics\n\n**Provable from code:** no\n\n**Look for:**\n- An inventory or query that maps tool and dependency versions to instances and tenants.\n- Quarantine controls for a shared tool or agent type (gateway deny rule, registry block, feature flag).\n- Alert routes for cross-agent abuse and budget alerts to a named responder team.\n- An incident runbook that names who decides on outside reporting and information sharing.\n\n**Red flags:**\n- No way to find which deployments or tenants use a given tool version.\n- Quarantine requires a code change and full redeploy.\n- Alerts go to an unowned channel or a single person.\n- No named decision-maker for outside reporting.\n\n**Where to look:**\n- incident runbooks and on-call config\n- alert routing config (PagerDuty, Opsgenie, Alertmanager)\n- gateway and tool registry block rules\n- SBOM or dependency inventory systems\n\n**Needs runtime evidence:** Almost all of this item is shown by the E12 drill: mark a shared tool compromised, query the impact, quarantine, revoke, alert, and measure stop times. Code only shows that the controls exist.\n\n**N/A when:** never\n","numLines":776,"startLine":1,"totalLines":776}}}
